{"D287/ch1/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch1-s1"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Framework vs Library (who calls whom?)</h3><p>Both are reusable code someone else wrote. The difference is <b>control</b>:</p><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE ONE-LINE DISTINCTION</div>&bull; A <b>library</b> is code <b>YOU call</b> when you want it (you own the program flow — e.g., calling a math or JSON function).<br>&bull; A <b>framework</b> <b>CALLS YOUR code</b> — it owns the flow and invokes the classes/methods you plug into it. This is called <b>Inversion of Control</b> (&ldquo;don&rsquo;t call us, we&rsquo;ll call you&rdquo; — the Hollywood Principle).</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — the same task both ways</div><b>Library style:</b> your main() runs, and you call <code>Json.parse(text)</code> when you need it. You wrote the loop, you decide the order.<br><br><b>Framework style (Spring):</b> you write<br><pre>@GetMapping("/hello")
public String hello() { return "hello"; }</pre>You never call hello() yourself. <b>Spring</b> receives the web request, decides your method matches, and calls it for you. You supplied a plug-in; the framework runs the show.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHY USE A FRAMEWORK? (the &ldquo;pros&rdquo; question)</div>The main benefit: the framework supplies the <b>plumbing</b> — request handling, database wiring, security, configuration — so you write only business logic. Less boilerplate, faster development, tested infrastructure, consistent structure across teams. Trade-off: you follow the framework&rsquo;s conventions and learning curve.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">SPRING IS MODULAR</div>Spring isn&rsquo;t one blob — you pull in the <b>modules</b> you need: <b>Spring Core</b> (the IoC container/DI), <b>Spring MVC</b> (web layer), <b>Spring Data/JPA</b> (databases), <b>Spring Security</b> (auth), <b>Spring Test</b>. Spring Boot then auto-configures the modules you include.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>&ldquo;Your code calls a library, but a framework calls your code.&rdquo; This reversal is called:<ol type="A"><li>Inversion of Control</li><li>Encapsulation</li><li>Compilation</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — Inversion of Control (IoC).</b> The framework owns the program flow and invokes your plug-in code.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Java has hundreds of libraries and a handful of dominant frameworks. The distinction is not academic — it shapes how you structure code, what control you give up, and where you spend your time. Knowing the difference is the price of admission to professional Java work.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. A library: you call it.</strong> A library is code you call from your code. You decide when, where, and how. Apache Commons, Guava, Jackson — you import them and invoke them on your terms. Your code is in charge.</p>
<p><strong>2. A framework: it calls you.</strong> A framework provides the skeleton; you provide the flesh. The framework decides the lifecycle — when methods run, how requests flow, when objects are created. Spring, Jakarta EE, Quarkus all work this way. The 'Hollywood Principle' captures it: don't call us, we'll call you.</p>
<p><strong>3. Inversion of Control is the boundary.</strong> The defining characteristic of a framework is Inversion of Control. Control over creation, configuration, and lifecycle moves from your code to the framework. You annotate, configure, or implement interfaces; the framework wires it together at runtime.</p>
<p><strong>4. Why frameworks exist.</strong> They solve recurring problems — wiring objects together, handling HTTP, mapping objects to databases, managing transactions, validating input — once, well. Without a framework, every project reinvents these solutions, badly.</p>
<p><strong>5. The Java framework landscape.</strong> Spring (and Spring Boot) dominates enterprise Java. Jakarta EE (formerly Java EE) is the standards-based alternative. Quarkus and Micronaut target cloud-native and fast-startup use cases. Hibernate is the dominant ORM. Apache Struts is legacy but still around.</p>
<p><strong>6. The cost of frameworks.</strong> You learn the framework's conventions, debug through framework code, and inherit its upgrade cycle. A library is a tool you pick up; a framework is a relationship. Choose deliberately — switching frameworks is expensive.</p>
<p><strong>7. When NOT to use a framework.</strong> Tiny utilities, one-off scripts, libraries meant to be embedded in other applications. The framework's overhead and conventions are taxes you only want to pay when the benefits — wiring, lifecycle, integration — are worth it.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Calling everything a 'framework' when most of it is just libraries — blurs the conceptual line.</li>
 <li>Mixing two frameworks in one app and getting stuck between two competing lifecycles.</li>
 <li>Picking the trendiest framework rather than one your team understands.</li>
 <li>Underestimating learning curve — productivity drops before it climbs.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Libraries are code you call; frameworks call your code. Inversion of Control is the line between them. Spring, Jakarta EE, and Quarkus dominate Java frameworks. They solve real problems but impose conventions and lifecycle costs. Pick one deliberately and learn it deeply.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Library</span></dt>
 <dd>Reusable code you import and call from your application.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Framework</span></dt>
 <dd>A skeleton that calls your code via defined extension points and lifecycle hooks.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Inversion of Control (IoC)</span></dt>
 <dd>The framework, not your code, controls object creation and lifecycle.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Hollywood Principle</span></dt>
 <dd>'Don't call us, we'll call you' — describes how frameworks invoke your code.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Spring Boot</span></dt>
 <dd>Opinionated framework on top of Spring that auto-configures sensible defaults for fast project setup.</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c1s1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the same two classes, two directions of call — that direction is the whole distinction</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 306" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk182a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="18" y="26" width="316" height="268" rx="10" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.10" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="176" y="50" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">LIBRARY — you call it</text><rect x="66" y="68" width="220" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="176" y="90" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">your main()</text><text x="176" y="105" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">you own the entry point</text><line x1="176" y1="114" x2="176" y2="158" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk182a)"/><text x="240" y="140" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">you decide when</text><rect x="66" y="158" width="220" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="176" y="180" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Jackson.readValue(..)</text><text x="176" y="195" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a method, nothing more</text><path d="M66,181 Q66,111 66,91" fill="none" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk182a)"/><text x="38" y="138" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">returns</text><text x="176" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Your code sits on top of the stack.</text><text x="176" y="254" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Delete the library and your program</text><text x="176" y="271" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">still has a shape.</text><rect x="346" y="26" width="316" height="268" rx="10" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="504" y="50" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">FRAMEWORK — it calls you</text><rect x="394" y="68" width="220" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="504" y="90" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">SpringApplication.run</text><text x="504" y="105" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">it owns the entry point</text><line x1="504" y1="114" x2="504" y2="158" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk182a)"/><text x="578" y="140" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">it decides when</text><rect x="394" y="158" width="220" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="504" y="180" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@GetMapping show(..)</text><text x="504" y="195" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">your code, called back</text><path d="M394,181 Q394,111 394,91" fill="none" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk182a)"/><text x="366" y="138" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">returns</text><text x="504" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">The framework sits on top.</text><text x="504" y="254" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Delete Spring and your controller</text><text x="504" y="271" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">is an unreachable method.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — deciding which one you are looking at</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">You are handed four dependencies and asked which are frameworks. The test is never the size of the jar or how many features it has — it is <strong>which side owns the call</strong>.</p><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Find the <code>main</code> method. Whoever writes it owns control. With Apache Commons you write <code>main</code>; with Spring Boot you write a one-line <code>main</code> that hands control to <code>SpringApplication.run</code> and never gets it back.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Ask what happens if you delete your own code. A library sits there unused. A framework left alone still starts, still opens port 8080, and simply serves nothing — proof the lifecycle was never yours.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Look for extension points. Frameworks ask you to implement an interface, extend a class, or annotate a method so they can find it later. Libraries ask you to call a method now.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Check whether the dependency imposes structure on your project — a required directory layout, a required base package, a required annotation on a startup class. That is a framework claiming ownership.</li></ol><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Dependency</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">You call it / it calls you</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Verdict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Jackson (<code>ObjectMapper</code>)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">You call <code>readValue</code> when you want to</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Library</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Apache Commons Lang</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">You call <code>StringUtils.isBlank</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Library</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Spring Boot</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">It scans, constructs, and invokes your <code>@GetMapping</code> method</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Framework</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Hibernate</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Both — you call the <code>EntityManager</code>, but it calls your entity callbacks and manages the lifecycle</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Framework (with a library-shaped API)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Hibernate is the useful edge case. The exam wants <em>framework</em>, because Hibernate controls when your entities are loaded, flushed and dirty-checked — you never write that loop.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — a five-line framework, so you can see the inversion happen</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">This is a whole framework in miniature. It owns a routing table and the dispatch loop; you contribute one class that implements its interface. Compile and run it and the inversion is not a metaphor any more.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">import java.util.*;

interface Handler { String handle(String path); }

// YOUR code — the framework will call this. You never call it yourself.
class PartsHandler implements Handler {
    public String handle(String path) { return "rendering the parts list for " + path; }
}

// THE FRAMEWORK — it owns the table and the loop.
class TinyFramework {
    private final Map&lt;String, Handler&gt; routes = new LinkedHashMap&lt;&gt;();
    void register(String path, Handler h) { routes.put(path, h); }
    void dispatch(String path) {
        Handler h = routes.get(path);
        System.out.println(h == null ? "404 " + path : h.handle(path));
    }
}

public class A {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        TinyFramework app = new TinyFramework();
        app.register("/parts", new PartsHandler());
        app.dispatch("/parts");
        app.dispatch("/products");
    }
}</code></pre><div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.07em; color:var(--text-3); margin:14px 0 -2px;">OUTPUT — verified on JDK 21</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto; border-left:3px solid #10b981;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">rendering the parts list for /parts
404 /products</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><code>register</code> is the extension point. It is the only place your class and the framework meet.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><code>dispatch</code> is the inverted call. <code>PartsHandler.handle</code> runs, but no line you wrote invokes it — the framework looked it up and called it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">The 404 line proves the framework, not you, decides what happens when there is no match. That decision was never in your code.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Scale <code>TinyFramework</code> up by a hundred thousand lines — add annotation scanning instead of <code>register</code>, an HTTP server instead of <code>dispatch</code>, a view resolver for the returned string — and you have Spring MVC. The shape does not change.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Inversion of Control is not an advanced feature of a framework. It is the definition of one.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Questions here are almost always one sentence: <em>"What distinguishes a framework from a library?"</em> The answer key phrase is <strong>Inversion of Control</strong> — the framework calls your code (the Hollywood Principle, "don't call us, we'll call you"). Distractors talk about size, jar count, or "frameworks are bigger". Ignore all of them and pick the option that mentions who controls the flow. If an option names a specific tool, remember: Spring, Spring Boot, Jakarta EE, Quarkus, Micronaut and Struts are frameworks; Jackson, Guava, Apache Commons and SLF4J are libraries.</p></div><i data-d287cov_11="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">The four words the rest of the course assumes</h4><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div>A <strong>bean</strong> is simply <strong>an object managed by the Spring IoC container</strong>. Spring creates it, injects whatever it needs, keeps one instance (by default), and hands it to anything that asks. "Bean" is not a special kind of class — your own <code>PartService</code> becomes a bean the moment Spring is told to manage it.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div>A <strong>starter POM</strong> — <code>spring-boot-starter-web</code>, <code>-data-jpa</code>, <code>-thymeleaf</code> — is a <strong>curated set of dependencies</strong> for one job. Adding the one line pulls in everything needed and, crucially, versions that are known to work together. Without starters you would list Spring MVC, Jackson, an embedded Tomcat and validation separately and reconcile their versions yourself.</div><p><strong>Annotations</strong> are how you talk to the framework. They <strong>mark classes and methods for Spring to detect and process</strong> — <code>@Service</code> says "manage this as a bean", <code>@GetMapping</code> says "route this URL here". Spring scans for them at start-up and wires the application accordingly, which is why so much of this course is learning what each annotation asks for.</p><p>The project itself is built by <strong>Maven or Gradle</strong>, which handle compilation and dependency management. Maven reads <code>pom.xml</code>; Gradle reads <code>build.gradle</code>. Either downloads the starters and their transitive dependencies from a repository so you never manage jar files by hand.</p><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Bean = object Spring manages. Starter = curated dependency bundle. Annotation = instruction to the framework. Maven/Gradle = the build tool that fetches it all.</div>"},"D287/ch1/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch1-s2"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — IoC &amp; Dependency Injection (Spring&rsquo;s core trick)</h3><p><b>Dependency Injection (DI)</b> means: instead of a class building the objects it needs with <code>new</code>, those objects are <b>handed to it</b> (&ldquo;injected&rdquo;) by the Spring <b>container</b>. The objects Spring manages are called <b>beans</b>.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — without DI vs with DI</div><b>Without DI</b> (tight coupling):<br><pre>class OrderService {
    private EmailSender sender = new EmailSender(); // builds its own
}</pre>OrderService is welded to one concrete EmailSender — hard to swap, hard to test.<br><br><b>With DI</b> (Spring injects it):<br><pre>@Service
class OrderService {
    private final EmailSender sender;
    OrderService(EmailSender sender) {   // constructor injection
        this.sender = sender;
    }
}</pre>Spring sees OrderService needs an EmailSender, finds the EmailSender bean it manages, and passes it into the constructor automatically. In a test you can hand in a fake sender — no Spring needed.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE VOCABULARY</div>&bull; <b>Bean</b> — an object created and managed by the Spring container.<br>&bull; <b>ApplicationContext</b> — the container itself: it creates beans, wires them together, and manages their lifecycle.<br>&bull; <b>@Autowired / constructor injection</b> — how you mark where a dependency should be injected (constructor injection is the recommended style).<br>&bull; By default beans are <b>singletons</b>: one shared instance per container.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ WHY DI MATTERS (the exam answer)</div>DI gives <b>loose coupling</b> (swap implementations without editing the class) and <b>testability</b> (inject mocks). If a question asks &ldquo;why not just use new?&rdquo; — that&rsquo;s the answer.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>In Spring, an object that the container creates and manages is called a:<ol type="A"><li>Bean</li><li>Servlet</li><li>Package</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — a bean.</b> The ApplicationContext creates it, injects its dependencies, and manages its lifecycle.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Inversion of Control is a Spring superpower, and Dependency Injection is its main expression. Once you internalize DI, your code becomes more testable, less coupled, and dramatically easier to evolve. It is the single biggest mindset shift moving from plain Java to Spring.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. What dependency injection means.</strong> Instead of a class creating its own dependencies, the dependencies are 'injected' — passed in from outside. A PartService doesn't 'new' a PartRepository; it receives one in its constructor. Who creates the repository becomes someone else's problem (Spring's, in this case).</p>
<p><strong>2. Constructor injection (preferred).</strong> Pass dependencies through the constructor. The class declares what it needs; the framework provides it. Constructor injection makes dependencies explicit, supports immutability (final fields), and fails fast at startup if something is missing.</p>
<p><strong>3. Setter and field injection.</strong> Setter injection: provide setters; framework calls them. Field injection: annotate fields directly with @Autowired. Field injection is concise but harder to test and hides dependencies. Constructor injection is the modern default; field injection is discouraged in production code.</p>
<p><strong>4. Spring's annotations for DI.</strong> @Component (generic Spring-managed bean), @Service (business-logic layer), @Repository (data-access layer), @Controller / @RestController (web layer). All make the class a Spring bean. @Autowired marks an injection point — though on constructors with one argument, it is now optional.</p>
<p><strong>5. The Spring container manages lifecycle.</strong> Spring builds a graph of beans at startup: creating instances, wiring dependencies, invoking initialization methods. You stop saying 'new' and start declaring 'this needs that.' The container does the wiring.</p>
<p><strong>6. Bean scope: singleton vs prototype.</strong> Default Spring scope is singleton — one shared instance per container. Prototype scope creates a new instance per injection. Web-specific scopes (request, session) also exist. Most beans are singletons; most teams forget the others exist until they need them.</p>
<p><strong>7. Why all this matters: testability.</strong> Without DI, testing PartService means testing the database too — they are tangled. With DI, you inject a mock PartRepository and test PartService in isolation. This single property pays back the conceptual overhead many times over.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Field injection (@Autowired on fields) — concise but harder to test and obscures dependencies.</li>
 <li>Circular dependencies — A needs B, B needs A. Spring will fail at startup; redesign rather than work around.</li>
 <li>Forgetting the @Component / @Service / @Repository annotation — Spring won't find the class.</li>
 <li>Creating beans manually with 'new' inside a Spring app — bypasses the container's wiring entirely.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>IoC means Spring controls object creation; DI is how dependencies are supplied (typically through constructors). Annotate classes with stereotype annotations (@Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller). The container wires the graph at startup. The biggest payoff is testability — mocking dependencies becomes trivial.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Dependency Injection (DI)</span></dt>
 <dd>Pattern of supplying a class's dependencies from outside rather than constructing them internally.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Autowired</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring annotation marking a constructor, setter, or field as an injection point.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Bean</span></dt>
 <dd>An object instance managed by the Spring container.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Stereotype annotation</span></dt>
 <dd>@Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller — marks a class as a Spring-managed bean.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Singleton scope</span></dt>
 <dd>Default bean scope — one shared instance per container.</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c1s2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the container wiring beans: scan, construct leaf-first, inject, then open for traffic</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 330" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk183a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="26" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">startup: the container builds the graph from the leaves inward</text><rect x="24" y="60" width="150" height="92" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="99" y="110" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"></text><text x="99" y="82" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">your classpath</text><text x="99" y="102" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Repository PartRepo</text><text x="99" y="118" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Service PartService</text><text x="99" y="134" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Controller PartCtl</text><line x1="174" y1="106" x2="214" y2="106" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk183a)"/><text x="194" y="96" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">scan</text><rect x="214" y="52" width="442" height="176" rx="10" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.10" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="435" y="72" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ApplicationContext — the IoC container</text><rect x="232" y="92" width="122" height="52" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="293" y="117" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PartRepository</text><text x="293" y="132" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">no dependencies</text><rect x="374" y="92" width="122" height="52" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="435" y="117" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PartService</text><text x="435" y="132" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">needs a repo</text><rect x="516" y="92" width="122" height="52" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="577" y="117" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PartController</text><text x="577" y="132" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">needs a service</text><line x1="354" y1="118" x2="372" y2="118" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk183a)"/><line x1="496" y1="118" x2="514" y2="118" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk183a)"/><circle cx="293" cy="162" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="293" y="166" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text x="293" y="186" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">built first</text><circle cx="435" cy="162" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="435" y="166" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text x="435" y="186" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">gets the repository</text><circle cx="577" cy="162" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="577" y="166" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="577" y="186" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">gets the service</text><text x="435" y="212" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">construction order runs leaf → root; nothing is ever half-built</text><line x1="435" y1="228" x2="435" y2="250" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk183a)"/><rect x="250" y="250" width="370" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="435" y="276" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">context ready — HTTP requests may now arrive</text><text x="340" y="316" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">A missing @Service or an ambiguous type fails HERE, at startup — not at request time.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — watching the container's construction order, in plain Java</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Spring builds the bean graph depth-first: before it can construct <code>PartService</code> it must have a <code>PartRepository</code> to pass in. This snippet does by hand exactly what the container does by reflection, so the order becomes visible.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">class PartRepository {
    PartRepository() { System.out.println("2. PartRepository constructed"); }
}
class PartService {
    private final PartRepository repo;
    PartService(PartRepository repo) {           // constructor injection
        this.repo = repo;
        System.out.println("3. PartService constructed with a " + repo.getClass().getSimpleName());
    }
    PartRepository repo() { return repo; }
}
class PartController {
    private final PartService service;
    PartController(PartService service) {
        this.service = service;
        System.out.println("4. PartController constructed");
    }
    PartService service() { return service; }
}

public class B {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("1. container starting");
        PartRepository repo = new PartRepository();
        PartService service = new PartService(repo);
        PartController controller = new PartController(service);
        System.out.println("same repository instance? " + (controller.service().repo() == repo));
    }
}</code></pre><div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.07em; color:var(--text-3); margin:14px 0 -2px;">OUTPUT — verified on JDK 21</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto; border-left:3px solid #10b981;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">1. container starting
2. PartRepository constructed
3. PartService constructed with a PartRepository
4. PartController constructed
same repository instance? true</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">The repository is built first because it needs nothing. Spring calls this a leaf of the dependency graph.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><code>PartService</code> cannot exist until it has a repository — its only constructor demands one. That is what makes constructor injection <em>fail fast</em>: an unsatisfiable dependency is a startup error, not a null pointer three days later.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Every field is <code>final</code>. Once the container hands the object over it is fully formed and cannot be re-wired. Field injection cannot give you that.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">The last line prints <code>true</code>: one <code>PartRepository</code> object reached the controller through the service. In Spring that is the singleton scope doing the same thing — one instance shared by everyone who asked for the type.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Add the annotations — <code>@Repository</code>, <code>@Service</code>, <code>@Controller</code> — and delete the three <code>new</code> calls, and this file becomes a Spring application with identical behaviour.</p></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — rewriting a tightly coupled class so it can be tested</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Here is the class the exam shows you and asks you to fix:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Service
public class PartService {
    private final PartRepository repo = new PartRepository();   // &lt;-- the problem

    public Part save(Part part) { return repo.save(part); }
}</code></pre><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">It compiles, it runs, and it is untestable. A unit test of <code>save</code> also tests whatever <code>new PartRepository()</code> connects to.</p><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Name what is wrong.</strong> The class both <em>uses</em> a repository and <em>decides which</em> repository. Those are two responsibilities, and the second one is the container's job.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Move the decision out.</strong> Declare the dependency in the constructor and keep the field final:</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Drop @Autowired.</strong> Since Spring 4.3 a class with exactly one constructor needs no <code>@Autowired</code> — the container uses it automatically. Writing it is not wrong, just noise.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Check the payoff in a test.</strong> No Spring context, no database, no annotations:</li></ol><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Service
public class PartService {
    private final PartRepository repo;

    public PartService(PartRepository repo) {   // Spring supplies it
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    public Part save(Part part) { return repo.save(part); }
}</code></pre><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Test
void saveDelegatesToTheRepository() {
    PartRepository fake = mock(PartRepository.class);
    PartService service = new PartService(fake);      // plain constructor call

    service.save(new Inhouse("Brake pad"));

    verify(fake).save(any(Part.class));
}</code></pre><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Constructor injection: the dependency is declared, immutable, satisfiable at startup, and replaceable in a test. That last property is the reason the pattern exists.</div></div><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — bean scopes: how many objects the container actually creates</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 340" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk184a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">two beans ask the container for the same type — how many objects come back?</text><rect x="20" y="40" width="640" height="106" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="126" y="60" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Scope("singleton")</text><text x="126" y="76" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the default — you never write it</text><rect x="240" y="52" width="150" height="32" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="315" y="72" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PartController</text><rect x="240" y="96" width="150" height="32" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="315" y="116" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ReportController</text><line x1="390" y1="68" x2="468" y2="82" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk184a)"/><line x1="390" y1="112" x2="468" y2="98" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk184a)"/><rect x="468" y="70" width="172" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="554" y="89" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PartService#1</text><text x="554" y="104" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">one shared instance</text><text x="126" y="122" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">so it must be stateless</text><rect x="20" y="156" width="640" height="122" rx="10" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="126" y="176" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Scope("prototype")</text><text x="126" y="192" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">a new object at every</text><text x="126" y="206" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">injection point</text><rect x="240" y="168" width="150" height="32" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="315" y="188" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PartController</text><rect x="240" y="224" width="150" height="32" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="315" y="244" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ReportController</text><line x1="390" y1="184" x2="468" y2="184" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk184a)"/><line x1="390" y1="240" x2="468" y2="240" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk184a)"/><rect x="468" y="166" width="172" height="36" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="554" y="188" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ImportJob#1</text><rect x="468" y="222" width="172" height="36" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="554" y="244" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ImportJob#2</text><text x="126" y="240" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">so state is safe, and</text><text x="126" y="254" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">nothing is shared</text><text x="340" y="304" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Singleton: one object per ApplicationContext. Prototype: one per injection point.</text><text x="340" y="324" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">The web scopes sit between them — one instance per HTTP request, or per user session.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — proving the difference between singleton and prototype scope</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Scope is a counting question: how many objects exist? This runnable model uses a map for the singleton scope and a plain factory for the prototype scope — which is, stripped of reflection, what the container does.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">import java.util.*;
import java.util.function.Supplier;

class AuditStamp {
    static int made = 0;
    final int serial;
    AuditStamp() { serial = ++made; }
}

public class C {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Map&lt;String, AuditStamp&gt; singletons = new HashMap&lt;&gt;();
        Supplier&lt;AuditStamp&gt; singletonScope = () -&gt; singletons.computeIfAbsent("auditStamp", k -&gt; new AuditStamp());
        Supplier&lt;AuditStamp&gt; prototypeScope = AuditStamp::new;

        System.out.println("singleton scope: " + singletonScope.get().serial + ", " + singletonScope.get().serial);
        System.out.println("prototype scope: " + prototypeScope.get().serial + ", " + prototypeScope.get().serial);
        System.out.println("objects actually created: " + AuditStamp.made);
    }
}</code></pre><div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.07em; color:var(--text-3); margin:14px 0 -2px;">OUTPUT — verified on JDK 21</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto; border-left:3px solid #10b981;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">singleton scope: 1, 1
prototype scope: 2, 3
objects actually created: 3</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Two lookups in singleton scope return serial 1 twice — <code>computeIfAbsent</code> caches, exactly like the container's singleton registry.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Two lookups in prototype scope return 2 and 3 — different objects. The container calls the constructor again every time.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Three objects for four lookups. That arithmetic is the whole of the topic.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">One consequence follows immediately: a singleton bean is shared by every concurrent request, so a mutable field on a <code>@Service</code> is a race condition. Prototype beans have no such problem, and no such sharing either.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Default scope is singleton — one instance per <code>ApplicationContext</code>, not one per JVM. Two contexts in one JVM means two "singletons".</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two things get asked. <strong>(1) Which injection style?</strong> The answer is constructor injection — it allows <code>final</code> fields, makes dependencies explicit, and fails at startup rather than at runtime. Field injection (<code>@Autowired</code> on a field) is always the wrong answer when "preferred" or "recommended" appears in the stem. <strong>(2) What is the default scope?</strong> Singleton. If a question describes two beans of the same type and a startup failure, the exception name is <code>NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException</code> and the two fixes are <code>@Primary</code> (pick a default) and <code>@Qualifier("name")</code> (pick at the injection point).</p></div><i data-d287cov_12="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">Dependency injection, and why it is worth the indirection</h4><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>Dependency Injection</strong> is the container <strong>supplying an object's dependencies from outside</strong>, rather than the object constructing them itself.</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">// without DI — the controller decides what it uses
class PartController {
    private PartService service = new PartService();   // welded in
}

// with DI — it declares what it needs and is handed one
@Controller
class PartController {
    private final PartService service;
    PartController(PartService service) { this.service = service; }
}</pre><p><strong>DI improves testability</strong> for one concrete reason: in the second version you can <strong>pass a mock dependency into the class under test</strong>. In the first you cannot — the real <code>PartService</code> is hard-coded, so testing the controller drags in the service, its repository, and a database.</p><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>If a question asks why DI helps testing, the answer is always about substituting a test double. Everything else — swappable implementations, looser coupling — follows from the same property.</div>"},"D287/ch1/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch1-s3"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — The Spring Ecosystem (what each project is FOR)</h3><p>&ldquo;Spring&rdquo; is a family. The exam wants you to match the project to its job:</p><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE FAMILY, ONE LINE EACH</div>&bull; <b>Spring Framework (Core)</b> — the IoC container + DI. Everything builds on this.<br>&bull; <b>Spring Boot</b> — opinionated quick-start: auto-configuration, embedded server (Tomcat), starter dependencies. &ldquo;Just run it.&rdquo;<br>&bull; <b>Spring MVC</b> — the web layer: controllers, request mappings, views.<br>&bull; <b>Spring Data JPA</b> — database access with almost no code: repository interfaces generate the queries.<br>&bull; <b>Spring Security</b> — authentication &amp; authorization.<br>&bull; <b>Spring Cloud</b> — building blocks for microservices (config, discovery, gateways).</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — pick the project from the need</div>&bull; &ldquo;We want login with roles&rdquo; → <b>Spring Security</b>.<br>&bull; &ldquo;We want to ship a runnable web app without configuring a server&rdquo; → <b>Spring Boot</b> (embedded Tomcat).<br>&bull; &ldquo;We want CRUD against MySQL without writing SQL&rdquo; → <b>Spring Data JPA</b>.<br>&bull; &ldquo;We&rsquo;re splitting into microservices and need service discovery&rdquo; → <b>Spring Cloud</b>.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ BOOT vs FRAMEWORK</div>Spring Boot is NOT a replacement for Spring — it sits ON TOP, pre-configuring it. The tell for Boot questions: <b>auto-configuration</b>, <b>starters</b> (spring-boot-starter-web), and the <b>embedded server</b> — no external Tomcat install, no XML.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Auto-configuration, starter dependencies, and an embedded Tomcat are hallmarks of:<ol type="A"><li>Spring Boot</li><li>Spring Security</li><li>Spring Cloud</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — Spring Boot.</b> Its job is to make a Spring app runnable with near-zero setup.</div></details></div></div><div class="vl" data-defglance="1"><div class="vl-box vl-rule def-glance"><div class="t">📖 DEFINITIONS AT A GLANCE — every key term in this section</div></div><table><tr><th style="width:32%">Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr><tr><td><b>IoC container</b></td><td>manages object creation and dependency injection AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming) — cross-cutting concerns like logging and transactions Spring MVC — the original web framework, built on Servlets JDBC abstraction, transaction management…</td></tr><tr><td><b>AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming)</b></td><td>cross-cutting concerns like logging and transactions Spring MVC — the original web framework, built on Servlets JDBC abstraction, transaction management You rarely use the core alone today; Spring Boot wraps it with sensible defaults.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spring MVC</b></td><td>the original web framework, built on Servlets JDBC abstraction, transaction management You rarely use the core alone today; Spring Boot wraps it with sensible defaults.</td></tr><tr><td><b>JDBC abstraction, transaction management</b></td><td>You rarely use the core alone today; Spring Boot wraps it with sensible defaults.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spring Framework</b></td><td>is the core IoC container, AOP, MVC, etc.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spring Boot</b></td><td>is an opinionated layer ON TOP of Spring that auto-configures everything and gives you starter dependencies.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Starter dependencies</b></td><td>bundle coherent jars: starter-web, starter-data-jpa, starter-security, etc.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spring Data</b></td><td>gives you repository interfaces; Spring writes the implementations Spring MVC = synchronous web; Spring WebFlux = reactive, non-blocking web Spring Security handles authentication and authorization via filters Spring Cloud is for microse…</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spring WebFlux</b></td><td>reactive, non-blocking web Spring Security handles authentication and authorization via filters Spring Cloud is for microservices (service discovery, config, gateways) Spring Test gives you slice tests like @DataJpaTest , @WebMvcTest , an</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spring Security</b></td><td>handles authentication and authorization via filters Spring Cloud is for microservices (service discovery, config, gateways) Spring Test gives you slice tests like @DataJpaTest , @WebMvcTest , and @MockBean For a typical web app: starter…</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spring Cloud</b></td><td>is for microservices (service discovery, config, gateways) Spring Test gives you slice tests like @DataJpaTest , @WebMvcTest , and @MockBean For a typical web app: starter-web + starter-data-jpa + starter-security covers 90% of needs</td></tr><tr><td><b>Spring Test</b></td><td>gives you slice tests like @DataJpaTest , @WebMvcTest , and @MockBean For a typical web app: starter-web + starter-data-jpa + starter-security covers 90% of needs</td></tr></table><div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-3);margin:-6px 0 14px">Full lesson with examples below ⬇️ — nothing removed.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
<div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
<p>Spring is not one framework — it's a constellation. Spring Framework provides the core; Spring Boot wraps it with sensible defaults; Spring Data, Spring Security, Spring Cloud, Spring MVC, Spring WebFlux and a dozen others extend it. New developers get overwhelmed because they don't know what each piece does or when to use it. This section maps the Spring landscape so you can navigate it confidently and pick the right pieces for any project. The WGU exam tests vocabulary — knowing what each Spring module is for separates the engineer from the cargo-culter.</p>
</div>

<h3>Spring Framework (the core)</h3>

<p>The original. Provides:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>IoC container</strong> — manages object creation and dependency injection</li>
<li><strong>AOP (Aspect-Oriented Programming)</strong> — cross-cutting concerns like logging and transactions</li>
<li><strong>Spring MVC</strong> — the original web framework, built on Servlets</li>
<li><strong>JDBC abstraction, transaction management</strong></li>
</ul>

<p>You rarely use the core alone today; Spring Boot wraps it with sensible defaults.</p>

<h3>Spring Boot — the convention layer</h3>

<p>Spring Boot adds opinionated auto-configuration, embedded servers (Tomcat by default), and starter dependencies. A Spring Boot app runs as a fat JAR — one file containing your code, all dependencies, and an embedded servlet container. No external Tomcat to install.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@SpringBootApplication
public class MyApp {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(MyApp.class, args);
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>That's a complete web app. With one <code>@SpringBootApplication</code> annotation, you get component scanning, auto-config, and a runnable program. Add <code>spring-boot-starter-web</code> to pom.xml and you have an HTTP server. This is why Spring Boot dominates Java back-end development.</p>

<h3>Starter dependencies</h3>

<p>Maven/Gradle dependencies that bundle a coherent set of jars:</p>

<table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;">
<thead><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--surface-3);">
<th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Starter</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">What you get</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>spring-boot-starter-web</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Spring MVC, embedded Tomcat, Jackson, validation</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Spring Data JPA, Hibernate, transactions</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>spring-boot-starter-security</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Spring Security, default login forms</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>spring-boot-starter-thymeleaf</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Thymeleaf templating engine</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>spring-boot-starter-test</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">JUnit, Mockito, Spring test support</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>spring-boot-starter-actuator</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Production endpoints: health, metrics, info</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>spring-boot-starter-validation</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Bean validation (JSR-380)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>spring-boot-starter-data-mongodb</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Spring Data MongoDB</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Spring Data — repositories for any data store</h3>

<p>Spring Data provides a unified abstraction over data stores. Define an interface; Spring writes the implementation.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>public interface UserRepository extends JpaRepository<User, Long> {
    List<User> findByLastName(String lastName);
    Optional<User> findByEmail(String email);
    @Query("SELECT u FROM User u WHERE u.active = true")
    List<User> findAllActive();
}
</code></pre>

<p>No <code>impl</code> class needed. Spring infers the SQL from the method name (<code>findByEmail</code> → <code>WHERE email = ?</code>) and generates the implementation at runtime. Variants exist for MongoDB, Cassandra, Redis, Neo4j, Elasticsearch, etc.</p>

<h3>Spring MVC — web requests</h3>

<p>The classic synchronous web framework. Controllers map URL patterns to methods.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/users")
public class UserController {
    private final UserRepository repo;

    public UserController(UserRepository repo) {
        this.repo = repo;
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")
    public User get(@PathVariable Long id) {
        return repo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    }

    @PostMapping
    public User create(@RequestBody User u) {
        return repo.save(u);
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h3>Spring WebFlux — reactive web</h3>

<p>For applications that need to handle thousands of concurrent connections (chat, streaming, etc.), Spring WebFlux offers a non-blocking, reactive alternative using <code>Mono</code> and <code>Flux</code> (from Project Reactor) instead of plain return types.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@GetMapping("/{id}")
public Mono<User> get(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return userRepo.findById(id);
}
</code></pre>

<p>Use WebFlux when you have a real reactive need. For typical CRUD apps, stick with the simpler Spring MVC.</p>

<h3>Spring Security — authentication and authorization</h3>

<p>Adds filters around HTTP requests to enforce login, role checks, CSRF protection, CORS, etc. Hugely customizable.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@Configuration
@EnableWebSecurity
public class SecurityConfig {
    @Bean
    public SecurityFilterChain filterChain(HttpSecurity http) throws Exception {
        http
            .authorizeHttpRequests(auth -> auth
                .requestMatchers("/api/public/**").permitAll()
                .requestMatchers("/api/admin/**").hasRole("ADMIN")
                .anyRequest().authenticated())
            .formLogin();
        return http.build();
    }
}
</code></pre>

<h3>Spring Cloud — microservices toolkit</h3>

<p>For distributed systems: service discovery (Eureka), config server, circuit breakers (Resilience4j), API gateways (Spring Cloud Gateway), distributed tracing. Used when you have multiple Spring Boot services that need to find and call each other.</p>

<h3>Spring Batch — large-scale data processing</h3>

<p>Framework for batch jobs: reading from a source, processing in chunks, writing to a destination, with restart and retry semantics. Heavily used in finance, ETL pipelines, and report generation.</p>

<h3>Spring Integration — messaging and EAI</h3>

<p>Enterprise Application Integration patterns: channels, transformers, splitters, adapters for FTP/JMS/AMQP/Kafka. Less used now than dedicated event-streaming platforms, but still relevant for legacy integration.</p>

<h3>Spring Test — testing support</h3>

<p>Spring Test gives you <code>@SpringBootTest</code> (load the full context for integration tests), <code>@DataJpaTest</code> (just the JPA layer with an in-memory DB), <code>@WebMvcTest</code> (just the web layer with mocked services), and <code>@MockBean</code> (replace a real bean with a Mockito mock).</p>

<h3>Module relationships</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>          Spring Boot (auto-config, starters, embedded server)
                              │
        ┌─────────────────────┼─────────────────────┐
        │                     │                     │
   Spring Web          Spring Data           Spring Security
        │                     │                     │
   Spring MVC          Spring Data JPA      Filter chain
   Spring WebFlux      Spring Data Mongo    OAuth2, JWT, etc.
                              │
                       Spring Core (IoC, AOP, beans)
</code></pre>

<h3>How to pick the right pieces</h3>

<table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;">
<thead><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--surface-3);">
<th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Need</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Use</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">CRUD web API</td><td style="padding:8px;">spring-boot-starter-web + spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Server-rendered HTML pages</td><td style="padding:8px;">spring-boot-starter-thymeleaf</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Login / role-based access</td><td style="padding:8px;">spring-boot-starter-security</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">High-concurrency streaming API</td><td style="padding:8px;">spring-boot-starter-webflux</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Health endpoints, metrics</td><td style="padding:8px;">spring-boot-starter-actuator</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Nightly ETL job</td><td style="padding:8px;">spring-batch</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;">Microservices with service registry</td><td style="padding:8px;">Spring Cloud</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Spring vs Spring Boot — the question that confuses everyone</h3>

<ul>
<li><strong>Spring Framework</strong> is the core IoC container, AOP, MVC, etc. — a powerful but verbose toolkit.</li>
<li><strong>Spring Boot</strong> is an opinionated layer ON TOP of Spring that auto-configures everything and gives you starter dependencies. You almost always want Spring Boot today.</li>
</ul>

<p>Saying "Spring Boot app" implies "Spring + Boot + sensible defaults + embedded Tomcat + everything just works." Saying "Spring app" alone implies you're configuring the old way (XML or extensive Java config). For D287 (Spring Boot Frameworks), you'll be using Spring Boot exclusively.</p>

<div class="callout callout-recall">
<div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div>
<ul>
<li><strong>Spring Framework:</strong> the core IoC container and supporting abstractions</li>
<li><strong>Spring Boot:</strong> opinionated, auto-configuring layer on top — almost always what you want</li>
<li><strong>Starter dependencies</strong> bundle coherent jars: starter-web, starter-data-jpa, starter-security, etc.</li>
<li><strong>Spring Data</strong> gives you repository interfaces; Spring writes the implementations</li>
<li><strong>Spring MVC</strong> = synchronous web; <strong>Spring WebFlux</strong> = reactive, non-blocking web</li>
<li><strong>Spring Security</strong> handles authentication and authorization via filters</li>
<li><strong>Spring Cloud</strong> is for microservices (service discovery, config, gateways)</li>
<li><strong>Spring Test</strong> gives you slice tests like <code>@DataJpaTest</code>, <code>@WebMvcTest</code>, and <code>@MockBean</code></li>
<li>For a typical web app: starter-web + starter-data-jpa + starter-security covers 90% of needs</li>
</ul>
</div>
