ddle">tomcat-embed-core</text><text x="506" y="164" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the server, inside your jar</text><path d="M234,84 Q266,125.80000000000001 298,160" fill="none" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk185a)"/><rect x="300" y="186" width="190" height="32" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="395" y="206" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">jackson-databind</text><text x="506" y="206" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">objects to and from JSON</text><path d="M234,84 Q266,148.9 298,202" fill="none" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk185a)"/><rect x="300" y="228" width="190" height="32" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="395" y="248" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">spring-boot-starter-json</text><text x="506" y="248" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the converters that use it</text><path d="M234,84 Q266,172 298,244" fill="none" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk185a)"/><text x="129" y="128" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a starter is</text><text x="129" y="146" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a shopping list,</text><text x="129" y="164" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not a library —</text><text x="129" y="182" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">it contains no</text><text x="129" y="200" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">code of its own</text><line x1="395" y1="262" x2="395" y2="292" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk185a)"/><rect x="250" y="292" width="290" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="395" y="316" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">auto-configuration reads the classpath</text><text x="340" y="356" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Tomcat on the classpath ⇒ Boot starts a web server. Remove the jar and that configuration silently stops applying.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — choosing the dependency set for the D287 inventory application</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">You are at start.spring.io with an empty dependency box and the PA brief: server-rendered HTML pages for parts and products, stored in an in-memory database, with validation rules on the forms. Pick the starters by working backwards from each requirement.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Requirement in the brief</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Starter to tick</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">What it puts on the classpath</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">HTTP endpoints and MVC controllers</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>spring-boot-starter-web</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring MVC, embedded Tomcat, Jackson</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Server-rendered HTML pages</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>spring-boot-starter-thymeleaf</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Thymeleaf engine + the Spring dialect</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Parts and products in a database</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>spring-boot-starter-data-jpa</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Data JPA, Hibernate, transactions</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">No database to install</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>com.h2database:h2</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">The H2 engine (a driver, not a starter)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Form validation with messages</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>spring-boot-starter-validation</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Jakarta Validation + Hibernate Validator</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Restart on save while coding</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>spring-boot-devtools</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Automatic restart (development only)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Web, not WebFlux.</strong> WebFlux is the reactive stack and returns <code>Mono</code>/<code>Flux</code>. An inventory CRUD app has no reactive need, and mixing the two stacks in one project causes hard-to-read failures. Take the simpler one.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Validation is a separate starter.</strong> This trips people up: <code>starter-web</code> stopped bundling the validator in Boot 2.3. Without <code>starter-validation</code>, your <code>@NotBlank</code> annotations compile and are simply never evaluated.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>H2 is not a starter.</strong> It is the database engine jar. Spring Boot notices it on the classpath and auto-configures a <code>DataSource</code> pointing at an in-memory database — no URL required for the simplest case.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Nothing else.</strong> No Security (the PA has no login), no Actuator, no Cloud. Every extra starter is more auto-configuration that can fail at startup and more surface area to explain in the write-up.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: web + thymeleaf + data-jpa + validation + the H2 driver, plus devtools while you work. Five lines in <code>pom.xml</code>.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — reading the module map to answer "where does X live?"</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Exam questions in this section are vocabulary placement: given a capability, name the Spring project. Work down the stack.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">You need to…</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Project</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Not to be confused with</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Create objects and inject them</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Framework (core container)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Boot, which only configures the core</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Skip the XML and boot in one line</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Boot</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Framework, which still needs configuring</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Map URLs to methods, render views</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring MVC (inside Spring Framework)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring WebFlux, the reactive alternative</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Get CRUD without writing a DAO</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Data JPA</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">JPA/Hibernate, which Spring Data sits on top of</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Require a login, guard a URL by role</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Security</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Cloud Gateway</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Handle a nightly file-import job</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Batch</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Integration (messaging, not chunked jobs)</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Let ten services find each other</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Cloud</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Boot alone, which knows nothing of peers</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Boot only the JPA layer in a test</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Test (<code>@DataJpaTest</code>)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>@SpringBootTest</code>, which loads everything</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The one distinction worth memorising word for word:</p><ul style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Spring Framework</strong> is the IoC container, AOP and MVC. It is a toolkit and it configures nothing for you.</li><li style="margin-bottom:5px;"><strong>Spring Boot</strong> is an opinionated layer <em>on top of</em> Spring Framework: auto-configuration, starter dependencies, an embedded server and an executable fat jar. It adds no new dependency-injection ability whatsoever.</li></ul><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Spring Boot is not a replacement for Spring — it is Spring plus defaults. Any option claiming Boot "replaces" or "is an alternative to" the Spring Framework is wrong.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">This section is pure matching, and it is cheap marks. Memorise the eight project names with one clause each (Data = repositories, Security = filters and roles, Cloud = microservices, Batch = chunked jobs, WebFlux = reactive, MVC = synchronous web, Boot = auto-config and starters, Framework = the container). Two traps recur: <strong>a starter contains no code</strong> — it is a list of other dependencies — and <strong>Spring Boot sits on Spring Framework</strong>, it does not replace it. If a stem mentions an embedded Tomcat and a runnable jar, the answer is Spring Boot; if it mentions <code>Mono</code> or <code>Flux</code>, the answer is WebFlux.</p></div><i data-d287cov_13="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">Starting a project, and what loose coupling buys</h4><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>Spring Initializr</strong> — the site at <strong>start.spring.io</strong> — <strong>generates a new project skeleton</strong> with the dependencies you tick. You choose Maven or Gradle, the Java and Boot versions, and starters such as Web, JPA, Thymeleaf and H2; it hands back a zip with the build file, the main application class and the standard directory layout already correct. Most IDEs embed the same tool in their "new Spring project" wizard.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>Loose coupling</strong> means <strong>components interact through abstractions, minimising direct dependencies</strong>. A controller that depends on a <code>PartService</code> interface does not care which implementation arrives, so the implementation can be swapped, mocked or rewritten without touching the controller. Dependency injection is the mechanism; loose coupling is the property it produces.</div>"},"D287/ch2/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch2-s1"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Project Setup &amp; Structure (what every file is)</h3><p>A Spring Boot project comes from <b>Spring Initializr</b> (start.spring.io): pick Maven/Gradle, Java version, and starters — it generates the skeleton.</p><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE SKELETON, FILE BY FILE</div><pre>pom.xml                    ← Maven build file: dependencies (starters) live here
src/main/java/...          ← your code; Application.java at the root package
src/main/resources/
  application.properties   ← configuration (port, DB url, ...)
  templates/               ← Thymeleaf HTML templates
  static/                  ← css/js/images served as-is
src/test/java/...          ← tests</pre>&bull; A <b>starter</b> (e.g. <code>spring-boot-starter-web</code>) is one Maven dependency that pulls in a whole matched set (MVC + JSON + embedded Tomcat).</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — add a database to the project</div>Need JPA + H2? Add to pom.xml:<br><pre>&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;org.springframework.boot&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-boot-starter-data-jpa&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
&lt;dependency&gt;
  &lt;groupId&gt;com.h2database&lt;/groupId&gt;
  &lt;artifactId&gt;h2&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;</pre>Rebuild — Boot detects JPA + H2 on the classpath and auto-configures a datasource. That&rsquo;s the Boot pattern: <b>add the starter, get the setup free</b>.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ WHERE Application.java LIVES MATTERS</div>The main class sits in the <b>root package</b>; component scanning finds beans in its package and BELOW. Put a controller in a package outside that tree and Spring silently never finds it — a classic &ldquo;my page 404s&rdquo; bug.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Thymeleaf template files belong in:<ol type="A"><li>src/main/resources/templates/</li><li>src/main/java/</li><li>the project root</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> templates/ under resources is where Thymeleaf looks; static/ serves unchanged assets like CSS.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>A new Spring Boot project has a specific structure that the framework expects. Following the conventions means everything 'just works' — auto-configuration finds your classes, tests run, the embedded server starts. Fighting the conventions costs hours.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Generating with Spring Initializr.</strong> Pick Maven or Gradle, Java version, packaging (Jar — almost always), language (Java), dependencies (Web, JPA, Thymeleaf, H2, Validation, DevTools for the PA). Download, unzip, open in IntelliJ or Eclipse. The skeleton is ready to run.</p>
<p><strong>2. The standard directory layout.</strong> src/main/java holds source code. src/main/resources holds configuration (application.properties), templates (templates/), and static assets (static/). src/test/java holds tests. pom.xml (Maven) or build.gradle (Gradle) declares dependencies. This layout is what auto-configuration assumes.</p>
<p><strong>3. The base package matters.</strong> Whatever package contains your @SpringBootApplication class becomes the root of component scanning. Spring discovers controllers, services, and repositories under that package. Putting things outside it is a common 'why doesn't Spring find my class?' source.</p>
<p><strong>4. pom.xml essentials.</strong> &lt;parent tag points to spring-boot-starter-parent (gives you sensible defaults). &lt;dependencies lists starters. &lt;build&lt;plugins includes spring-boot-maven-plugin to package the fat JAR. Most of the file is generated; you mainly edit dependencies.</p>
<p><strong>5. application.properties (or .yml).</strong> Lives in src/main/resources. Configures port, datasource, logging, JPA settings, anything. server.port=8080 sets the HTTP port; spring.datasource.url=... sets the database. YAML is hierarchical and often more readable for nested config.</p>
<p><strong>6. Running the application.</strong> From the IDE: right-click the @SpringBootApplication class → Run. From the command line: `./mvnw spring-boot:run` or `java -jar target/app.jar` after building. The embedded Tomcat starts; logs show the URL (typically http://localhost:8080).</p>
<p><strong>7. Spring Boot DevTools.</strong> Optional dependency that auto-restarts the application when classes change. Speeds up the edit-compile-test loop. Useful in development; should not be in production builds (it slows startup and changes classloader behavior).</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Putting controllers or services in a package outside the @SpringBootApplication class's package — component scanning misses them.</li>
 <li>Forgetting the spring-boot-maven-plugin in pom.xml — the resulting JAR isn't executable as a fat JAR.</li>
 <li>Editing application.properties without restarting (or without DevTools) — changes don't take effect.</li>
 <li>Hard-coding values (URLs, credentials) in Java code instead of in application.properties.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Generate with Spring Initializr. Follow the standard directory layout. Keep all your code under the @SpringBootApplication's package or below. Configure via application.properties. Run with the IDE or `mvnw spring-boot:run`. DevTools speeds development.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Spring Initializr</span></dt>
 <dd>start.spring.io — generates a Spring Boot project skeleton.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">pom.xml</span></dt>
 <dd>Maven's build configuration file declaring dependencies, plugins, and project metadata.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">application.properties</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Boot's main configuration file (key=value format) in src/main/resources.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Component scanning</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring's process of discovering @Component-annotated classes under the base package.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">DevTools</span></dt>
 <dd>Optional Spring Boot dependency providing automatic restart on code changes.</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c2s1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the scan radius: why a correctly annotated class can still be invisible to Spring</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 306" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk186a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="26" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">component scanning starts where @SpringBootApplication sits, and only looks downward</text><rect x="30" y="46" width="400" height="250" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="230" y="68" fill="#10b981" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">com.example.inventory</text><rect x="56" y="82" width="348" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="230" y="101" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">InventoryApplication</text><text x="230" y="116" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">@SpringBootApplication — the scan root</text><line x1="72" y1="122" x2="72" y2="148" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><line x1="72" y1="148" x2="88" y2="148" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="94" y="152" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">.controller</text><text x="186" y="152" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">PartController</text><text x="300" y="152" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Controller</text><text x="390" y="152" fill="#10b981" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✓</text><line x1="72" y1="122" x2="72" y2="186" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><line x1="72" y1="186" x2="88" y2="186" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="94" y="190" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">.service</text><text x="186" y="190" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">PartService</text><text x="300" y="190" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Service</text><text x="390" y="190" fill="#10b981" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✓</text><line x1="72" y1="122" x2="72" y2="224" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><line x1="72" y1="224" x2="88" y2="224" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="94" y="228" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">.repository</text><text x="186" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">PartRepository</text><text x="300" y="228" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Repository</text><text x="390" y="228" fill="#10b981" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✓</text><line x1="72" y1="122" x2="72" y2="262" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><line x1="72" y1="262" x2="88" y2="262" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="94" y="266" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">.entity</text><text x="186" y="266" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Part, Product</text><text x="300" y="266" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Entity</text><text x="390" y="266" fill="#10b981" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✓</text><text x="230" y="288" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">found: the container creates a bean for each</text><rect x="456" y="100" width="200" height="120" rx="10" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="556" y="124" fill="#e11d48" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">com.example.util</text><rect x="474" y="140" width="164" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="556" y="159" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">MailerService</text><text x="556" y="174" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">@Service</text><text x="556" y="202" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✗ never scanned</text><line x1="430" y1="160" x2="454" y2="160" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="443" y="148" fill="#e11d48" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✗</text><text x="556" y="252" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Injecting it fails at startup with</text><text x="556" y="268" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">NoSuchBeanDefinitionException.</text><text x="556" y="288" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Sibling package, not a child.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — placing a new class so the container will find it</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">You add a <code>PricingService</code> and inject it into <code>PartController</code>. The application refuses to start:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">Parameter 1 of constructor in com.example.inventory.controller.PartController
required a bean of type 'com.example.pricing.PricingService' that could not be found.</code></pre><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Nothing is wrong with the class. Work through the diagnosis in order.</p><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Find the scan root.</strong> It is the package of the class annotated <code>@SpringBootApplication</code> — here <code>com.example.inventory</code>. That package and everything below it is scanned.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Compare the two packages.</strong> <code>com.example.pricing</code> is a <em>sibling</em> of <code>com.example.inventory</code>, not a child. Sharing the <code>com.example</code> prefix is irrelevant; scanning is by package tree, not by prefix.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Fix it the ordinary way:</strong> move the class to <code>com.example.inventory.pricing</code>. This is the fix graders and reviewers expect, because it keeps the convention intact.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Know the escape hatches, and why they are second choice.</strong> <code>@ComponentScan(basePackages = {"com.example.inventory", "com.example.pricing"})</code> on the application class widens the radius, and an <code>@Bean</code> method in a <code>@Configuration</code> class registers the object explicitly. Both work; both mean every future reader has to discover the exception to the rule.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The same reasoning explains a related failure: putting <code>@SpringBootApplication</code> in the <em>default</em> (unnamed) package makes Spring try to scan every package on the classpath, which is slow and usually breaks.</p><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Keep the application class in the top package of your own code and put everything else beneath it. That single habit removes this entire class of error.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — reading a Spring Boot project layout file by file</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Six locations do all the work. Anything not in one of them is either your own choice or a mistake.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">inventory/
├── pom.xml                                  &lt;- dependencies + spring-boot-maven-plugin
├── mvnw, mvnw.cmd                           &lt;- the wrapper: builds without Maven installed
└── src/
    ├── main/
    │   ├── java/com/example/inventory/
    │   │   ├── InventoryApplication.java     &lt;- @SpringBootApplication + main()
    │   │   ├── controller/PartController.java
    │   │   ├── service/PartService.java
    │   │   ├── repository/PartRepository.java
    │   │   └── entity/Part.java
    │   └── resources/
    │       ├── application.properties         &lt;- config; NOT scanned, but always read
    │       ├── templates/parts/list.html      &lt;- Thymeleaf looks here, and only here
    │       ├── static/css/site.css            &lt;- served at /css/site.css
    │       └── data.sql                       &lt;- seed rows, run at startup
    └── test/java/com/example/inventory/
        └── PartValidationTest.java</code></pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Location</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Who reads it</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">What breaks if you move it</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>src/main/resources/templates/</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Thymeleaf view resolver</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Returning <code>"parts/list"</code> throws a 500 — template not found</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>src/main/resources/static/</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Spring Boot's resource handler</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Your CSS returns 404</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>src/main/resources/application.properties</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">The Environment, at startup</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Every property falls back to its default</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>src/test/java/</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Surefire, during <code>mvn test</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Tests are never compiled or run</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>pom.xml</code> → <code>spring-boot-maven-plugin</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">The <code>package</code> goal</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">The jar builds but has no manifest main class — "no main manifest attribute"</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Note that <code>resources/</code> is not scanned for components — it is the classpath root at runtime. <code>templates/parts/list.html</code> is found because the view resolver prepends <code>classpath:/templates/</code> and appends <code>.html</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Note that <code>mvnw</code> exists so a grader with no Maven installation can still run <code>./mvnw spring-boot:run</code>. Ship it; do not add it to <code>.gitignore</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">Note what should <em>not</em> be in a submission: <code>target/</code>, <code>.idea/</code>, <code>.vscode/</code>, and any <code>*.iml</code> file.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Conventions are the price of auto-configuration. Follow the layout and no configuration is needed; deviate and every deviation must be declared somewhere.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The high-frequency question is "why is my bean not found?" and the answer is always <strong>the class is outside the @SpringBootApplication class's package tree</strong>. Second most common: match the file to its directory — templates go in <code>src/main/resources/templates</code>, static assets in <code>src/main/resources/static</code>, configuration in <code>src/main/resources/application.properties</code>, tests in <code>src/test/java</code>. Third: <code>spring-boot-maven-plugin</code> is what makes the jar executable. Learn those three and this section is finished.</p></div><i data-d287cov_21="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>spring-boot-devtools</strong> is a development-only dependency whose main feature is <strong>automatic restart on classpath changes</strong> — the "hot reload" that lets you save a Java file and see the change without restarting by hand. It also disables template caching so Thymeleaf edits appear immediately, and enables a LiveReload server. It deactivates itself when the app runs from a packaged jar, so it never ships to production.</div>"},"D287/ch2/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch2-s2"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — @SpringBootApplication, Beans &amp; Stereotypes</h3><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">@SpringBootApplication = THREE annotations in one</div><pre>@SpringBootApplication
public class App {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        SpringApplication.run(App.class, args);
    }
}</pre>It bundles: <b>@Configuration</b> (this class can define beans) + <b>@EnableAutoConfiguration</b> (set things up from the classpath) + <b>@ComponentScan</b> (find annotated classes in this package and below).</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">STEREOTYPE ANNOTATIONS — same engine, different label</div><b>@Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller</b> all do the same core thing: mark the class so component scanning <b>registers it as a bean</b>. The names declare the LAYER for readability (and small extras: @Repository translates DB exceptions, @Controller enables request mappings).<br>&bull; @Component — generic bean&nbsp;&nbsp;&bull; @Service — business logic&nbsp;&nbsp;&bull; @Repository — data access&nbsp;&nbsp;&bull; @Controller — web layer.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — the bean lifecycle with @PostConstruct / @PreDestroy</div><pre>@Service
public class CacheService {
    @PostConstruct
    void warmUp() { System.out.println("cache loaded"); }   // runs AFTER the bean
                                                            // is built &amp; injected
    @PreDestroy
    void flush()  { System.out.println("cache saved"); }    // runs at shutdown,
                                                            // before the bean dies
}</pre>Startup prints &ldquo;cache loaded&rdquo; once (bean created → dependencies injected → @PostConstruct). Ctrl-C prints &ldquo;cache saved&rdquo; (container closing → @PreDestroy). Use them for init and cleanup, not constructors — at constructor time the dependencies aren&rsquo;t injected yet.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>A method that must run once the bean is fully created and its dependencies injected is annotated:<ol type="A"><li>@PostConstruct</li><li>@PreDestroy</li><li>@Autowired</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — @PostConstruct.</b> Init hook after construction + injection; @PreDestroy is the shutdown hook.</div></details></div></div><div class="vl" data-defglance="1"><div class="vl-box vl-rule def-glance"><div class="t">📖 DEFINITIONS AT A GLANCE — every key term in this section</div></div><table><tr><th style="width:32%">Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr><tr><td><b>Singleton</b></td><td>(the default): the container creates ONE shared instance of the bean per Spring container — every injection points at the same object.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Prototype</b></td><td>a NEW instance is created for each request/injection.</td></tr><tr><td><b>@Lazy</b></td><td>delays a bean’s creation until it is first used instead of at container startup.</td></tr><tr><td><b>Programmatic lookup</b></td><td>applicationContext.getBean(MyBean.class) fetches a bean from the container in code — rarely needed when injection is available.</td></tr><tr><td><b>@Profile</b></td><td>a bean annotated with a profile exists only when that profile is active (dev vs prod configurations).</td></tr></table><div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-3);margin:-6px 0 14px">Full lesson with examples below ⬇️ — nothing removed.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Two annotations — @SpringBootApplication and @Component (with its specializations) — are most of what you need to wire a Spring Boot app together. Understanding what each does and when to use which is the foundation of every Spring class you write.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. @SpringBootApplication is three annotations.</strong> It is shorthand for @Configuration (this class declares beans), @EnableAutoConfiguration (let Spring Boot auto-configure based on classpath), and @ComponentScan (find @Components in this package and below). The single annotation gives you the full Spring Boot startup behavior.</p>
<p><strong>2. The main method.</strong> The class with @SpringBootApplication has a main method that calls SpringApplication.run(MyApp.class, args). That kicks off the entire framework: auto-configuration, component scanning, embedded server, dependency injection, the whole machine.</p>
<p><strong>3. Stereotype annotations name layers.</strong> @Component is generic. @Service marks a business-logic class. @Repository marks a data-access class (and adds JPA exception translation). @Controller / @RestController mark web-layer classes. Functionally similar; semantically distinct. Pick the one that matches the role for clarity.</p>
<p><strong>4. @Bean for explicit bean definitions.</strong> When you cannot annotate the class (third-party code) or need to construct it specially, declare a bean with @Bean inside an @Configuration class. The method's return becomes a Spring-managed bean. @Component is for classes you own; @Bean is for everything else.</p>
<p><strong>5. @Autowired and constructor injection.</strong> @Autowired marks an injection point. On a single-argument constructor, it is now implicit. The modern style: write a constructor with the dependencies as parameters, declare them final, let Spring inject them. No annotations on fields.</p>
<p><strong>6. @Qualifier and @Primary for ambiguity.</strong> If two beans of the same type exist, Spring needs to know which to inject. @Primary on one bean marks it the default. @Qualifier("name") on the injection point picks a specific one. Common when you have multiple implementations of an interface.</p>
<p><strong>7. Beans are wired at startup.</strong> Spring builds the bean graph once when the app starts. Misconfigurations (missing beans, ambiguous beans, circular dependencies) fail fast — you see the error at startup, not at request time. Read the startup error carefully; it usually names exactly what is wrong.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Forgetting @Service / @Repository / @Controller — the class is just a POJO and not managed by Spring.</li>
 <li>Declaring two beans of the same type without @Primary or @Qualifier — startup fails with NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException.</li>
 <li>Field injection on @Autowired fields — works but discouraged; prefer constructor injection.</li>
 <li>Using `new` inside Spring code to create what should be a bean — sidesteps DI and breaks testability.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>@SpringBootApplication packs three meta-annotations and starts the whole framework. Stereotype annotations (@Service, @Repository, @Controller) name the role of each class. @Bean declares beans for code you don't own. Constructor injection is the modern default. Read startup errors carefully — they tell you exactly what is misconfigured.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@SpringBootApplication</span></dt>
 <dd>Composite annotation: @Configuration + @EnableAutoConfiguration + @ComponentScan.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Service</span></dt>
 <dd>Stereotype annotation marking a business-logic class.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Repository</span></dt>
 <dd>Stereotype annotation marking a data-access class; also enables JPA exception translation.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Bean</span></dt>
 <dd>Method-level annotation declaring its return value as a Spring-managed bean.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Qualifier</span></dt>
 <dd>Annotation specifying which bean to inject when multiple match by type.</dd>
</dl>
<h3>📘 Deep dive: Spring bean scopes & lifecycle</h3><p><strong>Singleton</strong> (the default): the container creates ONE shared instance of the bean per Spring container — every injection points at the same object. <strong>Prototype:</strong> a NEW instance is created for each request/injection. <strong>@Lazy</strong> delays a bean’s creation until it is first used instead of at container startup. Default bean naming is the class name camelCased (CustomerService → customerService). Choose prototype for stateful, per-use objects; singletons must be stateless or thread-safe.</p><p><strong>Programmatic lookup:</strong> applicationContext.getBean(MyBean.class) fetches a bean from the container in code — rarely needed when injection is available. <strong>@Profile:</strong> a bean annotated with a profile exists only when that profile is active (dev vs prod configurations).</p><i data-tb287c2s2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — @SpringBootApplication unpacked into its three annotations, and the startup order they produce</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 352" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk187a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="238" y="24" width="204" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="340" y="48" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@SpringBootApplication</text><line x1="340" y1="64" x2="124" y2="100" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk187a)"/><rect x="30" y="100" width="188" height="56" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="124" y="132" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@Configuration</text><text x="124" y="176" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">this class may</text><text x="124" y="192" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">declare @Bean methods</text><line x1="340" y1="64" x2="340" y2="100" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk187a)"/><rect x="246" y="100" width="188" height="56" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="340" y="132" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@EnableAutoConfiguration</text><text x="340" y="176" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">configure things the</text><text x="340" y="192" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">classpath implies</text><line x1="340" y1="64" x2="556" y2="100" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk187a)"/><rect x="462" y="100" width="188" height="56" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="556" y="132" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@ComponentScan</text><text x="556" y="176" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">find @Component below</text><text x="556" y="192" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">this package</text><text x="340" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">SpringApplication.run(InventoryApplication.class, args) — what happens, in order</text><line x1="46" y1="268" x2="634" y2="268" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk187a)"/><line x1="80" y1="262" x2="80" y2="274" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="80" cy="248" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="80" y="252" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text x="80" y="292" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">scan</text><text x="80" y="308" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">classes → bean definitions</text><line x1="230" y1="262" x2="230" y2="274" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="230" cy="248" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="230" y="252" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text x="230" y="292" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">auto-config</text><text x="230" y="308" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">DataSource, MVC, Thymeleaf</text><line x1="390" y1="262" x2="390" y2="274" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="390" cy="248" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="390" y="252" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="390" y="292" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">instantiate</text><text x="390" y="308" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">construct + inject, leaf first</text><line x1="545" y1="262" x2="545" y2="274" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="545" cy="248" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="545" y="252" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="545" y="292" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">listen</text><text x="545" y="308" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Tomcat opens :8080</text><text x="340" y="338" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Everything above happens once, before the first request. That is why misconfiguration is a startup failure.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — choosing between @Component and @Bean</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two classes need to become beans. One is yours; one comes from a third-party jar. The annotation is not a style choice — only one of them is even possible in each case.</p><h4 style="margin:20px 0 6px; font-size:15.5px; font-weight:700;">Case 1 — a class you wrote</h4><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">package com.example.inventory.service;

@Service                                  // a @Component with a role name
public class PartService {
    private final PartRepository repo;
    public PartService(PartRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }
}</code></pre><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Component scanning finds the annotation, so nothing else is needed. <code>@Service</code>, <code>@Repository</code> and <code>@Controller</code> are all <code>@Component</code> with extra meaning — and, in two cases, extra behaviour: <code>@Repository</code> adds translation of vendor SQL exceptions into Spring's <code>DataAccessException</code> hierarchy, and <code>@Controller</code> makes the class visible to MVC handler mapping.</p><h4 style="margin:20px 0 6px; font-size:15.5px; font-weight:700;">Case 2 — a class from a library</h4><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">package com.example.inventory.config;

@Configuration
public class AppConfig {

    @Bean
    public RestClient inventoryApiClient() {          // you cannot annotate RestClient
        return RestClient.builder()
                         .baseUrl("https://supplier.example.com/api")
                         .build();
    }
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">You cannot add <code>@Component</code> to <code>RestClient</code> — the source is not yours, and even if it were, one library class cannot know which of your applications wants it.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><code>@Bean</code> puts the decision in a method you control: the method body constructs the object however it likes, and the return value becomes the bean.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;">The bean's default name is the <strong>method name</strong> — here <code>inventoryApiClient</code>. That is what <code>@Qualifier("inventoryApiClient")</code> would refer to. For <code>@Component</code> classes the default name is the class name with a lowercase first letter (<code>partService</code>).</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><code>@Bean</code> methods must live in a class Spring already manages — normally one annotated <code>@Configuration</code>. A <code>@Bean</code> method in an unmanaged class is just a method.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: <code>@Component</code> (and its stereotypes) for classes you own; <code>@Bean</code> inside <code>@Configuration</code> for everything else. The question "who owns the source file?" decides it every time.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — resolving NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two implementations of one interface, and the application will not start:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">public interface PriceCalculator { BigDecimal price(Part part); }

@Service class StandardPricing  implements PriceCalculator { /* ... */ }
@Service class WholesalePricing implements PriceCalculator { /* ... */ }

@Service
public class QuoteService {
    private final PriceCalculator calculator;
    public QuoteService(PriceCalculator calculator) { this.calculator = calculator; }
}</code></pre><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException: expected single matching bean but found 2:
standardPricing,wholesalePricing</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Read what Spring actually did.</strong> It matched by <em>type</em>, found two candidates, and refused to guess. The exception even lists the two bean names it considered.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Fix A — name a default with <code>@Primary</code>.</strong> Put <code>@Primary</code> on <code>StandardPricing</code>. Every injection point that does not say otherwise now gets it. Use this when one implementation is the normal case.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Fix B — choose at the injection point with <code>@Qualifier</code>.</strong> Write <code>public QuoteService(@Qualifier("wholesalePricing") PriceCalculator calculator)</code>. Use this when different consumers genuinely want different implementations.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Fix C — match by name.</strong> Renaming the parameter to <code>standardPricing</code> also resolves it, because Spring falls back to the bean name when types tie. It works, but it makes a parameter name load-bearing, so prefer A or B.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Check the mental model.</strong> Injection resolves <em>by type first, by name second</em>. Every question in this area is a consequence of that sentence.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: <code>@Primary</code> sets the default on the bean; <code>@Qualifier</code> overrides it at the injection point. They combine: mark one <code>@Primary</code> and let the one unusual consumer ask for the other by name.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Expect one decomposition question — <code>@SpringBootApplication</code> = <strong>@Configuration + @EnableAutoConfiguration + @ComponentScan</strong>; memorise those three words in that order. Expect one stereotype question: they are all <code>@Component</code> underneath, and the only two with extra behaviour are <code>@Repository</code> (exception translation) and <code>@Controller</code> (handler mapping). And expect the ambiguity question: two beans of one type ⇒ <code>NoUniqueBeanDefinitionException</code> ⇒ fixed by <code>@Primary</code> or <code>@Qualifier</code>.</p></div><i data-d287cov_22="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">Profiles, and devtools in one line</h4><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>@Profile("prod")</strong> on a bean means that bean <strong>exists only when the "prod" profile is active</strong>. It is how one build serves several environments: an H2 datasource under <code>@Profile("dev")</code>, the real one under <code>@Profile("prod")</code>, and nothing else in the app changes.</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">@Bean
@Profile("dev")
DataSource devDataSource() { … }   // only when dev is active

# activate with any of:
#   spring.profiles.active=dev        in application.properties
#   --spring.profiles.active=dev      on the command line
#   SPRING_PROFILES_ACTIVE=dev        as an environment variable</pre><p>Profile-specific property files are picked up automatically by name: <code>application-dev.properties</code> is loaded on top of <code>application.properties</code> when <code>dev</code> is active.</p><p>And to restate the neighbouring fact plainly, since it is asked as a separate question: <strong>Spring Boot DevTools primarily provides automatic application restart on code changes</strong>, for faster development.</p>"},"D287/ch2/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch2-s3"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Configuration Properties</h3><p>Settings live OUTSIDE the code, in <code>src/main/resources/application.properties</code> — so you can change the port or database without recompiling.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a real properties file, line by line</div><pre>server.port=8080                       ← which port Tomcat listens on
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:db   ← where the database is
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update   ← let Hibernate create/update tables
app.welcome=Hello Natasha!             ← your own custom property</pre>Read your custom property in code:<br><pre>@Value("${app.welcome}")
private String welcome;    // "Hello Natasha!" injected at startup</pre></div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHAT TO KNOW</div>&bull; Boot reads application.properties (or .yml) automatically.<br>&bull; <b>@Value("${key}")</b> injects a single property.<br>&bull; <b>Profiles</b>: application-dev.properties vs application-prod.properties, activated with <code>spring.profiles.active=dev</code> — different settings per environment without code changes.<br>&bull; ddl-auto values worth knowing: <code>update</code> (evolve schema), <code>create-drop</code> (fresh each run — good for demos/tests).</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ CONFIG BEATS CODE EDITS</div>If an exam question asks &ldquo;how do you change the port / DB URL,&rdquo; the answer is a properties change (<code>server.port=9090</code>), never &ldquo;edit and recompile Java.&rdquo;</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Which file sets server.port for a Spring Boot app?<ol type="A"><li>application.properties</li><li>pom.xml</li><li>index.html</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — application.properties</b> (in src/main/resources). pom.xml manages dependencies, not runtime settings.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Hard-coded values in Java code mean every environment change is a recompile. Spring Boot's configuration system externalizes these values to properties files, environment variables, and profiles. Done right, the same JAR runs in dev, test, and production with no code changes.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. application.properties basics.</strong> Lives in src/main/resources. Key=value format. server.port=8080. spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb. spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=update. Spring Boot reads it at startup and applies the values throughout.</p>
<p><strong>2. YAML alternative.</strong> application.yml is hierarchical: indentation maps nested keys. Same values, often more readable when keys are deeply nested. Spring Boot accepts either; some teams prefer YAML for complex configs and properties for simple ones.</p>
<p><strong>3. @Value injection.</strong> Inject a single property into a field or method parameter: @Value("${app.greeting}") private String greeting. Works for primitives, strings, and SpEL expressions. Quick for one-off values; awkward when you have many related properties.</p>
<p><strong>4. @ConfigurationProperties for grouped values.</strong> For a group of related properties, define a class with @ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "app") and bean fields matching the property names. Spring binds the entire group at startup. Type-safe, validated, and IDE-discoverable. The right answer for non-trivial config.</p>
<p><strong>5. Profiles for environment differences.</strong> application-dev.properties for development. application-prod.properties for production. Activate with spring.profiles.active=prod (in main file or via env variable). Different profiles override base values for environment-specific settings.</p>
<p><strong>6. Environment variables and command-line override.</strong> Spring Boot reads from many sources in priority order: command-line args > env variables > application-{profile}.properties > application.properties. SERVER_PORT=9090 java -jar app.jar overrides server.port. Production deployments typically supply secrets and environment-specific values via env variables.</p>
<p><strong>7. Don't commit secrets.</strong> Database passwords, API keys, JWT secrets — never commit these to source control. Use environment variables, a secrets manager (Vault, AWS Secrets Manager), or Spring Cloud Config. application.properties gets you started; production needs more.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Hard-coding configuration values in Java instead of in application.properties.</li>
 <li>Mixing @Value and @ConfigurationProperties for the same property group — confusing and error-prone.</li>
 <li>Forgetting to activate a profile in production — getting development defaults in prod.</li>
 <li>Committing application-prod.properties with real credentials to git.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Externalize configuration to application.properties or .yml. Use @Value for one-off properties and @ConfigurationProperties for grouped, type-safe binding. Profiles handle environment-specific overrides. Environment variables and command-line args override file-based config. Keep secrets out of source control.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">application.properties</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Boot's main configuration file, key=value format.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">YAML config (.yml)</span></dt>
 <dd>Hierarchical alternative format for application configuration.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Value</span></dt>
 <dd>Injects a single property value into a field or parameter.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ConfigurationProperties</span></dt>
 <dd>Type-safe binding of a property prefix to a class's fields.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Spring profile</span></dt>
 <dd>A named configuration variant (dev, test, prod) activated at startup.</dd>
</dl>
<p><strong>Targeted logging:</strong> the property logging.level.com.app=DEBUG sets the logging level for the com.app package (and its children) to DEBUG — more detail exactly where you need it, without drowning the whole application in noise.</p><i data-tb287c2s3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the property source ladder: why an environment variable beats a file, and a flag beats both</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 316" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk188a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="26" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">resolving server.port — the first source that has a value wins</text><rect x="64" y="52" width="300" height="36" rx="8" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/><text x="80" y="74" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">command line</text><text x="374" y="74" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">--server.port=9090</text><text x="30" y="74" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">HIGHEST</text><line x1="214" y1="88" x2="214" y2="98" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk188a)"/><rect x="64" y="98" width="300" height="36" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="80" y="120" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">OS environment variable</text><text x="374" y="120" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">SERVER_PORT=8081</text><line x1="214" y1="134" x2="214" y2="144" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk188a)"/><rect x="64" y="144" width="300" height="36" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="80" y="166" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">application-prod.properties</text><text x="374" y="166" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">server.port=8000</text><line x1="214" y1="180" x2="214" y2="190" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk188a)"/><rect x="64" y="190" width="300" height="36" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="80" y="212" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">application.properties</text><text x="374" y="212" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">server.port=8080</text><line x1="214" y1="226" x2="214" y2="236" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk188a)"/><rect x="64" y="236" width="300" height="36" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.3" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="80" y="258" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">Spring Boot default</text><text x="374" y="258" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">(none set)</text><text x="30" y="258" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">LOWEST</text><line x1="560" y1="74" x2="610" y2="74" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk188a)"/><text x="628" y="62" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">app</text><text x="628" y="78" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">binds</text><text x="628" y="94" fill="#10b981" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">9090</text><text x="340" y="300" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The same jar therefore runs in three environments unchanged — you override from outside, never recompile.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — converting scattered @Value fields into one typed configuration class</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A service has grown five injected properties. It works, and it is the wrong shape:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Service
public class ReorderService {
    @Value("${inventory.reorder.threshold}")      private int threshold;
    @Value("${inventory.reorder.batch-size}")     private int batchSize;
    @Value("${inventory.reorder.supplier-email}") private String supplierEmail;
    @Value("${inventory.reorder.enabled}")        private boolean enabled;
    @Value("${inventory.reorder.currency:USD}")   private String currency;   // :USD is the default
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Notice the smell.</strong> Five string literals repeat one prefix. A typo in any of them fails at startup with <code>Could not resolve placeholder</code>; a typo in the <em>properties file</em> fails silently unless a default was supplied.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Create a class for the group</strong> with fields named after the property suffixes. Kebab-case in the file (<code>batch-size</code>) binds to camelCase in Java (<code>batchSize</code>) — this relaxed binding is exactly why the two look different.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Register it</strong> with <code>@EnableConfigurationProperties(ReorderProperties.class)</code> on a configuration class, or by annotating the class <code>@Component</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Inject the whole group</strong> as one constructor parameter. The service now depends on one object instead of five loose values, and a test can build that object with <code>new</code>.</li></ol><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "inventory.reorder")
@Validated
public class ReorderProperties {
    @Min(1) private int threshold;
    private int batchSize;
    @Email private String supplierEmail;
    private boolean enabled;
    private String currency = "USD";        // default lives in Java now
    // getters and setters — binding uses the setters
}

@Service
public class ReorderService {
    private final ReorderProperties props;
    public ReorderService(ReorderProperties props) { this.props = props; }
}</code></pre><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;"># application.properties
inventory.reorder.threshold=25
inventory.reorder.batch-size=100
inventory.reorder.supplier-email=orders@supplier.example.com
inventory.reorder.enabled=true</code></pre><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: <code>@Value</code> for one or two isolated values; <code>@ConfigurationProperties</code> as soon as the values share a prefix. The typed version also gets you validation (<code>@Validated</code> plus constraint annotations) and IDE completion for the property names.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — tracing which value the application actually uses</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The same property is set in four places. The deployment runs:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL=jdbc:h2:mem:staging \
  java -jar inventory.jar --spring.profiles.active=prod --server.port=9090</code></pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Where it is set</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Property</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Value</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>application.properties</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>server.port</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">8080</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>application.properties</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>spring.datasource.url</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>jdbc:h2:mem:testdb</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>application-prod.properties</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>server.port</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">8000</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">OS environment</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>jdbc:h2:mem:staging</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Command line</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>--server.port</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">9090</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>server.port</code>:</strong> three candidates. Command line outranks the profile file, which outranks the base file. The port is <strong>9090</strong>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>spring.datasource.url</code>:</strong> two candidates. <code>SPRING_DATASOURCE_URL</code> is an environment variable, which outranks any properties file. Relaxed binding maps the shouty underscored name onto the dotted one — upper-case, underscores for dots and hyphens. The URL is <strong><code>jdbc:h2:mem:staging</code></strong>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The profile:</strong> <code>--spring.profiles.active=prod</code> makes <code>application-prod.properties</code> load <em>in addition to</em> the base file, not instead of it. Anything the profile file does not mention still comes from <code>application.properties</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The general rule:</strong> the further from the source tree, the higher the priority. Command line > OS environment > profile-specific file > base file > framework default.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Port 9090, datasource <code>jdbc:h2:mem:staging</code>, profile <code>prod</code> layered on top of the base properties. No recompilation was involved in any of it — which is the entire point of externalised configuration.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two reliable question shapes. <strong>Precedence:</strong> given the same key in a file and on the command line, the command line wins — remember the ladder <em>command line → environment → profile file → base file → default</em>. <strong>@Value vs @ConfigurationProperties:</strong> one value, use <code>@Value("${key}")</code>; a group sharing a prefix, use <code>@ConfigurationProperties(prefix = "...")</code> with a class, which is also the only one of the two that gives you type safety and validation. Remember the default syntax <code>@Value("${key:fallback}")</code> and that profile files are named <code>application-{profile}.properties</code> and are <em>additive</em>.</p></div>"},"D287/ch3/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch3-s1"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — @Controller &amp; Request Mappings</h3><p>A <b>controller</b> is the class that receives web requests. Annotate the class <code>@Controller</code>, then map URLs to methods:</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a controller, traced end to end</div><pre>@Controller
public class PartController {

    @GetMapping("/parts")            // GET /parts → this method
    public String list(Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("parts", service.findAll());
        return "partList";           // render templates/partList.html
    }

    @PostMapping("/parts")           // POST /parts → form submission
    public String add(Part part) {
        service.save(part);
        return "redirect:/parts";    // browser redirects to GET /parts
    }
}</pre>Browser hits GET /parts → Spring routes to list() → data goes into the <b>Model</b> → the returned String names the <b>template</b> to render. The POST handler saves, then <code>redirect:</code> sends the browser back to the list (the post-redirect-get pattern that stops double submits).</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE MAPPING TOOLKIT</div>&bull; <b>@GetMapping / @PostMapping</b> (+ Put/Delete) — HTTP verb + URL → method.<br>&bull; <b>@PathVariable</b> — pull a value from the URL: <code>@GetMapping("/parts/{id}")</code> + <code>@PathVariable Long id</code> → /parts/7 gives id = 7.<br>&bull; <b>@RequestParam</b> — pull a query value: /parts?q=bolt → <code>@RequestParam String q</code>.<br>&bull; <b>Wildcard</b>: <code>@GetMapping("/files/*")</code> matches ONE path segment — /files/a.txt yes, /files/sub/a.txt no (that needs /**).<br>&bull; <b>@RestController</b> = @Controller + @ResponseBody: methods return data (JSON) instead of a template name.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>In @GetMapping("/parts/{id}"), the {id} value is bound to a parameter with:<ol type="A"><li>@PathVariable</li><li>@RequestParam</li><li>@Value</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — @PathVariable.</b> Path pieces use @PathVariable; ?key=value query strings use @RequestParam.</div></details></div></div><div class="vl" data-defglance="1"><div class="vl-box vl-rule def-glance"><div class="t">📖 DEFINITIONS AT A GLANCE — every key term in this section</div></div><table><tr><th style="width:32%">Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr><tr><td><b>Request in, response out</b></td><td>@RequestBody reads the HTTP request body — typically JSON — and deserializes it into your object.</td></tr><tr><td><b>@RequestBody</b></td><td>reads the HTTP request body — typically JSON — and deserializes it into your object.</td></tr><tr><td><b>ResponseEntity&lt;T&gt;</b></td><td>gives full control of the HTTP response: status code, headers, and body together.</td></tr></table><div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-3);margin:-6px 0 14px">Full lesson with examples below ⬇️ — nothing removed.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Controllers are the front door of your application. They map URLs to methods, handle HTTP requests, and decide what to render or return. Getting controllers right is the difference between a clean RESTful service and a tangled mess of URL handlers.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. @Controller vs @RestController.</strong> @Controller returns view names — Spring resolves them to templates (Thymeleaf, JSP). @RestController returns objects — Spring serializes them to JSON or XML. @RestController = @Controller + @ResponseBody on every method. Pick @Controller for server-rendered pages, @RestController for APIs.</p>
<p><strong>2. @RequestMapping and HTTP-specific shortcuts.</strong> @RequestMapping("/parts") on the class sets the base path. @GetMapping("/{id}") on a method maps GET /parts/{id}. Similar shortcuts: @PostMapping, @PutMapping, @DeleteMapping, @PatchMapping. Cleaner than the generic @RequestMapping(method = ...) for each method.</p>
<p><strong>3. @PathVariable for URL segments.</strong> @GetMapping("/parts/{id}") public String show(@PathVariable Long id). The {id} placeholder in the URL maps to the id parameter. Type conversion is automatic — Spring parses 'parts/42' into a Long. Validation can be added with regex constraints in the path.</p>
<p><strong>4. @RequestParam for query strings.</strong> /parts?type=in-house maps to @RequestParam String type. Optional with required = false and a default with defaultValue = "all". For form-encoded POST data, @RequestParam works the same way.</p>
<p><strong>5. @ModelAttribute for form binding.</strong> When a controller receives a posted form, @ModelAttribute Part part binds the form fields to a Part object's properties. Spring matches form field names to setter methods. Powerful and conventional; covered more in section 3.</p>
<p><strong>6. Returning views vs returning data.</strong> From @Controller, return a String view name (e.g., "parts/list") and Spring resolves it to templates/parts/list.html. Add data with the Model parameter: model.addAttribute("parts", partList). The view template renders using the attributes. From @RestController, just return the object.</p>
<p><strong>7. Mapping HTTP methods to actions.</strong> GET retrieves; POST creates; PUT updates entirely; PATCH updates partially; DELETE removes. Following these conventions makes your URLs predictable and tooling-friendly. Even if the PA does not require strict REST, internalizing the mapping makes your code clearer.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Mixing @Controller and @RestController in confusing ways — same class returning sometimes views, sometimes JSON.</li>
 <li>Using @RequestParam where @PathVariable belongs (or vice versa) — works sometimes but breaks REST conventions.</li>
 <li>Forgetting @Valid on @ModelAttribute parameters — validation annotations on the entity get ignored.</li>
 <li>Not adding model attributes — the view renders empty fields and you wonder why.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>@Controller for server-rendered views; @RestController for JSON APIs. @RequestMapping sets paths; @GetMapping/@PostMapping/etc. handle specific HTTP methods. @PathVariable binds URL segments; @RequestParam binds query strings; @ModelAttribute binds forms. Return view names from @Controller, populate Model with data, and let the template render.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Controller</span></dt>
 <dd>Marks a class as a Spring MVC controller returning view names.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@RestController</span></dt>
 <dd>Combines @Controller and @ResponseBody — every method returns serialized data.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@PathVariable</span></dt>
 <dd>Annotation binding a URL path segment to a method parameter.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@RequestParam</span></dt>
 <dd>Annotation binding a query string parameter or form field to a method parameter.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Model</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring MVC's container for attributes passed from controller to view.</dd>
</dl>
<p><strong>Request in, response out:</strong> <strong>@RequestBody</strong> reads the HTTP request body — typically JSON — and deserializes it into your object. <strong>ResponseEntity&lt;T&gt;</strong> gives full control of the HTTP response: status code, headers, and body together.</p><i data-tb287c3s1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — one request through the dispatcher: match the route, bind the parameters, then fork on the return type</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 444" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk189a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="28" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="13" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">GET /parts/42?format=short</text><line x1="340" y1="36" x2="340" y2="62" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk189a)"/><rect x="232" y="62" width="216" height="38" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="340" y="85" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">DispatcherServlet</text><text x="462" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the one servlet Boot registers</text><line x1="340" y1="100" x2="340" y2="126" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk189a)"/><rect x="120" y="126" width="440" height="92" rx="10" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.10" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="340" y="146" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">HandlerMapping — the routing table built at startup</text><text x="140" y="170" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">GET  /parts</text><text x="290" y="170" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">list()</text><text x="440" y="170" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace"></text><text x="140" y="187" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">GET  /parts/{id}</text><text x="290" y="187" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">show(Long id)</text><text x="440" y="187" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">← matches</text><text x="140" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">POST /parts</text><text x="290" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">save(Part part)</text><text x="440" y="204" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace"></text><line x1="340" y1="218" x2="340" y2="244" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk189a)"/><rect x="150" y="244" width="380" height="62" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="340" y="279" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"></text><text x="340" y="266" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@GetMapping("/parts/{id}")</text><text x="340" y="286" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">show(@PathVariable Long id, @RequestParam String format)</text><text x="142" y="268" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">42</text><text x="142" y="252" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">from the path</text><text x="538" y="268" fill="#e8795a" font-size="12" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"short"</text><text x="538" y="252" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">from the query string</text><line x1="340" y1="306" x2="340" y2="318" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk189a)"/><rect x="46" y="336" width="272" height="68" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="182" y="374" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"></text><text x="182" y="358" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Controller</text><text x="182" y="376" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">returns "parts/detail" → ViewResolver</text><text x="182" y="392" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">→ templates/parts/detail.html</text><rect x="362" y="336" width="272" height="68" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="498" y="374" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"></text><text x="498" y="358" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@RestController</text><text x="498" y="376" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">returns a Part → HttpMessageConverter</text><text x="498" y="392" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">→ JSON in the response body</text><line x1="336" y1="318" x2="190" y2="334" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk189a)"/><line x1="344" y1="318" x2="490" y2="334" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk189a)"/><text x="340" y="428" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="11" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Same routing, same parameter binding. The only difference is what happens to the return value.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — writing the five handlers of a CRUD controller</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The whole of server-rendered CRUD is five routes. Written out once, the pattern is memorised.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Controller
@RequestMapping("/parts")                 // every path below is relative to this
public class PartController {

    private final PartService service;
    public PartController(PartService service) { this.service = service; }

    @GetMapping                            // GET /parts
    public String list(Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("parts", service.findAll());
        return "parts/list";               // -&gt; templates/parts/list.html
    }

    @GetMapping("/new")                    // GET /parts/new
    public String showCreateForm(Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("part", new Inhouse());
        return "parts/form";
    }

    @GetMapping("/{id}")                   // GET /parts/42
    public String show(@PathVariable Long id, Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("part", service.findById(id));
        return "parts/detail";
    }

    @PostMapping                           // POST /parts
    public String save(@ModelAttribute Part part) {
        service.save(part);
        return "redirect:/parts";          // Post-Redirect-Get
    }

    @GetMapping("/search")                 // GET /parts/search?q=brake
    public String search(@RequestParam(defaultValue = "") String q, Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("parts", service.searchByName(q));
        return "parts/list";
    }
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Class-level <code>@RequestMapping</code> sets the prefix.</strong> A method-level <code>@GetMapping</code> with no value inherits that path exactly — which is how <code>list()</code> ends up mapped to <code>/parts</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Path segment vs query string.</strong> <code>/{id}</code> identifies <em>which</em> resource, so it is a <code>@PathVariable</code>. <code>?q=brake</code> modifies <em>how</em> the collection is filtered, so it is a <code>@RequestParam</code>. Swapping them still compiles, which is why it is such a common distractor.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Matching is by specificity, not declaration order.</strong> The URL <code>/parts/new</code> matches both <code>/parts/new</code> and <code>/parts/{id}</code>; Spring prefers the exact literal, so <code>showCreateForm</code> wins and you never need to reorder the methods.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The returned String is a view name, never a URL</strong> — unless it starts with <code>redirect:</code> or <code>forward:</code>. <code>"parts/list"</code> becomes <code>templates/parts/list.html</code>; <code>"redirect:/parts"</code> becomes a 302 to the URL <code>/parts</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>defaultValue</code> implies optional.</strong> With it, a request carrying no <code>q</code> still matches. Without it, a missing required parameter is a 400 Bad Request before your method ever runs.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Five routes — list, form, detail, save, search. Everything else in a CRUD controller is a variation on those.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — predicting what each controller sends back</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Three classes, one method body each. Say precisely what the client receives.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Controller
public class A {
    @GetMapping("/parts/{id}")
    public String show(@PathVariable Long id) { return "parts/detail"; }
}

@RestController
public class B {
    @GetMapping("/api/parts/{id}")
    public Part show(@PathVariable Long id) { return service.findById(id); }
}

@Controller
public class C {
    @GetMapping("/api/raw/{id}")
    @ResponseBody
    public Part show(@PathVariable Long id) { return service.findById(id); }
}</code></pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Class</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">The return value is treated as</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Response body</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Content-Type</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">A — <code>@Controller</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a view name</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the rendered <code>templates/parts/detail.html</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>text/html</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">B — <code>@RestController</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the response body itself</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the Part serialised by Jackson</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>application/json</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">C — <code>@Controller</code> + <code>@ResponseBody</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the response body itself</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">identical to B</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>application/json</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>A returns a String, so the view resolver claims it.</strong> If <code>templates/parts/detail.html</code> does not exist the request fails with 500 and "Error resolving template".</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>B and C are the same thing.</strong> <code>@RestController</code> is defined as <code>@Controller</code> plus <code>@ResponseBody</code> on every method; C spells out the long form.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The trap lives in A.</strong> If a <code>@Controller</code> method returns a <code>Part</code> instead of a String, Spring does not serialise it — it treats the object as a model attribute and tries to derive a view name from the request path, producing a confusing 404 or 500.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Jackson does the serialising in B and C.</strong> It arrives with <code>spring-boot-starter-web</code> and you never call it. A field missing from the JSON means a missing getter, not a Spring problem.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: <code>@Controller</code> → the String names a template. <code>@RestController</code> (or <code>@ResponseBody</code>) → the return value <em>is</em> the response body.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Nearly every question here reduces to one of three pairs. <strong>@Controller vs @RestController:</strong> views vs serialised data, and <code>@RestController = @Controller + @ResponseBody</code>. <strong>@PathVariable vs @RequestParam:</strong> <code>/parts/42</code> is a path variable, <code>?type=inhouse</code> is a request parameter. <strong>Verb to annotation:</strong> GET reads, POST creates, PUT replaces, PATCH partially updates, DELETE removes — with <code>@GetMapping</code>, <code>@PostMapping</code> and friends as shorthand for <code>@RequestMapping(method = ...)</code>. Also remember that <code>"redirect:/parts"</code> causes a fresh browser request while <code>"parts/list"</code> renders a template inside the current one.</p></div><i data-d287cov_31="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">Why constructor injection is preferred</h4><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Constructor injection</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Field injection</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Looks like</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>Ctl(Service s) { this.s = s; }</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>@Autowired private Service s;</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Fields can be <code>final</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>yes</strong> — immutable after construction</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">no</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Dependencies are</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>explicit</strong> — visible in the signature</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">hidden inside the class</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">In a plain unit test</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>just call the constructor</strong> with mocks</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">needs reflection or a Spring context</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Missing dependency shows up</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">at start-up</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">as a NullPointerException later</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>So the reason is the combination: constructor injection <strong>allows dependencies to be final and makes them explicit and testable</strong>. A class with five constructor parameters is also visibly doing too much, which field injection quietly hides.</p>"},"D287/ch3/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch3-s2"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Thymeleaf Templates</h3><p><b>Thymeleaf</b> is the HTML template engine: you write normal HTML plus <code>th:</code> attributes that Spring fills with data from the Model.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — render a list of parts</div>Controller put <code>parts</code> in the Model. Template partList.html:<br><pre>&lt;table&gt;
  &lt;tr th:each="p : ${parts}"&gt;      ← loop: one row per part
    &lt;td th:text="${p.name}"&gt;name&lt;/td&gt;   ← replace text with p.name
    &lt;td th:text="${p.price}"&gt;0.00&lt;/td&gt;
  &lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;</pre>With parts = [Bolt 0.25, Nut 0.10] the rendered page has two rows: Bolt/0.25 and Nut/0.10. The static text (&ldquo;name&rdquo;, &ldquo;0.00&rdquo;) is only a placeholder you see when opening the raw file.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE th: TOOLKIT</div>&bull; <b>th:text="${x}"</b> — put a value into the element&rsquo;s text.<br>&bull; <b>th:each="item : ${list}"</b> — repeat the element per item.<br>&bull; <b>th:if / th:unless</b> — render conditionally.<br>&bull; <b>th:href="@{/parts/{id}(id=${p.id})}"</b> — build URLs safely.<br>&bull; <b>th:field="*{name}"</b> — bind a form input to a field of the form-backing object.<br>&bull; <b>Comments:</b> standard &lt;!-- --&gt; stays in the page source; Thymeleaf&rsquo;s parser-level comments <code>&lt;!--/* ... */--&gt;</code> are removed during rendering — the browser never sees them.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ ${...} vs *{...}</div><code>${x}</code> reads from the Model. <code>*{x}</code> reads a field of the object named by th:object (used in forms). Mixing them up is the top Thymeleaf error.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Which attribute repeats an element once per item of a collection?<ol type="A"><li>th:each</li><li>th:text</li><li>th:if</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — th:each</b>, e.g. th:each="p : ${parts}" renders the element for every part.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
<div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
<p>Thymeleaf is Spring Boot's default templating engine for server-rendered HTML. While modern SPAs (React, Vue) handle UI on the client, server-rendered HTML is still everywhere — admin dashboards, email templates, content sites, internal tools. Thymeleaf is also what your WGU D287 Performance Assessment uses. Knowing it well makes the PA significantly easier. Thymeleaf's hallmark is that its templates are valid HTML — a designer can open the file in a browser and see it render, with sample data, before the back-end is wired up. That's its main differentiator vs JSP and other older engines.</p>
</div>

<h3>Adding Thymeleaf to Spring Boot</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;dependency&gt;
    &lt;groupId&gt;org.springframework.boot&lt;/groupId&gt;
    &lt;artifactId&gt;spring-boot-starter-thymeleaf&lt;/artifactId&gt;
&lt;/dependency&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>That's it. By default, Spring Boot looks for templates in <code>src/main/resources/templates/</code> and serves them via controllers that return view names (instead of <code>@ResponseBody</code> data).</p>

<h3>A minimal controller + template</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@Controller
public class HomeController {
    @GetMapping("/")
    public String home(Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("name", "Natasha");
        return "home";   // resolves to templates/home.html
    }
}
</code></pre>

<p>And <code>templates/home.html</code>:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html xmlns:th="http://www.thymeleaf.org"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;Home&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;h1 th:text="'Hello, ' + ${name}"&gt;Hello, World&lt;/h1&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>The <code>th:text</code> attribute REPLACES the element's text content at render time. The literal "Hello, World" inside the h1 is the DESIGN-TIME placeholder — what shows when you open the .html directly in a browser. At runtime, Thymeleaf substitutes <code>"Hello, Natasha"</code>.</p>

<h3>Thymeleaf attribute syntax</h3>

<p>All Thymeleaf attributes start with <code>th:</code>. They override (or add to) standard HTML attributes when the template is processed.</p>

<table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;">
<thead><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--surface-3);">
<th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Attribute</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">What it does</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:text</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Replace text content (HTML-escaped)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:utext</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Replace text content, UN-escaped (raw HTML — dangerous with user data)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:if</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Conditionally include the element</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:unless</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Inverse of th:if</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:each</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Repeat the element for each item in a collection</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:href</code>, <code>th:src</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Substitute URLs (with proper context-path handling)</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:value</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Set form input value</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:object</code>, <code>th:field</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Bind form to a model object</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:class</code>, <code>th:classappend</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Set / append CSS classes dynamically</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>th:fragment</code>, <code>th:insert</code>, <code>th:replace</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Template composition (layouts, includes)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<h3>Iterating with th:each</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;table&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Email&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;
    &lt;tr th:each="user : ${users}"&gt;
        &lt;td th:text="${user.name}"&gt;Default name&lt;/td&gt;
        &lt;td th:text="${user.email}"&gt;sample@example.com&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
&lt;/table&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>If <code>users</code> has 3 entries, you get 3 rows. The placeholder row stays visible in static preview.</p>

<p>For loop status, add a second variable:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;tr th:each="user, iter : ${users}"&gt;
    &lt;td th:text="${iter.index}"&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;       &lt;!-- 0-based index --&gt;
    &lt;td th:text="${iter.count}"&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;       &lt;!-- 1-based count --&gt;
    &lt;td th:text="${iter.first}"&gt;false&lt;/td&gt;   &lt;!-- true for first row --&gt;
    &lt;td th:text="${iter.last}"&gt;false&lt;/td&gt;    &lt;!-- true for last row --&gt;
&lt;/tr&gt;
</code></pre>

<h3>Conditionals</h3>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;p th:if="${user.active}"&gt;Active user&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p th:unless="${user.active}"&gt;Inactive user&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;!-- Switch / case --&gt;
&lt;div th:switch="${user.role}"&gt;
    &lt;p th:case="'ADMIN'"&gt;Administrator&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p th:case="'USER'"&gt;Regular user&lt;/p&gt;
    &lt;p th:case="*"&gt;Other role&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>

<h3>URL handling with @{...}</h3>

<p>Use the <code>@{...}</code> syntax for URLs so Thymeleaf adds the application context path automatically:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;a th:href="@{/users}"&gt;Users&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a th:href="@{/users/{id}(id=${user.id})}"&gt;Edit&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;a th:href="@{/search(q=${query},page=${page})}"&gt;Next page&lt;/a&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>The path-variable syntax is <code>{id}</code> in the URL with values supplied in parentheses. Query parameters go in the same parentheses without curly braces.</p>

<h3>Form binding</h3>

<p>Thymeleaf can bind an entire HTML form to a Java object, so user input flows naturally back to your controller.</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;form th:action="@{/users}" th:object="${user}" method="post"&gt;
    &lt;input type="text" th:field="*{name}" /&gt;
    &lt;input type="email" th:field="*{email}" /&gt;
    &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;
</code></pre>

<p>The <code>th:field="*{name}"</code> on an input generates <code>id="name" name="name" value="..."</code> automatically.</p>

<p>Controller side:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>@GetMapping("/users/new")
public String newUser(Model model) {
    model.addAttribute("user", new User());
    return "user-form";
}

@PostMapping("/users")
public String save(@ModelAttribute User user) {
    userRepo.save(user);
    return "redirect:/users";
}
</code></pre>

<h3>Fragments — template reuse</h3>

<p>Define a reusable fragment once, include it from many pages:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;!-- templates/fragments/nav.html --&gt;
&lt;nav th:fragment="topnav"&gt;
    &lt;a th:href="@{/}"&gt;Home&lt;/a&gt;
    &lt;a th:href="@{/users}"&gt;Users&lt;/a&gt;
&lt;/nav&gt;

&lt;!-- templates/home.html --&gt;
&lt;div th:insert="~{fragments/nav :: topnav}"&gt;&lt;/div&gt;
</code></pre>

<p><code>th:insert</code> places the fragment INSIDE the host element. <code>th:replace</code> REPLACES the host element. <code>th:include</code> is deprecated.</p>

<h3>Layout dialect — full template inheritance</h3>

<p>For "master pages" with content slots, use the <strong>Thymeleaf Layout Dialect</strong>:</p>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;!-- templates/layout.html --&gt;
&lt;html lang="en" xmlns:th="http://www.thymeleaf.org"
      xmlns:layout="http://www.ultraq.net.nz/thymeleaf/layout"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;&lt;title layout:title-pattern="$CONTENT_TITLE - MyApp"&gt;MyApp&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;nav&gt;...&lt;/nav&gt;
    &lt;main layout:fragment="content"&gt;&lt;p&gt;Default content&lt;/p&gt;&lt;/main&gt;
    &lt;footer&gt;...&lt;/footer&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;

&lt;!-- templates/home.html --&gt;
&lt;html layout:decorate="~{layout}"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;Home&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
    &lt;main layout:fragment="content"&gt;
        &lt;h1&gt;Welcome&lt;/h1&gt;
    &lt;/main&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;&lt;/html&gt;
</code></pre>

<h3>Standard expressions</h3>

<ul>
<li><code>${expr}</code> — variable expression (most common)</li>
<li><code>*{expr}</code> — selection expression (relative to a <code>th:object</code>)</li>
<li><code>#{key}</code> — message expression (i18n bundle lookup)</li>
<li><code>@{url}</code> — link/URL expression</li>
<li><code>~{template :: fragment}</code> — fragment expression</li>
</ul>

<h3>Built-in utility objects</h3>

<table style="width:100%; border-collapse:collapse; margin:12px 0;">
<thead><tr style="border-bottom: 1px solid var(--surface-3);">
<th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Object</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:8px;">Use for</th>
</tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>#dates, #temporals</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Formatting dates and times</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>#numbers</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Number formatting</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>#strings</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">String operations</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>#lists, #sets, #maps</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Collection operations</td></tr>
<tr><td style="padding:8px;"><code>#authentication</code></td><td style="padding:8px;">Current user info (with Spring Security)</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>

<pre style="background: var(--surface-2); padding: 12px; border-radius: 6px;"><code>&lt;p&gt;Order placed: &lt;span th:text="${#temporals.format(order.placedAt, 'yyyy-MM-dd HH:mm')}"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;Total: &lt;span th:text="${#numbers.formatCurrency(order.total)}"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
</code></pre>

<h3>XSS protection</h3>

<p>Thymeleaf's <code>th:text</code> automatically HTML-escapes content. Special characters in user input become safe entities (<code>&lt;</code> becomes <code>&lt;</code>). This is the default and is critical for security.</p>

<p>Only use <code>th:utext</code> (unescaped) when you absolutely need raw HTML AND you completely trust the source. Never use it for user-supplied data.</p>

<h3>Common patterns in the D287 PA</h3>

<ul>
<li>List view of items → <code>th:each</code> over a collection</li>
<li>Create/edit forms → <code>th:object</code> + <code>th:field</code> + <code>@ModelAttribute</code></li>
<li>Conditional UI (e.g., "Save" vs "Update") → <code>th:if</code> on id presence</li>
<li>Layout templates → fragments with layout dialect</li>
<li>Error display → <code>th:errors="*{fieldName}"</code> after a validation failure</li>
</ul>

<div class="callout callout-recall">
<div class="callout-label">✓ KEY TAKEAWAYS</div>
<ul>
<li>Thymeleaf templates are VALID HTML — they preview correctly without the server</li>
<li>Add it with <code>spring-boot-starter-thymeleaf</code>; templates go in <code>src/main/resources/templates/</code></li>
<li>Attributes start with <code>th:</code>: <code>th:text</code>, <code>th:if</code>, <code>th:each</code>, <code>th:href</code>, etc.</li>
<li>Expression syntax: <code>${var}</code>, <code>*{field}</code>, <code>#{message}</code>, <code>@{url}</code>, <code>~{fragment}</code></li>
<li>Use <code>th:each="item : ${list}"</code> for iteration; add a status variable for index/first/last</li>
<li>Form binding: <code>th:object</code> on the form, <code>th:field="*{name}"</code> on inputs</li>
<li>Fragments + layout dialect provide template reuse (master/detail pages)</li>
<li>Built-in utility objects: <code>#dates, #numbers, #strings, #lists</code>, etc.</li>
<li><code>th:text</code> auto-escapes — safe by default. <code>th:utext</code> doesn't — use only with trusted content</li>
</ul>
</div>
<i data-tb287c3s2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the MVC round trip that renders a template: model in, view name resolved, th: attributes evaluated, HTML out</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 448" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk190a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the MVC round trip: nothing renders until the controller has returned</text><rect x="24" y="46" width="130" height="48" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="89" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">browser</text><text x="89" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">GET /parts</text><line x1="154" y1="62" x2="210" y2="62" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><circle cx="182" cy="46" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="182" y="50" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="210" y="40" width="204" height="58" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="312" y="68" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PartController.list</text><text x="312" y="83" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">model.addAttribute(...)</text><line x1="312" y1="98" x2="312" y2="130" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><circle cx="334" cy="114" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="334" y="118" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="192" y="130" width="244" height="76" rx="10" fill="#8b5cf6" opacity="0.10" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="314" y="150" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Model — a map of names to objects</text><text x="314" y="172" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"parts" → List of 3 Part rows</text><text x="314" y="190" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"title" → "Parts on hand"</text><line x1="414" y1="62" x2="462" y2="62" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><text x="556" y="50" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">"parts/list"</text><text x="556" y="68" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a view NAME, not a file path</text><line x1="556" y1="76" x2="556" y2="104" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><rect x="452" y="104" width="208" height="48" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="556" y="127" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ViewResolver</text><text x="556" y="142" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">prefix + name + suffix</text><text x="556" y="174" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">classpath:/templates/</text><text x="556" y="192" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">parts/list.html</text><line x1="556" y1="152" x2="556" y2="162" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><line x1="556" y1="202" x2="556" y2="224" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><line x1="314" y1="206" x2="380" y2="226" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><rect x="216" y="224" width="340" height="50" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="386" y="248" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Thymeleaf template engine</text><text x="386" y="263" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">walks the HTML and evaluates every th: attribute</text><line x1="386" y1="274" x2="386" y2="300" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><rect x="24" y="300" width="296" height="100" rx="10" fill="var(--text-3)" opacity="0.10" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="172" y="318" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the template on disk — valid HTML</text><text x="172" y="340" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;td th:text="${p.name}"&gt;</text><text x="172" y="356" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Sample part</text><text x="172" y="372" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;/td&gt;</text><text x="172" y="391" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the literal text is the design-time preview</text><line x1="326" y1="346" x2="362" y2="346" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><rect x="368" y="300" width="292" height="100" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="514" y="318" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the HTML sent to the browser</text><text x="514" y="342" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;td&gt;Brake pad&lt;/td&gt;</text><text x="514" y="360" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;td&gt;Hub bearing&lt;/td&gt;</text><text x="514" y="378" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">&lt;td&gt;Wiper blade&lt;/td&gt;</text><line x1="340" y1="434" x2="90" y2="434" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk190a)"/><text x="220" y="424" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">response — one complete HTML document</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — turning a list of parts into a table, attribute by attribute</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The controller has put a list into the model under the key <code>parts</code>. Build the page that renders it.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@GetMapping("/parts")
public String list(Model model) {
    model.addAttribute("parts", service.findAll());
    return "parts/list";
}</code></pre><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">&lt;!DOCTYPE html&gt;
&lt;html xmlns:th="http://www.thymeleaf.org"&gt;
&lt;head&gt;&lt;title&gt;Parts&lt;/title&gt;&lt;/head&gt;
&lt;body&gt;
  &lt;table&gt;
    &lt;tr&gt;&lt;th&gt;#&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Name&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;On hand&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;Price&lt;/th&gt;&lt;th&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;

    &lt;tr th:each="part, iter : ${parts}"&gt;
      &lt;td th:text="${iter.count}"&gt;1&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td th:text="${part.name}"&gt;Sample part&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td th:text="${part.inventory}"&gt;0&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td th:text="${#numbers.formatDecimal(part.price, 1, 2)}"&gt;0.00&lt;/td&gt;
      &lt;td&gt;&lt;a th:href="@{/parts/{id}/edit(id=${part.id})}"&gt;Edit&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;

    &lt;tr th:if="${#lists.isEmpty(parts)}"&gt;
      &lt;td colspan="5"&gt;No parts yet.&lt;/td&gt;
    &lt;/tr&gt;
  &lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/body&gt;
&lt;/html&gt;</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>th:each</code> repeats the element it sits on</strong>, not that element's children. It belongs on the <code>&lt;tr&gt;</code>, giving one row per part. Put it on the <code>&lt;table&gt;</code> by mistake and you get three complete tables.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The status variable <code>iter</code> is optional</strong> and carries <code>index</code> (0-based), <code>count</code> (1-based), <code>first</code>, <code>last</code>, <code>size</code>, <code>even</code> and <code>odd</code>. A visible row number wants <code>count</code>, not <code>index</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>th:text</code> replaces the element's content and escapes it.</strong> The literal <code>Sample part</code> inside the tag never reaches the browser — it is there so a designer can open the raw file and see a plausible page, which is Thymeleaf's defining feature.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>@{...}</code> is the URL syntax and it is not decoration.</strong> <code>@{/parts/{id}/edit(id=${part.id})}</code> produces <code>/parts/42/edit</code>, and prefixes the application context path if the app is not deployed at the root. String-concatenating the URL breaks the moment it is.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>th:if</code> removes the element entirely when false.</strong> The empty-state row is absent from the HTML when the list has entries — this is not CSS hiding it.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Four attributes carry almost every Thymeleaf page: <code>th:each</code> to repeat, <code>th:text</code> to fill, <code>th:if</code> to include, and <code>th:href="@{...}"</code> to link.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — choosing between th:text and th:utext, and why it is a security decision</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A supplier note has been typed into the parts form. The stored value is, character for character:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">Fits &lt;b&gt;most&lt;/b&gt; 2019+ vans &amp; pickups</code></pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Template attribute</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">What the browser receives</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">What the user sees</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>th:text="${part.note}"</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the angle brackets and ampersand escaped to <code>&lt;</code>, <code>&gt;</code>, <code>&amp;</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the text exactly as it was typed, tags visible</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>th:utext="${part.note}"</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the characters spliced in as live markup</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>most</strong> in bold — the <code>&lt;b&gt;</code> tag executes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>th:text</code> escapes.</strong> <code>&lt;</code> becomes <code>&lt;</code> and <code>&amp;</code> becomes <code>&amp;</code>, so the browser draws the characters instead of interpreting them. This is the default, and it is the safe one.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>th:utext</code> does not escape.</strong> Whatever the value holds is inserted as markup. If the value came from a user, so did the markup — and a note reading <code>&lt;script&gt;…&lt;/script&gt;</code> would then run in the browser of everyone who opens the page. That is stored cross-site scripting.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The rule that follows:</strong> use <code>th:utext</code> only for HTML your own application produced and stored, never for anything a user could influence.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Recognising each failure.</strong> Literal tags on screen where formatting was expected means <code>th:text</code> was used on trusted HTML — a cosmetic bug. Untrusted content being interpreted as markup means <code>th:utext</code> was used on user data — a security bug.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: <code>th:text</code> escapes and is safe by default; <code>th:utext</code> emits raw HTML and is safe only for content you generated yourself.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Learn the four expression prefixes cold, because a question will hand you one and ask what it does: <code>${...}</code> variable, <code>*{...}</code> selection relative to the enclosing <code>th:object</code>, <code>#{...}</code> message bundle for i18n, <code>@{...}</code> URL. Then the attribute pairs: <code>th:text</code> escapes and <code>th:utext</code> does not; <code>th:insert</code> puts a fragment <em>inside</em> the host element while <code>th:replace</code> swaps the host element out; <code>th:if</code> and <code>th:unless</code> are inverses. One file-path fact is worth a mark on its own: templates live in <code>src/main/resources/templates/</code>, so returning <code>"parts/list"</code> resolves to <code>templates/parts/list.html</code>.</p></div><i data-d287cov_32="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div>Since <strong>Spring 4.3</strong>, <code>@Autowired</code> on a constructor is <strong>optional when the class has exactly one constructor</strong> — Spring uses it automatically. Write the annotation only when there are several constructors and you must say which one to use. This is why modern Spring examples show constructors with no annotation at all and it still works.</div>"},"D287/ch3/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch3-s3"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Forms &amp; Model Binding</h3><p>Model binding = Spring automatically <b>fills a Java object from an HTML form</b>. Field names in the form match property names on the object; Spring does the copying.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a complete add-part form, both sides</div><b>Controller</b> (show the form, then handle the submit):<br><pre>@GetMapping("/parts/new")
public String form(Model m) {
    m.addAttribute("part", new Part());   // empty backing object
    return "partForm";
}

@PostMapping("/parts")
public String save(@ModelAttribute Part part) {  // ← bound from the form
    service.save(part);
    return "redirect:/parts";
}</pre><b>Template</b> partForm.html:<br><pre>&lt;form th:action="@{/parts}" th:object="${part}" method="post"&gt;
  &lt;input type="text" th:field="*{name}" /&gt;
  &lt;input type="number" th:field="*{price}" /&gt;
  &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</pre>Typing &ldquo;Bolt&rdquo; and 0.25 and submitting: the POST body has name=Bolt&amp;price=0.25 → Spring creates a Part, sets part.name = "Bolt", part.price = 0.25, and hands it to save(). th:field generated the input&rsquo;s name attribute so the two sides match automatically.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE BINDING CHAIN MUST MATCH</div>th:object names the backing object; th:field="*{name}" must match a real property (getName/setName). A typo breaks silently — the object arrives with null in that field. When a form &ldquo;loses&rdquo; a value, check the field/property names first.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>@ModelAttribute on a controller parameter tells Spring to:<ol type="A"><li>Build the object from the submitted form fields</li><li>Read a path variable</li><li>Return JSON</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Spring instantiates the object and copies matching form fields into its properties — that&rsquo;s model binding.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Forms are how users send data to your application. Spring's model binding takes the tedium out of forms — you write a class and a template, and Spring handles parsing, conversion, and population. Understanding the binding model is what lets you handle real CRUD apps fluently.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. The model binding loop.</strong> (1) Controller method GET adds an empty (or pre-filled) object to the model. (2) Template renders form using th:object and th:field. (3) User submits. (4) Controller method POST receives the populated object via @ModelAttribute. (5) Process and persist. The same object travels round-trip; Spring populates it from form data automatically.</p>
<p><strong>2. @ModelAttribute on a parameter.</strong> @PostMapping("/parts") public String save(@ModelAttribute Part part). Spring instantiates Part, populates each field from form data with matching names, and passes it to your method. No request.getParameter() boilerplate.</p>
<p><strong>3. @ModelAttribute on a method.</strong> On an @ModelAttribute-annotated method in a controller, Spring runs it before each request handler and adds its return to the model. Useful for populating dropdown options, default values, or shared data every page in the controller needs.</p>
<p><strong>4. GET and POST handlers as a pair.</strong> Typical pattern: GET /parts/new shows the empty form; POST /parts processes submission. GET /parts/{id}/edit shows the form pre-filled; POST /parts/{id} updates. The two-handler pattern is universal across server-rendered CRUD.</p>
<p><strong>5. Type conversion is automatic.</strong> Form fields arrive as strings; Spring converts to int, long, BigDecimal, LocalDate, enums automatically. Custom converters extend this. If conversion fails, binding errors are added to BindingResult.</p>
<p><strong>6. BindingResult catches binding errors.</strong> @PostMapping public String save(@Valid @ModelAttribute Part part, BindingResult result) — BindingResult MUST come immediately after the @ModelAttribute parameter. If it does not, validation errors throw an exception instead of being captured. Forgetting this is a top-five Spring MVC mistake.</p>
<p><strong>7. Redirect after POST.</strong> After successfully processing a POST, redirect (return "redirect:/parts") rather than rendering a view directly. Prevents resubmission on browser refresh. The Post-Redirect-Get pattern; bake it into every form-handling controller.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Misnamed form fields — Spring silently fails to bind them and leaves the property null.</li>
 <li>BindingResult parameter not immediately after the @ModelAttribute parameter — validation errors throw exceptions instead of populating BindingResult.</li>
 <li>Forgetting redirect-after-POST — refresh reposts the form, creating duplicate records.</li>
 <li>Mutating the bound object in unexpected ways before save — you lose user-entered data.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Forms work via a round-trip: controller adds object to model, template renders it, user submits, controller receives populated object via @ModelAttribute. BindingResult catches binding/validation errors but only if positioned correctly. Always redirect after a successful POST.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ModelAttribute</span></dt>
 <dd>Annotation binding form data to a method parameter or pre-populating model attributes.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">BindingResult</span></dt>
 <dd>Holds errors from data binding and validation; must immediately follow the bound parameter.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Type conversion</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring's automatic conversion from form-string values to typed Java fields.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Post-Redirect-Get (PRG)</span></dt>
 <dd>Pattern of redirecting after POST to avoid resubmission on refresh.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Form backing object</span></dt>
 <dd>The Java object form fields bind to (typically the entity or a DTO).</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c3s3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the form round trip, and the one branch that decides whether the user keeps their typing</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 414" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk191a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">one object, five stops — the form round trip</text><circle cx="46" cy="68" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="46" y="72" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><rect x="66" y="44" width="200" height="52" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="166" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">GET /parts/new</text><text x="166" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">model.addAttribute("part", new Inhouse())</text><line x1="266" y1="70" x2="316" y2="70" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk191a)"/><circle cx="336" cy="68" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="336" y="72" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><rect x="356" y="44" width="296" height="52" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="504" y="69" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the template renders the form</text><text x="504" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">th:object="${part}"   th:field="*{name}"</text><path d="M560,96 Q560,122 560,138" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk191a)"/><text x="618" y="122" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the user types</text><circle cx="336" cy="162" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="336" y="166" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><rect x="356" y="138" width="296" height="52" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="504" y="163" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">POST /parts</text><text x="504" y="178" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">name=Brake+pad&amp;price=12.50&amp;inventory=40</text><line x1="354" y1="164" x2="306" y2="164" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk191a)"/><rect x="66" y="138" width="240" height="52" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="186" y="163" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">WebDataBinder</text><text x="186" y="178" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">matches posted names to setters</text><line x1="186" y1="190" x2="186" y2="224" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk191a)"/><text x="196" y="210" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">every value arrives as a String</text><circle cx="46" cy="250" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="46" y="254" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><rect x="66" y="224" width="240" height="54" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="186" y="250" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">type conversion</text><text x="186" y="265" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">"12.50" → BigDecimal, "40" → int</text><line x1="306" y1="251" x2="350" y2="251" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk191a)"/><rect x="350" y="224" width="210" height="54" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="455" y="250" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@Valid runs</text><text x="455" y="265" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">violations land in BindingResult</text><line x1="455" y1="278" x2="455" y2="298" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk191a)"/><text x="468" y="294" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">result.hasErrors()?</text><circle cx="46" cy="344" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="46" y="348" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">5</text><rect x="66" y="318" width="262" height="56" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="197" y="345" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">true → return "parts/form"</text><text x="197" y="360" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">same request, the typing survives</text><rect x="370" y="318" width="282" height="56" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="511" y="345" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">false → save, then "redirect:/parts"</text><text x="511" y="360" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a fresh GET; refresh is harmless</text><line x1="451" y1="300" x2="300" y2="316" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk191a)"/><line x1="459" y1="300" x2="520" y2="316" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk191a)"/><text x="340" y="398" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Redirecting on error would discard everything the user typed. Re-render on error; redirect only on success.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the GET/POST pair for creating a part</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A create form is always two handlers around one object, with the template between them.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@GetMapping("/parts/new")
public String showForm(Model model) {
    model.addAttribute("part", new Inhouse());   // the form-backing object
    return "parts/form";
}

@PostMapping("/parts")
public String save(@Valid @ModelAttribute("part") Inhouse part,
                   BindingResult result,          // MUST be the next parameter
                   RedirectAttributes redirect) {
    if (result.hasErrors()) {
        return "parts/form";                       // re-render, values intact
    }
    service.save(part);
    redirect.addFlashAttribute("message", "Part saved.");
    return "redirect:/parts";                      // Post-Redirect-Get
}</code></pre><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">&lt;form th:action="@{/parts}" th:object="${part}" method="post"&gt;

  &lt;input type="text" th:field="*{name}" /&gt;
  &lt;span th:if="${#fields.hasErrors('name')}" th:errors="*{name}"&gt;error&lt;/span&gt;

  &lt;input type="number" th:field="*{inventory}" /&gt;
  &lt;input type="number" step="0.01" th:field="*{price}" /&gt;

  &lt;input type="hidden" th:field="*{id}" /&gt;       &lt;!-- empty when creating --&gt;
  &lt;button type="submit"&gt;Save&lt;/button&gt;
&lt;/form&gt;</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The GET handler must put an object in the model.</strong> Without it <code>th:object="${part}"</code> fails at render time with "Neither BindingResult nor plain target object for bean name 'part' available" — a message that looks like a validation problem but is really a missing model attribute.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>th:field="*{name}"</code> writes three attributes at once</strong>: <code>id="name"</code>, <code>name="name"</code>, and <code>value</code> taken from the current property. That generated <code>name</code> is what makes binding work on the way back, which is exactly why hand-writing <code>name=</code> is where mis-binding starts.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Binding happens by property name, silently.</strong> A field posted as <code>partName</code> when the property is <code>name</code> raises no error at all — the property stays null and a half-empty row is saved.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>BindingResult</code> must be the parameter immediately after the validated object.</strong> Slide a <code>Model</code> in between and Spring no longer treats it as that object's error holder, so failures propagate as an exception instead of being collected.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Success redirects, and flash attributes cross the redirect.</strong> <code>addFlashAttribute</code> survives exactly one redirect, which is how "Part saved." appears on the list page without becoming a query parameter.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: GET builds an empty object; POST receives the filled one. The only two hard rules are the position of <code>BindingResult</code> and re-render-on-error / redirect-on-success.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — debugging a form field that is always null</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Parts save without complaint, but <code>machine_id</code> is <code>NULL</code> in every row. No exception, no validation error. Walk the round trip in order — the fault is always at one of four points.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Check</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">How to test it</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">What the failure looks like</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">1. The input is inside the form element</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">View source in the browser</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the input renders, but outside <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>, so it is never submitted</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">2. The posted name matches the property</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">DevTools → Network → the POST → Form Data</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>machineID=55</code> posted for a property named <code>machineId</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">3. A setter exists with the matching name</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Look for <code>setMachineId</code> on the class</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">only a field, or a setter spelled <code>setMachineID</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">4. The bound type declares the property</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Check the <code>@ModelAttribute</code> parameter type</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">binding to <code>Part</code> when <code>machineId</code> is declared on <code>Inhouse</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Reproduce from the raw request first.</strong> If the posted form data does not contain the field at all, no amount of controller work helps — the problem is in the template.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Prefer <code>th:field</code> to a hand-written name.</strong> <code>th:field="*{machineId}"</code> derives the name from the property, so checks 2 and 3 cannot disagree with each other.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Remember binding uses setters, not fields.</strong> A property is whatever <code>getX</code>/<code>setX</code> say it is, and JavaBeans naming makes <code>setMachineID</code> the setter for a property called <code>machineID</code> — which is not <code>machineId</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Watch the inheritance case, which is the one the PA hits.</strong> <code>machineId</code> belongs to <code>Inhouse</code> and <code>companyName</code> to <code>Outsourced</code>. A handler declaring <code>@ModelAttribute Part part</code> gives the subclass-only fields nowhere to bind, and they are dropped without complaint.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Confirm by logging the errors:</strong> <code>if (result.hasErrors()) log.warn("binding errors: {}", result.getAllErrors());</code>. Conversion failures — a letter typed into a number field — also land in <code>BindingResult</code> rather than throwing, so they hide in exactly the same way.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: A silently null property is a name mismatch nine times in ten. Spring binds by property name and says nothing when no property matches, which is why <code>th:field</code> — which generates the name for you — prevents the bug outright.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The single most-tested fact in this section: <strong><code>BindingResult</code> must immediately follow the <code>@Valid</code> parameter</strong> — put anything between them and validation errors are thrown instead of collected. Second: after a successful POST, <strong>redirect</strong> (<code>return "redirect:/parts"</code>) so a refresh does not resubmit; the name for this is Post-Redirect-Get. Third: on a validation failure, <strong>return the view name rather than redirecting</strong>, or the user's input is lost. Fourth, the three-annotation trio: <code>th:object</code> on the <code>&lt;form&gt;</code>, <code>th:field="*{property}"</code> on each input, <code>@ModelAttribute</code> on the handler parameter that receives the object.</p></div><i data-d287cov_33="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>CSRF</strong> (cross-site request forgery) protection is <strong>enabled by default by Spring Security</strong> for state-changing requests — POST, PUT, DELETE. Each rendered form must carry a token that the server then verifies. When Thymeleaf is integrated with Spring Security, a form using <code>th:action</code> gets the <strong>hidden token input added automatically</strong>.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>A POST form that suddenly returns 403 Forbidden is the classic symptom of a missing CSRF token — usually a form written with a plain <code>action=</code> instead of <code>th:action=</code>, so Thymeleaf never injected the hidden field.</div>"},"D287/ch4/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch4-s1"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — JPA Entities (a class that IS a table)</h3><p><b>JPA</b> maps Java objects to database rows so you work with objects, not SQL. <b>Hibernate</b> is the engine that does the mapping. An <b>entity</b> is a class marked as a table:</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — an entity, annotation by annotation</div><pre>@Entity                       // ← this class maps to a table
public class Part {
    @Id                                              // primary key
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY) // DB assigns it
    private Long id;

    private String name;      // becomes column "name" automatically
    private double price;

    @Transient                // ← NOT stored in the database
    private double priceWithTax;   // computed at runtime only
}</pre>Saving <code>new Part("Bolt", 0.25)</code> inserts a row with an auto-generated id. Loading it back builds a Part object from the row. priceWithTax never touches the DB — that&rsquo;s exactly what <b>@Transient</b> means: a field the mapping skips.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE ANNOTATIONS TO KNOW</div>&bull; <b>@Entity</b> — map class ↔ table. Needs a no-arg constructor.<br>&bull; <b>@Id</b> — the primary-key field.<br>&bull; <b>@GeneratedValue</b> — the database generates the key (IDENTITY = auto-increment).<br>&bull; <b>@Column(name="unit_price")</b> — customize the column; optional when names match.<br>&bull; <b>@Transient</b> — exclude a field from persistence.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>A field that should exist on the object but never be saved to the database is marked:<ol type="A"><li>@Transient</li><li>@Id</li><li>@Column</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — @Transient.</b> JPA skips it when reading and writing rows.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>JPA entities are how Spring Data persists your objects to a database. Get the annotations right and you barely think about SQL. Get them wrong and you fight Hibernate exceptions for days. The mapping is small but precise; understanding each annotation pays back constantly.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. @Entity marks a class as persistent.</strong> @Entity on a class tells JPA this represents a database table. By default, the table name matches the class name (Part → PART). Override with @Table(name = "parts"). The class needs a no-arg constructor (JPA's reflection requires it) and a primary key.</p>
<p><strong>2. @Id marks the primary key.</strong> Every entity needs exactly one @Id field. Without it, JPA fails at startup. The id is what JPA uses to identify and look up entities.</p>
<p><strong>3. @GeneratedValue automates IDs.</strong> @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY) lets the database assign auto-increment IDs (typical for MySQL, H2). GenerationType.SEQUENCE uses a database sequence (Oracle, Postgres). GenerationType.AUTO lets the provider pick. For the PA's H2 setup, IDENTITY is the standard choice.</p>
<p><strong>4. @Column for column-level details.</strong> @Column(name = "part_name", length = 100, nullable = false) overrides the default column name and adds constraints. Many fields need no @Column at all — the defaults are sensible. Add it only when you need to override something.</p>
<p><strong>5. Field types and conversion.</strong> Java types map to SQL types automatically: String → VARCHAR, int → INTEGER, BigDecimal → DECIMAL, LocalDate → DATE, Enum → either string or ordinal (use @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING) for safety). Hibernate handles most conversions; explicit @Convert handles the rest.</p>
<p><strong>6. Inheritance: @Inheritance and @DiscriminatorColumn.</strong> When entities share fields (Inhouse and Outsourced both extend Part in the D287 PA), JPA can persist them three ways: SINGLE_TABLE (one table with a discriminator column — usually default and best), JOINED (one table per class, joined by id), or TABLE_PER_CLASS. SINGLE_TABLE is the typical choice.</p>
<p><strong>7. Equals and hashCode quirks.</strong> JPA entities have surprising equality semantics. The id is null until persistence; it changes after save. Common safe patterns: use UUIDs to assign on construction, or use the id but exclude null values from hash. Easiest rule: don't use entities in HashSet/HashMap keys unless you have thought this through.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Missing no-arg constructor — JPA fails at startup with cryptic 'no default constructor' errors.</li>
 <li>@GeneratedValue without specifying strategy — sometimes works but not portable across databases.</li>
 <li>Storing enums with EnumType.ORDINAL — adding a value to the enum reorders the database mapping disastrously. Always use EnumType.STRING.</li>
 <li>Implementing equals/hashCode using @Id while it can be null — entities behave inconsistently in collections.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>@Entity marks the class. @Id picks the primary key field. @GeneratedValue auto-generates it. @Column tunes column-level details. @Inheritance handles entity inheritance — SINGLE_TABLE with @DiscriminatorColumn is the default. Use EnumType.STRING. Be careful with equals/hashCode and entities in sets/maps.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Entity</span></dt>
 <dd>Marks a class as a JPA-managed persistent entity.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Id</span></dt>
 <dd>Marks the field that serves as the entity's primary key.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@GeneratedValue</span></dt>
 <dd>Specifies that the primary key is auto-generated; strategies include IDENTITY, SEQUENCE, AUTO.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Column</span></dt>
 <dd>Configures column-level details: name, length, nullability, uniqueness.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Inheritance</span></dt>
 <dd>Specifies how an entity hierarchy is mapped: SINGLE_TABLE, JOINED, or TABLE_PER_CLASS.</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c4s1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — one entity beside the table it maps to, field by field</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 378" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk192a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the annotations are a mapping, and the mapping is one arrow per field</text><rect x="18" y="40" width="300" height="280" rx="10" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.10" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="168" y="60" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Part.java</text><text x="34" y="74" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Entity</text><text x="34" y="92" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Table(name = "parts")</text><text x="34" y="110" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">public class Part {</text><text x="34" y="134" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">    @Id</text><text x="34" y="152" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">    @GeneratedValue(strategy = IDENTITY)</text><text x="34" y="170" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">    private Long id;</text><text x="34" y="200" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">    @Column(nullable = false, length = 100)</text><text x="34" y="218" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">    private String name;</text><text x="34" y="248" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">    private BigDecimal price;</text><text x="34" y="278" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">    private int inventory;</text><text x="34" y="302" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">}</text><rect x="392" y="40" width="270" height="280" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="527" y="60" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PARTS table in H2</text><rect x="406" y="76" width="242" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="416" y="96" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">id</text><text x="500" y="96" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">BIGINT</text><text x="527" y="114" fill="#10b981" font-size="8.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PRIMARY KEY, auto</text><rect x="406" y="136" width="242" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="416" y="156" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">name</text><text x="500" y="156" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">VARCHAR(100)</text><text x="527" y="174" fill="#10b981" font-size="8.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">NOT NULL</text><rect x="406" y="184" width="242" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="416" y="204" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">price</text><text x="500" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">DECIMAL(19,2)</text><rect x="406" y="232" width="242" height="30" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="416" y="252" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">inventory</text><text x="500" y="252" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">INTEGER</text><line x1="216" y1="170" x2="402" y2="92" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk192a)"/><line x1="216" y1="218" x2="402" y2="152" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk192a)"/><line x1="216" y1="248" x2="402" y2="200" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk192a)"/><line x1="216" y1="278" x2="402" y2="248" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk192a)"/><text x="340" y="348" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">No @Column on price or inventory: the defaults apply. camelCase becomes snake_case, so a field</text><text x="340" y="364" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">named machineId would map to a column named machine_id without you writing anything.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — mapping a Product entity from a table specification</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">You are given the schema and asked for the entity. Take the columns one at a time; each one is a decision with a default.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Column</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Type</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>id</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">BIGINT</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">primary key, database-generated</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>name</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">VARCHAR(120)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">required, unique</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>price</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">DECIMAL(19,2)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">required</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>inventory</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">INTEGER</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">required</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>status</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">VARCHAR(20)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">one of ACTIVE, DISCONTINUED</td></tr></tbody></table></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Entity
@Table(name = "products")
public class Product {

    @Id
    @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @Column(nullable = false, length = 120, unique = true)
    private String name;

    @Column(nullable = false, precision = 19, scale = 2)
    private BigDecimal price;

    @Column(nullable = false)
    private int inventory;

    @Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)          // never ORDINAL
    @Column(length = 20)
    private ProductStatus status;

    protected Product() { }               // JPA needs a no-arg constructor

    public Product(String name, BigDecimal price, int inventory) {
        this.name = name; this.price = price; this.inventory = inventory;
    }
    // getters and setters
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>@Entity</code> plus <code>@Id</code> is the minimum.</strong> Leave out <code>@Id</code> and startup fails with "No identifier specified for entity". Leave out <code>@Entity</code> and the class is an ordinary object the repository cannot handle.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>@Table</code> is optional</strong> — without it the table name defaults to the class name. Name it explicitly when the table is plural or already exists, which is both of those cases here.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>IDENTITY</code> is the right strategy for H2 and MySQL</strong> — the database assigns an auto-increment value on INSERT. <code>SEQUENCE</code> suits Postgres and Oracle; <code>AUTO</code> lets the provider choose and is the reason two developers can get different schemas from the same code.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Use <code>BigDecimal</code> for money, never <code>double</code>.</strong> <code>double</code> cannot represent 0.10 exactly, so totals drift by fractions of a cent and never reconcile. <code>precision</code> and <code>scale</code> map to DECIMAL(19,2).</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)</code> is not a preference.</strong> The default is <code>ORDINAL</code>, which stores the enum's position as an integer — so inserting a new constant in the middle of the enum silently reinterprets every row already in the database.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The no-arg constructor can be <code>protected</code>.</strong> JPA instantiates entities reflectively and needs one; making it protected keeps it out of your own code's way while remaining visible to the provider.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Six annotations do all of it: <code>@Entity</code>, <code>@Table</code>, <code>@Id</code>, <code>@GeneratedValue</code>, <code>@Column</code>, <code>@Enumerated</code>. Everything else is a default you chose not to override.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — why an entity disappears from a HashSet after you save it</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">This is the equals/hashCode trap, and it is much easier to believe once you have watched it happen. The entity uses the standard "compare by id" implementation that IDEs generate:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">import java.util.*;

class Part {
    Long id;
    String name;
    Part(Long id, String name) { this.id = id; this.name = name; }

    @Override public boolean equals(Object o) {
        if (this == o) return true;
        if (!(o instanceof Part)) return false;
        return Objects.equals(id, ((Part) o).id);
    }
    @Override public int hashCode() { return Objects.hash(id); }
}

public class D {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Set&lt;Part&gt; cart = new HashSet&lt;&gt;();
        Part brakePad = new Part(null, "Brake pad");
        cart.add(brakePad);
        System.out.println("contains before save: " + cart.contains(brakePad));
        brakePad.id = 17L;                       // exactly what save() does
        System.out.println("contains after save:  " + cart.contains(brakePad));
        System.out.println("set size: " + cart.size());
    }
}</code></pre><div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.07em; color:var(--text-3); margin:14px 0 -2px;">OUTPUT — verified on JDK 21</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto; border-left:3px solid #10b981;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">contains before save: true
contains after save:  false
set size: 1</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The object was added while <code>id</code> was null</strong>, so <code>HashSet</code> filed it under <code>Objects.hash(null)</code>, which is 31.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>save()</code> assigns the generated id.</strong> The very same object now hashes to <code>Objects.hash(17L)</code> — a different bucket entirely.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>contains</code> looks in the new bucket and finds nothing</strong>, so it reports <code>false</code> about an object the set is still holding. Nothing was removed: the size is still 1.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>This is not a JPA bug.</strong> It is the documented contract of <code>HashSet</code>: an object's hash code must not change while it is in the set. <code>@GeneratedValue</code> guarantees that it will.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The practical rules.</strong> Do not put unsaved entities in a <code>HashSet</code> or use them as <code>HashMap</code> keys. If you must implement <code>equals</code>, base it on a natural business key that is set at construction (a part number, a SKU) rather than on the generated id, or assign a UUID yourself in the constructor.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: The set never lost the object — the object moved. A generated id changes the hash code after insertion, which is precisely the situation <code>HashSet</code> is documented not to survive.</div></div><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the persistence lifecycle as a state machine, with the transition that surprises people drawn in</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 338" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk193a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the persistence lifecycle — the state an object is in decides whether saving it does anything</text><rect x="30" y="60" width="160" height="58" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="110" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">TRANSIENT</text><text x="110" y="103" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">new Part() — no id yet</text><text x="110" y="138" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the database has</text><text x="110" y="152" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">never heard of it</text><line x1="190" y1="89" x2="256" y2="89" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk193a)"/><text x="223" y="76" fill="#10b981" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">persist / save</text><rect x="258" y="60" width="176" height="58" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="346" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">MANAGED</text><text x="346" y="103" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">in the persistence context</text><line x1="434" y1="82" x2="508" y2="82" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk193a)"/><text x="471" y="66" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">detach / close</text><rect x="510" y="60" width="150" height="58" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="585" y="88" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">DETACHED</text><text x="585" y="103" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">still has an id</text><path d="M510,110 Q473,150 436,110" fill="none" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk193a)"/><text x="473" y="150" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">merge</text><line x1="346" y1="118" x2="346" y2="174" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk193a)"/><text x="358" y="150" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">remove</text><rect x="258" y="174" width="176" height="52" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="346" y="199" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">REMOVED</text><text x="346" y="214" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">scheduled for deletion</text><line x1="346" y1="226" x2="346" y2="250" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk193a)"/><text x="346" y="266" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">DELETE FROM parts WHERE id = ? — issued at flush, not at remove()</text><text x="340" y="300" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">MANAGED: dirty checking is on — a setter call becomes an UPDATE at the next flush, with no save() at all.</text><text x="340" y="322" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">DETACHED: changing the object changes nothing. That is the whole reason merge() exists.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Three facts carry this section. <strong>(1) The minimum entity</strong> is <code>@Entity</code> + <code>@Id</code> + a no-arg constructor; missing either annotation is a startup failure with a very readable message. <strong>(2) <code>@GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)</code></strong> is the answer whenever H2 or MySQL auto-increment is described. <strong>(3) <code>@Enumerated(EnumType.STRING)</code></strong> is always the correct choice, because the ORDINAL default breaks when the enum is reordered. If a question shows a <code>double</code> price, that is the wrong answer — money is <code>BigDecimal</code>.</p></div><i data-d287cov_41="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">ORM, and the two controller annotations</h4><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>ORM</strong> stands for <strong>Object-Relational Mapping</strong>: the technique of mapping Java objects onto database rows so you work with objects instead of SQL. JPA is the specification, Hibernate the implementation Spring Boot uses by default.</div><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;"></th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">@Controller</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">@RestController</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Return value means</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a <strong>view name</strong> to render (Thymeleaf)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the <strong>response body</strong> itself</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Equivalent to</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">—</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>@Controller</code> + <code>@ResponseBody</code> on every method</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Typical use</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">server-rendered HTML pages</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a JSON REST API</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>That is exactly the distinction the question draws: <code>@RestController</code> <strong>adds @ResponseBody to every method, so return values become the response body</strong> — serialised to JSON by Jackson — rather than being treated as the name of a template.</p><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Returning a String from a <code>@RestController</code> sends that text as the body. The same code in a <code>@Controller</code> looks for a <em>template</em> with that name and fails with a view-resolution error. Mixing the two up is the most common cause of "why is my page showing raw text".</div>"},"D287/ch4/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch4-s2"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Entity Relationships (@OneToMany &amp; friends)</h3><p>Tables relate (a product HAS many parts); entities mirror that with relationship annotations.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — Product ↔ Part, both directions</div><pre>@Entity
public class Product {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "product",
               cascade = CascadeType.ALL,
               orphanRemoval = true)
    private List&lt;Part&gt; parts = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
}

@Entity
public class Part {
    @Id @GeneratedValue private Long id;

    @ManyToOne
    @JoinColumn(name = "product_id")   // the foreign-key column
    private Product product;
}</pre>The Part table carries a product_id column — the <b>many side owns the foreign key</b>. <code>mappedBy="product"</code> tells Product that Part&rsquo;s field is in charge (no duplicate join table).</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE RELATIONSHIP TOOLKIT</div>&bull; <b>@OneToMany / @ManyToOne</b> — the classic parent/child pair.<br>&bull; <b>@ManyToMany</b> — needs a join table.<br>&bull; <b>cascade = CascadeType.ALL</b> — operations on the parent ripple to children (save the product → its parts save too).<br>&bull; <b>orphanRemoval = true</b> — remove a child from the parent&rsquo;s collection and it is <b>DELETED from the database</b> (an &ldquo;orphan&rdquo; with no parent is purged).<br>&bull; <b>FetchType.LAZY vs EAGER</b> — load children on first use vs immediately.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ orphanRemoval ≠ cascade REMOVE</div>Cascade REMOVE fires when the PARENT is deleted. <b>orphanRemoval</b> also fires when a child is merely <b>removed from the collection</b> — product.getParts().remove(p) deletes p&rsquo;s row. Know that difference; it&rsquo;s a favorite question.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>With orphanRemoval = true, removing a Part from product.getParts() causes:<ol type="A"><li>The part&rsquo;s row to be deleted from the database</li><li>Nothing — the row stays</li><li>The product to be deleted</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> A child detached from its parent collection becomes an orphan and is deleted.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Real domain models have relationships: a Product has Parts; an Order has LineItems; a Course has Students. JPA relationship annotations express these in code, but the way they're configured affects performance, query behavior, and even whether deletes cascade correctly. Most production JPA bugs trace back to relationship missteps.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. @OneToMany and @ManyToOne are paired.</strong> A Part has one Product (@ManyToOne); a Product has many Parts (@OneToMany). The owning side (typically the @ManyToOne) holds the foreign key column. The inverse side uses mappedBy to point back: @OneToMany(mappedBy = "product").</p>
<p><strong>2. @JoinColumn on the owning side.</strong> @ManyToOne @JoinColumn(name = "product_id") Part part. This says: the parts table has a product_id column referencing products(id). If you do not specify @JoinColumn, JPA picks a default name; often you want explicit control.</p>
<p><strong>3. @ManyToMany requires a join table.</strong> Each side has @ManyToMany; one side names the join table with @JoinTable(name = "product_parts", joinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "product_id"), inverseJoinColumns = @JoinColumn(name = "part_id")). The other side uses mappedBy. The PA uses this for the products-parts relationship.</p>
<p><strong>4. Cascade types.</strong> CascadeType.PERSIST means saving the parent saves the children. CascadeType.REMOVE deletes the children when the parent is deleted. CascadeType.ALL covers all of them. Be careful with REMOVE — accidentally cascading can wipe out half your database. Default is no cascade.</p>
<p><strong>5. FetchType: LAZY vs EAGER.</strong> FetchType.LAZY loads associated entities on first access. FetchType.EAGER loads them immediately with the parent. @ManyToOne defaults to EAGER (often wrong). @OneToMany defaults to LAZY (usually right). Eager fetching is the leading cause of N+1 query problems.</p>
<p><strong>6. The N+1 query problem.</strong> Loading 100 products and accessing each one's parts triggers 1 query for products + 100 queries for parts. Fix with JPQL fetch joins (LEFT JOIN FETCH) or @EntityGraph. Profile your queries; this problem hides until it dominates response time.</p>
<p><strong>7. Bidirectional relationships need helper methods.</strong> Setting product.parts.add(part) does not update part.product. Add helper methods (addPart, removePart) that update both sides atomically. Otherwise data flushes inconsistently and queries return stale results.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Defaulting to @ManyToOne EAGER fetching everywhere — every parent load explodes into N+1 queries.</li>
 <li>CascadeType.ALL on relationships where children should outlive the parent — accidental data loss.</li>
 <li>Bidirectional @OneToMany where only one side is updated — data inconsistency until next reload.</li>
 <li>Forgetting mappedBy on the inverse side — JPA creates two relationships and a phantom join table.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>@OneToMany / @ManyToOne pair, with mappedBy on the inverse side. @ManyToMany uses @JoinTable. @JoinColumn names the foreign key. Cascade types control propagation; default to none and add carefully. Fetch types control loading; lazy by default for performance. Use helper methods to keep bidirectional relationships consistent.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@OneToMany / @ManyToOne</span></dt>
 <dd>JPA annotations for one-to-many parent-child relationships.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ManyToMany</span></dt>
 <dd>JPA annotation for many-to-many relationships, requiring a join table.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">mappedBy</span></dt>
 <dd>Names the field on the owning side; declares this side as the inverse.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Cascade type</span></dt>
 <dd>Controls how operations (persist, remove, etc.) propagate from parent to children.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">N+1 query problem</span></dt>
 <dd>Performance issue from loading children one at a time after loading the parent collection.</dd>
</dl>
