0" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">7</text><rect x="134" y="218" width="104" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="186" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Hub bearing</text><rect x="238" y="218" width="104" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="290" y="236" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><rect x="30" y="244" width="104" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="82" y="262" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">8</text><rect x="134" y="244" width="104" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="186" y="262" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Wheel nut</text><rect x="238" y="244" width="104" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="290" y="262" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><rect x="30" y="270" width="104" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="82" y="288" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">9</text><rect x="134" y="270" width="104" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="186" y="288" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Brake pad</text><rect x="238" y="270" width="104" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="290" y="288" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><text x="478" y="180" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">PRODUCTS</text><rect x="430" y="192" width="96" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="478" y="210" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">id</text><rect x="526" y="192" width="96" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface-2)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.3"/><text x="574" y="210" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">name</text><rect x="430" y="218" width="96" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="478" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">1</text><rect x="526" y="218" width="96" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="574" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Wheel assembly</text><rect x="430" y="244" width="96" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="478" y="262" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">2</text><rect x="526" y="244" width="96" height="26" rx="0" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.1"/><text x="574" y="262" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Brake kit</text><line x1="344" y1="231" x2="426" y2="231" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk194a)"/><line x1="344" y1="257" x2="426" y2="233" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk194a)"/><line x1="344" y1="283" x2="426" y2="259" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk194a)"/><text x="385" y="186" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">foreign key</text><text x="340" y="320" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Three rows in PARTS point at two rows in PRODUCTS. PRODUCTS has no column pointing back —</text><text x="340" y="336" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the "many" end of a one-to-many is always where the foreign key is stored.</text><text x="340" y="366" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Drop mappedBy and JPA assumes two separate relationships, inventing a PRODUCTS_PARTS join table nobody asked for.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — setting one side of a bidirectional relationship and losing the data</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Adding a part to a product's list looks like it should be enough. Run it and watch what the foreign-key column would hold.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">import java.util.*;

class Product {
    String name;
    List&lt;Part&gt; parts = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();      // the inverse side (mappedBy)
    Product(String name) { this.name = name; }
}
class Part {
    String name;
    Product product;                           // the owning side (@JoinColumn)
    Part(String name) { this.name = name; }
}

public class E {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Product wheelAssembly = new Product("Wheel assembly");
        Part hubBearing = new Part("Hub bearing");

        wheelAssembly.parts.add(hubBearing);   // only the inverse side is set
        System.out.println("product lists the part? " + wheelAssembly.parts.contains(hubBearing));
        System.out.println("part_id column would be: " + (hubBearing.product == null ? "NULL" : hubBearing.product.name));

        hubBearing.product = wheelAssembly;    // now the owning side too
        System.out.println("after setting the owning side: " + hubBearing.product.name);
    }
}</code></pre><div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.07em; color:var(--text-3); margin:14px 0 -2px;">OUTPUT — verified on JDK 21</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto; border-left:3px solid #10b981;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">product lists the part? true
part_id column would be: NULL
after setting the owning side: Wheel assembly</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>In memory the relationship looks correct</strong> — the product's list contains the part, and any test that only checks the list passes.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The database sees nothing.</strong> Hibernate writes the foreign key from the <em>owning</em> side, which is the field annotated <code>@ManyToOne @JoinColumn</code>. That field is still null, so <code>parts.product_id</code> is written as NULL.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The bug hides until the next reload.</strong> Within the same persistence context the in-memory list is returned as-is. Restart, or clear the context, and the part has silently detached from its product.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>mappedBy</code> is the declaration of who owns it.</strong> <code>@OneToMany(mappedBy = "product")</code> means "the <code>product</code> field on <code>Part</code> is in charge of this relationship; I only read it."</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The fix is a helper method</strong> that updates both sides at once, so no caller can set only half of it:</li></ol><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">// on Product
public void addPart(Part part) {
    parts.add(part);          // inverse side, for in-memory navigation
    part.setProduct(this);    // owning side, for the foreign key
}

public void removePart(Part part) {
    parts.remove(part);
    part.setProduct(null);
}</code></pre><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Set the owning side or the database does not change. The inverse side exists for navigation in Java; it is not what gets written.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — reading fetch types and cascade rules off a mapping</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Given this mapping, answer three questions without running anything.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Entity
public class Product {
    @OneToMany(mappedBy = "product", cascade = CascadeType.ALL, orphanRemoval = true)
    private List&lt;Part&gt; parts = new ArrayList&lt;&gt;();
}

@Entity
public class Part {
    @ManyToOne                       // note: no fetch attribute
    @JoinColumn(name = "product_id")
    private Product product;
}</code></pre><h4 style="margin:20px 0 6px; font-size:15.5px; font-weight:700;">1. How many queries does loading 50 products and printing each product's part count cost?</h4><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;"><code>@OneToMany</code> defaults to <code>LAZY</code>, so <code>findAll()</code> costs one query. Touching <code>product.getParts().size()</code> on each product triggers one more query per product. Total: <strong>1 + 50 = 51</strong>. This is the N+1 problem, and the fix is a fetch join — <code>@Query("select distinct p from Product p left join fetch p.parts")</code> — or an <code>@EntityGraph</code> on the repository method.</p><h4 style="margin:20px 0 6px; font-size:15.5px; font-weight:700;">2. What happens when a Part is loaded on its own?</h4><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;"><code>@ManyToOne</code> defaults to <strong><code>EAGER</code></strong>, which is the opposite of the <code>@OneToMany</code> default and catches almost everybody. Loading one part also loads its product, immediately, in the same or a joined query. Loading 200 parts can therefore mean 201 queries even though no relationship was touched in the code. Writing <code>@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)</code> is the standard correction.</p><h4 style="margin:20px 0 6px; font-size:15.5px; font-weight:700;">3. What does deleting a Product do?</h4><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;"><code>CascadeType.ALL</code> includes <code>REMOVE</code>, so deleting the product deletes all of its parts. <code>orphanRemoval = true</code> goes further: removing a part from the <code>parts</code> list — without deleting anything explicitly — deletes that part's row. That combination is right for a true parent-child ownership and dangerous for anything shared. In the inventory PA, parts can belong to several products, so parts must <em>not</em> be cascade-removed with a product.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Annotation</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Default fetch</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Usually correct</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>@OneToMany</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">LAZY</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">leave it LAZY</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>@ManyToOne</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>EAGER</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">change it to LAZY</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>@ManyToMany</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">LAZY</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">leave it LAZY</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>@OneToOne</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>EAGER</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">change it to LAZY</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: 51 queries; a Part eagerly drags its Product along; and <code>ALL</code> + <code>orphanRemoval</code> means the children die with the parent — deliberate for a composition, catastrophic for a shared entity.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Memorise the fetch defaults as a single sentence: <strong>the "to-one" ends are EAGER, the "to-many" ends are LAZY</strong> — which is backwards from what people expect, and is why <code>@ManyToOne(fetch = FetchType.LAZY)</code> appears in almost every real codebase. Then: <strong><code>mappedBy</code> marks the inverse side</strong> and always names a <em>field on the other class</em>; the side with <code>@JoinColumn</code> owns the foreign key and is the only side whose changes are persisted. <code>@ManyToMany</code> needs <code>@JoinTable</code> on one side and <code>mappedBy</code> on the other. If a stem describes "one query for the list, then one per row", the answer is the N+1 problem and the fix is a fetch join.</p></div><i data-d287cov_42="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">Entity inheritance — and the PA's Part hierarchy</h4><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div>JPA maps an inheritance hierarchy with <strong>@Inheritance</strong>, choosing one of three strategies: <strong>SINGLE_TABLE</strong> (all subclasses in one table, told apart by a discriminator column), <strong>JOINED</strong> (a shared table plus one per subclass), and <strong>TABLE_PER_CLASS</strong> (a separate complete table each).</div><p>This is not abstract for D287: the performance assessment has <strong>InHousePart and OutsourcedPart extending Part</strong>, which is precisely this situation. The usual answer is <strong>SINGLE_TABLE with a @DiscriminatorColumn</strong> — the default, the simplest, and fast because no join is needed.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">@Entity
@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)
@DiscriminatorColumn(name = "part_type")
public abstract class Part { … }

@Entity @DiscriminatorValue("INHOUSE")
public class InHousePart extends Part { private int partId; }

@Entity @DiscriminatorValue("OUTSOURCED")
public class OutsourcedPart extends Part { private String companyName; }</pre><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>SINGLE_TABLE's cost is that subclass-specific columns must be nullable, since rows of the other type leave them empty. That trade — nullable columns in exchange for no joins — is what the strategy questions are really about.</div>"},"D287/ch4/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch4-s3"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Spring Data JPA Repositories (queries without SQL)</h3><p>The magic of Spring Data: you write an <b>interface</b>, Spring writes the implementation.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a repository and what each call does</div><pre>public interface PartRepository extends CrudRepository&lt;Part, Long&gt; {
    List&lt;Part&gt; findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(String q);  // derived query!
}</pre>You NEVER implement this. Spring generates it, giving you free:<br>&bull; <code>repo.save(part)</code> — insert or update<br>&bull; <code>repo.findById(7L)</code> — returns <code>Optional&lt;Part&gt;</code><br>&bull; <code>repo.findAll()</code>, <code>repo.deleteById(7L)</code>, <code>repo.count()</code><br>And the <b>derived query</b>: the method NAME is parsed — findBy + Name + Containing + IgnoreCase → <code>WHERE lower(name) LIKE lower(&#39;%q%&#39;)</code>. Calling <code>findByNameContainingIgnoreCase("bolt")</code> finds &ldquo;Bolt&rdquo;, &ldquo;BOLT-2&rdquo;, &ldquo;carriage bolt.&rdquo;</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHAT TO KNOW</div>&bull; <b>CrudRepository&lt;T, ID&gt;</b> — basic CRUD. <b>JpaRepository</b> extends it (adds paging/flush).<br>&bull; Derived queries: findBy&lt;Field&gt;&lt;Op&gt; — GreaterThan, Between, OrderBy&hellip;<br>&bull; <b>@Query("...")</b> — write JPQL yourself when the name-parser isn&rsquo;t enough.<br>&bull; findById returns <b>Optional</b> — handle the missing case (orElseThrow) instead of getting null.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Who implements PartRepository extends CrudRepository?<ol type="A"><li>Spring Data generates the implementation at runtime</li><li>You must write a PartRepositoryImpl class</li><li>Hibernate requires XML mapping for it</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Declaring the interface is enough — Spring Data builds the working implementation, including queries derived from method names.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Spring Data JPA generates repository implementations from interface declarations. You write three lines; you get findById, save, delete, count, findAll, paging, sorting — the entire CRUD layer. Knowing how to use it (and what it generates) lets you skip thousands of lines of boilerplate.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Extend JpaRepository.</strong> interface PartRepository extends JpaRepository&lt;Part, Long. Two type parameters: the entity type and the primary-key type. Spring creates an implementation at startup. You inject the interface; the magic is invisible.</p>
<p><strong>2. Built-in CRUD methods.</strong> save(entity) — insert or update. findById(id) — Optional&lt;Part. findAll() — List&lt;Part. delete(entity), deleteById(id), count(), existsById(id). Plus paging and sorting variants. You get all of these from one interface declaration.</p>
<p><strong>3. Derived query methods.</strong> Method names parsed into queries: findByName(String name), findByPriceLessThan(BigDecimal price), findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(String fragment). The naming convention is a small DSL — verb + property + operator. Most CRUD apps don't need anything else.</p>
<p><strong>4. @Query for custom JPQL.</strong> When derived names get long or queries get complex, write JPQL: @Query("SELECT p FROM Part p WHERE p.inventory < :min"). JPQL operates on entities, not tables. Native SQL is also available: @Query(value = "...", nativeQuery = true).</p>
<p><strong>5. Pagination and sorting.</strong> PartRepository extends JpaRepository — findAll(Pageable pageable) returns Page&lt;Part. Pass PageRequest.of(0, 10, Sort.by("name")) and get the first 10 sorted by name. Pagination is built in; you don't write a single line.</p>
<p><strong>6. Custom query method derivation rules.</strong> Keywords: And, Or, Between, LessThan, GreaterThan, Like, In, IsNull, OrderBy. Method like findByNameAndInventoryGreaterThanOrderByPriceDesc parses unambiguously. Refer to the Spring Data documentation for the full grammar; you will reach for it often.</p>
<p><strong>7. When to break out into Service layer.</strong> Repository methods stay simple — single-purpose CRUD and queries. Put business logic (validating inventory limits, calculating totals, sending notifications) in @Service classes that use repositories. Keeping repositories thin keeps test mocks simple.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Long derived method names that are harder to read than custom JPQL — switch to @Query when names exceed ~5 keywords.</li>
 <li>Calling findAll() on huge tables and iterating in memory — use Pageable to fetch in chunks.</li>
 <li>Putting business logic in repositories — they should only know about persistence, not domain rules.</li>
 <li>Native SQL in @Query when JPQL would do — locks you to one database vendor.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Extend JpaRepository&lt;Entity, Id and get full CRUD for free. Derived query methods follow a simple naming DSL. @Query handles complex cases with JPQL or native SQL. Pagination and sorting are built in. Keep repositories thin; put business logic in services.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">JpaRepository</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Data interface providing CRUD, paging, and sorting for an entity type.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Derived query method</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Data convention parsing method names into queries (findByName, findByPriceLessThan).</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Query</span></dt>
 <dd>Custom JPQL or native-SQL query attached to a repository method.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Pageable</span></dt>
 <dd>Argument carrying page number, size, and sort; returns a Page&lt;Entity.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">JPQL</span></dt>
 <dd>Java Persistence Query Language — entity-aware query syntax similar to SQL but operating on entities.</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c4s3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — a derived query method name, taken apart token by token</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 440" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk195a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Spring Data parses the method NAME — this is the whole mechanism</text><text x="130" y="66" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">find</text><line x1="132" y1="74" x2="158.2" y2="74" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="2"/><text x="162.2" y="66" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">By</text><line x1="164.2" y1="74" x2="174.29999999999998" y2="74" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="2"/><text x="178.29999999999998" y="66" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Name</text><line x1="180.29999999999998" y1="74" x2="206.5" y2="74" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="2"/><text x="210.5" y="66" fill="#e8795a" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Containing</text><line x1="212.5" y1="74" x2="287" y2="74" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="2"/><text x="291" y="66" fill="#e8795a" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">IgnoreCase</text><line x1="293" y1="74" x2="367.5" y2="74" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="2"/><text x="371.5" y="66" fill="#10b981" font-size="13.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">OrderByPriceDesc</text><line x1="373.5" y1="74" x2="496.3" y2="74" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="2"/><text x="268" y="100" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">find</text><text x="282" y="100" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">→</text><text x="302" y="100" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the subject — return the matching rows</text><text x="268" y="121" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">By</text><text x="282" y="121" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">→</text><text x="302" y="121" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the criteria start here</text><text x="268" y="142" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Name</text><text x="282" y="142" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">→</text><text x="302" y="142" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a property declared on the Part entity</text><text x="268" y="163" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">Containing</text><text x="282" y="163" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">→</text><text x="302" y="163" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">LIKE %value%</text><text x="268" y="184" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">IgnoreCase</text><text x="282" y="184" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">→</text><text x="302" y="184" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">LOWER() applied to both sides</text><text x="268" y="205" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">OrderByPriceDesc</text><text x="282" y="205" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">→</text><text x="302" y="205" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">ORDER BY price DESC</text><line x1="310" y1="236" x2="310" y2="258" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk195a)"/><rect x="80" y="258" width="460" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="310" y="284" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"></text><text x="310" y="285" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">select p from Part p where lower(p.name) like lower(?) order by p.price desc</text><text x="552" y="285" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">JPQL</text><line x1="310" y1="302" x2="310" y2="324" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk195a)"/><rect x="80" y="324" width="460" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="310" y="350" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"></text><text x="310" y="351" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">SELECT * FROM parts WHERE LOWER(name) LIKE ? ORDER BY price DESC</text><text x="552" y="351" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">SQL</text><text x="340" y="396" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">You wrote an interface. Spring Data generated the class at startup — there is no implementation to write, and none to find.</text><text x="340" y="424" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">A property that does not exist fails at STARTUP, not at query time: "No property xyz found for type Part".</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — turning five requirements into repository methods</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Each line of the brief becomes one method. The only skill is knowing when the name stops being readable and a <code>@Query</code> takes over.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">public interface PartRepository extends JpaRepository&lt;Part, Long&gt; {

    // 1. "find a part by its exact name"
    Optional&lt;Part&gt; findByName(String name);

    // 2. "search parts whose name contains a fragment, case-insensitively"
    List&lt;Part&gt; findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(String fragment);

    // 3. "list parts that are below their minimum, cheapest first"
    List&lt;Part&gt; findByInventoryLessThanOrderByPriceAsc(int minimum);

    // 4. "count the in-house parts made on a given machine"
    long countByMachineId(int machineId);

    // 5. "parts under a price that are attached to no product at all"
    @Query("select p from Part p where p.price &lt; :max and p.products is empty")
    List&lt;Part&gt; findCheapOrphans(@Param("max") BigDecimal max);
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The return type is part of the contract.</strong> <code>Optional&lt;Part&gt;</code> for at most one result; <code>List&lt;Part&gt;</code> for many; <code>long</code> for a <code>count…</code> method; <code>boolean</code> for an <code>exists…</code> method. Declaring <code>Part findByName(...)</code> when two rows match throws <code>IncorrectResultSizeDataAccessException</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Keywords chain in a fixed grammar:</strong> subject (<code>find</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>exists</code>, <code>delete</code>), then <code>By</code>, then property + operator pairs joined by <code>And</code>/<code>Or</code>, then an optional <code>OrderBy…Asc|Desc</code>. The operators worth memorising are <code>Containing</code>, <code>StartingWith</code>, <code>Between</code>, <code>LessThan</code>, <code>GreaterThanEqual</code>, <code>In</code>, <code>IsNull</code>, <code>Not</code>, <code>IgnoreCase</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Parameter order is positional.</strong> The first parameter fills the first criterion. There are no names to get wrong, and no way to reorder them — which is why long derived names get fragile.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Method 5 is where derivation stops paying.</strong> "attached to no product" has no keyword, so JPQL is the honest answer. Note it is JPQL, not SQL: <code>Part</code> is the <em>entity</em> name and <code>p.products</code> is a <em>field</em>, so the query keeps working if the table or column is renamed.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Everything else is inherited.</strong> <code>save</code>, <code>findById</code>, <code>findAll</code>, <code>deleteById</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>existsById</code> and the <code>Pageable</code> variants all come from <code>JpaRepository</code> — no declaration needed.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Four derived methods and one <code>@Query</code>. Switch to <code>@Query</code> as soon as the method name needs more than about five keywords to say what it means.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — reading a startup failure caused by one wrong letter</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The application will not start. The stack trace ends with:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">org.springframework.data.mapping.PropertyReferenceException:
    No property 'inventoryCount' found for type 'Part'
    Did you mean: inventory?
  at ... createQuery(PartRepository.findByInventoryCountLessThan)</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Read where it happened: startup, not a request.</strong> Spring Data builds every derived query when it creates the repository proxy, so a name it cannot parse is a context-initialisation failure. This is a feature — the alternative is finding out in production.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Read what it tried to parse.</strong> The method is <code>findByInventoryCountLessThan</code>. Stripping <code>findBy</code> and the <code>LessThan</code> operator leaves the property <code>inventoryCount</code>, which the <code>Part</code> entity does not declare.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Read the suggestion.</strong> "Did you mean: inventory?" — Spring Data compares against the entity's actual properties and offers the nearest. The fix is to rename the method to <code>findByInventoryLessThan</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Know the other two shapes of this error.</strong> Renaming an entity field without renaming the repository methods that mention it produces exactly this message. And a genuinely ambiguous name — a property <code>addressZip</code> alongside a nested <code>address.zip</code> — is resolved by writing an underscore where the traversal happens: <code>findByAddress_Zip</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Note what this buys you.</strong> Because the method names are validated against the entity at startup, a typo in a query cannot reach a user. A hand-written JDBC string has no such protection.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Rename the method to match the property. The exception names the offending method, the missing property, and the property you probably meant — all three are in the message.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Two guaranteed question shapes. <strong>"What does this method name generate?"</strong> — decode left to right: subject, <code>By</code>, property, operator, <code>OrderBy</code>. <code>findByPriceBetween</code> takes two arguments; <code>findByNameContaining</code> is a LIKE with wildcards on both sides; <code>IgnoreCase</code> lower-cases both sides. <strong>"What do you get for free from JpaRepository?"</strong> — <code>save</code>, <code>saveAll</code>, <code>findById</code>, <code>findAll</code>, <code>deleteById</code>, <code>delete</code>, <code>count</code>, <code>existsById</code>, plus paging and sorting. And remember that you never write an implementation class: Spring Data creates the proxy at startup, which is also when a bad property name blows up.</p></div><i data-d287cov_43="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">Modifying queries, and where a parameter comes from</h4><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>@Modifying</strong> must be added to a <code>@Query</code> that performs an <strong>UPDATE or DELETE</strong>. Without it Spring Data runs the statement as a query expecting a result set, and it fails. It is normally paired with <code>@Transactional</code>, since a write needs a transaction.</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">@Modifying
@Transactional
@Query("UPDATE Part p SET p.inv = :inv WHERE p.id = :id")
int updateInventory(@Param("id") Long id, @Param("inv") int inv);</pre><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Annotation</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Reads from</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">URL it matches</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><strong>@RequestParam</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the <strong>query string</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>/search<strong>?term=spring</strong></code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><strong>@PathVariable</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a placeholder <strong>in the path</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>/parts/<strong>42</strong></code> with <code>@GetMapping("/parts/{id}")</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>So for <code>/search?term=spring</code>, <code>term</code> is after the question mark and is read with <strong>@RequestParam</strong>.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">@GetMapping("/search")
String search(@RequestParam String term) { … }

@GetMapping("/parts/{id}")
String one(@PathVariable Long id) { … }</pre>"},"D287/ch4/s4":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch4-s4"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — H2 In-Memory Database &amp; Seeding Data</h3><p><b>H2</b> is a tiny database that runs INSIDE your app&rsquo;s memory — perfect for development: zero install, fresh on every restart.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — configure H2 and seed it at startup</div>application.properties:<br><pre>spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:partsdb
spring.h2.console.enabled=true      ← browse the DB at /h2-console
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop</pre>Seed rows with a <b>CommandLineRunner</b> — a bean whose run() executes ONCE after the app starts:<br><pre>@Bean
CommandLineRunner seed(PartRepository repo) {
    return args -&gt; {
        if (repo.count() == 0) {          // guard: don&#39;t double-seed
            repo.save(new Part("Bolt", 0.25));
            repo.save(new Part("Nut", 0.10));
        }
    };
}</pre>Start the app → tables are created (ddl-auto) → seed runs → /parts shows Bolt and Nut. Restart → memory wiped → seeded fresh again.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ IN-MEMORY MEANS GONE ON RESTART</div>H2 (jdbc:h2:<b>mem</b>:...) loses everything when the app stops — that&rsquo;s a feature in dev and a bug in production. The PA expects sample data to appear via a seeder (CommandLineRunner or data.sql), with a count()==0 guard so re-runs don&rsquo;t duplicate rows.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>A CommandLineRunner bean&rsquo;s run() executes:<ol type="A"><li>Once, right after the application starts</li><li>On every web request</li><li>Only during tests</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> It&rsquo;s the standard hook for one-time startup work like seeding sample data.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>H2 is the database that ships with Spring Boot's default starter. It runs in-memory, requires no setup, and resets every time the app restarts — perfect for development and the D287 PA. Knowing how to configure it, browse it, and seed it makes prototyping fast.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. What H2 provides.</strong> A full Java database engine. Speaks SQL. Can run in-memory (mode mem), file-based (mode file), or as a separate server. For development, in-memory mode is the default — no installation, no persistence, no contention.</p>
<p><strong>2. Configuring H2 in application.properties.</strong> spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb. spring.datasource.driverClassName=org.h2.Driver. spring.datasource.username=sa. spring.datasource.password= (often blank). spring.jpa.database-platform=org.hibernate.dialect.H2Dialect. Spring Boot auto-configures most of this if h2 is on the classpath.</p>
<p><strong>3. The H2 console.</strong> Set spring.h2.console.enabled=true. Browse to http://localhost:8080/h2-console at runtime. Connect with the JDBC URL from your config (e.g., jdbc:h2:mem:testdb). Run SQL queries against your live application's database — invaluable for debugging.</p>
<p><strong>4. spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto.</strong> Controls schema management. NONE: do nothing. VALIDATE: confirm entities match schema. UPDATE: incrementally update schema. CREATE: drop and recreate at startup. CREATE-DROP: create at startup, drop at shutdown. For PA development, UPDATE or CREATE-DROP is typical. NEVER use UPDATE in production.</p>
<p><strong>5. Initialize data with data.sql.</strong> Place a data.sql file in src/main/resources. Spring Boot runs it after schema initialization. Useful for seeding sample parts, products, users. Schema initialization (schema.sql) runs first if present.</p>
<p><strong>6. Migrating to a real database.</strong> When the PA is over and you want to use Postgres or MySQL, change the JDBC URL, driver, and dialect; provide credentials; ensure the schema exists. Most code does not change because JPA is the abstraction layer.</p>
<p><strong>7. When H2's in-memory model bites.</strong> Data is gone on restart. Connections from the H2 console show the SAME database your app is using ONLY when both share the URL and the app is running. Two separate JVMs can't share an in-memory H2 — for that, you need server mode or a real database.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Connecting the H2 console with a different URL than the app uses — you see an empty database and panic.</li>
 <li>ddl-auto = UPDATE in production — silent schema drift; data loss is a matter of when, not if.</li>
 <li>Expecting in-memory data to survive restart — the PA grader will restart your app and lose your seed data; use data.sql.</li>
 <li>Hard-coding H2 in code — keep the dialect and driver in application.properties so production swap is config-only.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>H2 is Spring Boot's default development database — in-memory, zero-setup, perfect for the PA. Configure URL, driver, dialect in application.properties. Enable the H2 console for live SQL access. Use data.sql to seed; ddl-auto manages schema. Migrate to a real database by changing config, not code.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">H2</span></dt>
 <dd>Java in-memory / file-based database, default in Spring Boot's starter.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">JDBC URL</span></dt>
 <dd>Connection string identifying the database (jdbc:h2:mem:testdb).</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">H2 console</span></dt>
 <dd>Web UI for running SQL against H2, served at /h2-console when enabled.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">ddl-auto</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Boot setting controlling schema management on startup.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">data.sql</span></dt>
 <dd>Resource file Spring Boot runs after schema init to seed initial data.</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c4s4="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — where an in-memory H2 database actually lives, and what runs before your first request</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 406" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk196a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">in-memory means inside this JVM's heap — that single fact explains every H2 surprise</text><rect x="20" y="42" width="400" height="210" rx="10" fill="#0ea5e9" opacity="0.10" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="220" y="62" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">one JVM — java -jar inventory.jar</text><rect x="40" y="76" width="168" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="124" y="98" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">your Spring app</text><text x="124" y="113" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">repositories, services</text><rect x="40" y="140" width="168" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="124" y="162" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">/h2-console</text><text x="124" y="177" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a page the app serves</text><rect x="248" y="100" width="152" height="76" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="324" y="126" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">H2 engine</text><text x="324" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">jdbc:h2:mem:</text><text x="324" y="164" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">testdb</text><line x1="208" y1="99" x2="246" y2="122" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk196a)"/><line x1="208" y1="163" x2="246" y2="152" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#fk196a)"/><text x="220" y="226" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">same process, same heap, same rows —</text><text x="220" y="240" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the console shows exactly what the app sees</text><rect x="452" y="42" width="208" height="210" rx="10" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="556" y="62" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">any other process</text><rect x="474" y="86" width="164" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="556" y="112" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">a second JVM</text><line x1="556" y1="130" x2="556" y2="156" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" stroke-dasharray="4 3" marker-end="url(#fk196a)"/><rect x="474" y="156" width="164" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="556" y="182" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">its own empty testdb</text><text x="556" y="224" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">same URL, different heap.</text><text x="556" y="238" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Nothing is shared. Nothing is wrong.</text><text x="340" y="284" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">what happens at startup, in this order</text><line x1="56" y1="322" x2="624" y2="322" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.8" marker-end="url(#fk196a)"/><line x1="90" y1="316" x2="90" y2="328" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="90" cy="302" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="90" y="306" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text x="90" y="344" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ddl-auto</text><text x="90" y="360" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">create the schema from the entities</text><line x1="252" y1="316" x2="252" y2="328" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="252" cy="302" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="252" y="306" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text x="252" y="344" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">schema.sql</text><text x="252" y="360" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">if present, run it</text><line x1="410" y1="316" x2="410" y2="328" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="410" cy="302" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="410" y="306" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="410" y="344" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">data.sql</text><text x="410" y="360" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">seed the rows</text><line x1="560" y1="316" x2="560" y2="328" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6"/><circle cx="560" cy="302" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="560" y="306" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="560" y="344" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">ready</text><text x="560" y="360" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">Tomcat accepts requests</text><text x="340" y="390" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">On shutdown the heap is freed and the database ceases to exist. That is why data.sql, not manual inserts, is how you seed.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — configuring H2 and the console for the inventory application</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Six properties. Each one exists for a reason worth knowing, because the exam asks about the reasons rather than the syntax.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;"># --- the database ---------------------------------------------------
spring.datasource.url=jdbc:h2:mem:testdb
spring.datasource.driverClassName=org.h2.Driver
spring.datasource.username=sa
spring.datasource.password=

# --- the browser console ---------------------------------------------
spring.h2.console.enabled=true

# --- schema management ------------------------------------------------
spring.jpa.hibernate.ddl-auto=create-drop
spring.jpa.show-sql=true</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>jdbc:h2:mem:testdb</code></strong> reads as: the H2 driver, <code>mem</code> for in-memory (as opposed to <code>file:</code> or <code>tcp:</code>), and <code>testdb</code> as the database's name inside this JVM. Change <code>mem</code> to <code>file:./data/inventory</code> and the same application survives restarts.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Spring Boot can infer nearly all of this.</strong> With only the H2 jar on the classpath and no <code>spring.datasource.*</code> properties at all, Boot auto-configures an in-memory database with a random name. Writing the URL explicitly is what lets the console connect to a name you know.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The console is served by your application</strong>, at <code>http://localhost:8080/h2-console</code>, only while it is running. Its JDBC URL field defaults to <code>jdbc:h2:~/test</code> — a <em>file</em> database in your home directory. Leaving that default is the single most common H2 confusion: you connect successfully to a completely different, empty database.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>ddl-auto</code> has five values</strong> — <code>none</code>, <code>validate</code>, <code>update</code>, <code>create</code>, <code>create-drop</code>. In-memory development wants <code>create-drop</code> (fresh schema each run, dropped at shutdown) or <code>update</code>. Production wants <code>validate</code> or <code>none</code> with a migration tool, because <code>update</code> never drops or narrows anything and silently accumulates drift.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>show-sql=true</code> prints the generated SQL</strong> to the console. Turn it on the moment a query surprises you — it is how you see the N+1 problem happening rather than deducing it.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: URL, driver, credentials, console flag, <code>ddl-auto</code>. For an in-memory PA project the safe pair is <code>create-drop</code> plus a <code>data.sql</code> seed file.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — why the H2 console shows an empty database</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The application is running, parts have been added through the UI, and the console shows the tables with zero rows — or no tables at all. Diagnose it in order; only the first cause is common.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Cause</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Symptom in the console</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Connected to the default URL <code>jdbc:h2:~/test</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Connects fine, but there are no tables at all</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Type the URL from <code>application.properties</code>: <code>jdbc:h2:mem:testdb</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">The app was restarted since the data was entered</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Tables exist, all empty</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Expected. In-memory data does not survive a restart — seed with <code>data.sql</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>ddl-auto=create-drop</code> and you are looking after shutdown</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Cannot connect at all</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">The database only exists while the app runs</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">A second JVM (a test run) holds its own <code>testdb</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Tables exist, wrong rows</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">Two JVMs never share an in-memory database — use <code>file:</code> mode or a real server</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Check the URL field first.</strong> The console remembers its last-used connection, and its factory default points at a file database in your home directory. It will connect happily and show you nothing, because there genuinely is nothing there.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Confirm from the application log.</strong> On startup Spring Boot logs the datasource it built. Whatever URL appears there is the one the console must be given, character for character.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Understand why sharing is impossible.</strong> "In-memory" is the JVM's heap. Another process cannot read another process's heap, so the same URL in a different JVM creates a new, empty database rather than joining the existing one.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Seed with <code>data.sql</code>, not by hand.</strong> Put <code>INSERT</code> statements in <code>src/main/resources/data.sql</code> and they run on every startup, after the schema is created. A grader who restarts your application then sees the sample parts you intended them to see.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Nine times in ten the console is connected to <code>jdbc:h2:~/test</code> instead of <code>jdbc:h2:mem:testdb</code>. The database is not empty — you are looking at a different one.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Learn the five <code>ddl-auto</code> values and their one-line meanings: <code>none</code>, <code>validate</code> (check only), <code>update</code> (add, never remove), <code>create</code> (drop and rebuild at startup), <code>create-drop</code> (rebuild at startup, drop at shutdown). Development uses <code>update</code> or <code>create-drop</code>; production never uses <code>update</code>. Then two facts that questions love: <strong>the console is enabled with <code>spring.h2.console.enabled=true</code> and lives at <code>/h2-console</code></strong>, and <strong>the console must be given the same JDBC URL the application uses</strong>. Finally, <code>data.sql</code> seeds rows and runs after schema creation; <code>schema.sql</code> runs before it.</p></div><i data-d287cov_44="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">H2, and reading a JSON request body</h4><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>H2</strong> in a Boot project is used as a <strong>fast in-memory database for development and testing</strong>. It starts with the application, needs no install, and — being in memory — is empty again on every restart, which is exactly what you want for repeatable tests and exactly what you do not want in production.</div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>@RequestBody</strong> on a method parameter tells Spring to <strong>deserialise the JSON request body into that object</strong>, using Jackson. It is the mirror image of <code>@ResponseBody</code>, which serialises the return value back to JSON.</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">@PostMapping("/api/parts")
Part create(@RequestBody Part part) { return service.save(part); }</pre><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Keep the three straight by <em>where the data is</em>: query string → <code>@RequestParam</code>; inside the path → <code>@PathVariable</code>; in the body → <code>@RequestBody</code>.</div>"},"D287/ch5/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch5-s1"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Bean Validation (@NotNull, @Min, @Valid)</h3><p>Validation rules live as annotations ON the entity/form object; Spring checks them when a form binds.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — validate a Part form, end to end</div><pre>public class Part {
    @NotBlank(message = "Name is required")
    private String name;

    @Min(value = 0, message = "Price cannot be negative")
    private double price;
}

@PostMapping("/parts")
public String save(@Valid Part part, BindingResult result) {
    if (result.hasErrors()) return "partForm";  // redisplay with messages
    repo.save(part);
    return "redirect:/parts";
}</pre>Submit an empty name and price −5: binding runs the rules, <b>BindingResult</b> collects both failures, hasErrors() is true, and the form re-renders. Thymeleaf shows messages with <code>th:errors="*{name}"</code>. Nothing invalid ever reaches the database.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE RULES YOU&rsquo;LL USE</div>&bull; <b>@NotNull</b> — must exist. <b>@NotBlank</b> — string with real content. <b>@NotEmpty</b> — not empty (string/collection).<br>&bull; <b>@Min / @Max</b> — numeric bounds (the PA&rsquo;s min/max inventory checks!).<br>&bull; <b>@Size(min, max)</b> — length bounds. <b>@Email</b>, <b>@Pattern</b> — formats.<br>&bull; <b>@Valid</b> on the controller parameter triggers the checks; <b>BindingResult must come immediately after it</b> in the parameter list.</div><div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ ORDER OF PARAMETERS</div><code>save(@Valid Part part, BindingResult result)</code> works. Put another parameter between them and Spring throws instead of collecting errors. BindingResult directly follows the @Valid object — memorize that pairing.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Which annotation rejects a submitted price of −5 (must be ≥ 0)?<ol type="A"><li>@Min(0)</li><li>@NotBlank</li><li>@Size(0)</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — @Min(0).</b> @NotBlank is for strings; @Size bounds lengths, not values.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Validation prevents bad data from reaching your business logic and your database. Bean Validation (Jakarta Validation) provides annotation-driven rules that run automatically. Used well, your controller methods stay clean and your error pages get user-friendly messages for free.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Annotations on entity / DTO fields.</strong> @NotNull (must not be null). @NotBlank (string must contain non-whitespace). @Size(min, max) (string or collection length). @Min, @Max (numeric bounds). @Email (valid email pattern). @Pattern (regex). Apply directly to entity fields and they become contracts.</p>
<p><strong>2. @Valid triggers validation.</strong> @PostMapping("/parts") public String save(@Valid @ModelAttribute Part part, BindingResult result). Without @Valid, the annotations are decoration only — Spring won't check them. With @Valid, validation runs and errors land in BindingResult.</p>
<p><strong>3. BindingResult must follow the validated parameter.</strong> The order matters: @Valid Part part, BindingResult result. If anything sits between them, validation errors throw ConstraintViolationException instead of populating BindingResult. Top-five Spring MVC mistake; check this first when debugging.</p>
<p><strong>4. Custom error messages.</strong> @NotBlank(message = "Part name is required"). @Min(value = 1, message = "Inventory must be at least {value}"). Messages can be hard-coded or pulled from message bundles (messages.properties) for i18n. Defaults are usually too generic to show users.</p>
<p><strong>5. Custom validators.</strong> When built-in annotations aren't enough (e.g., 'inventory must be between min and max'), define a custom annotation + ConstraintValidator. The PA's typical inventory rule (min ≤ inventory ≤ max) is a class-level validation — applied to the whole Part, not a single field.</p>
<p><strong>6. Class-level validation.</strong> @ValidPart on the class, with a ConstraintValidator that checks cross-field rules. Different from field-level annotations. Reach for class-level when the rule depends on multiple fields together.</p>
<p><strong>7. Showing errors in Thymeleaf.</strong> &lt;div th:if="${#fields.hasErrors('name')}" th:errors="*{name}"&gt;. Or &lt;ul th:each="err : ${#fields.errors('*')}"&gt;&lt;li th:text="${err}"&gt;. Combine with controller logic that re-renders the form view when result.hasErrors() — never redirect with errors, you'll lose the user's input.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Forgetting @Valid — validation annotations sit unused.</li>
 <li>BindingResult parameter not immediately after the validated parameter — exceptions instead of error collection.</li>
 <li>Redirecting on validation failure — user-typed values vanish.</li>
 <li>Generic default messages — 'must not be blank' is less useful than 'Part name is required.'</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Annotate fields with @NotNull, @NotBlank, @Size, @Min, @Max, @Email, @Pattern. Trigger validation with @Valid in the controller. Position BindingResult immediately after the validated parameter. Customize messages. Define custom validators for cross-field rules. Re-render the form (don't redirect) on errors so user input is preserved.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Bean Validation (JSR-380)</span></dt>
 <dd>Java standard for annotation-driven validation.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@Valid</span></dt>
 <dd>Triggers validation of the annotated parameter; without it, validation annotations are inert.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">BindingResult</span></dt>
 <dd>Holds binding and validation errors; must immediately follow the validated parameter.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">ConstraintValidator</span></dt>
 <dd>Interface implemented for custom validation logic.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Class-level constraint</span></dt>
 <dd>Validation annotation on the class to check rules involving multiple fields.</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c5s1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — where validation happens in the request, and what switches it on</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 474" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk197a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">validation is a stage in the binding pipeline, not something the entity does by itself</text><rect x="200" y="44" width="280" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="340" y="66" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">POST /parts</text><text x="340" y="81" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">name=&price=-3.00&inventory=5</text><line x1="340" y1="90" x2="340" y2="118" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk197a)"/><rect x="200" y="118" width="280" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="340" y="140" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">WebDataBinder</text><text x="340" y="155" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">strings → typed properties</text><text x="496" y="134" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">a letter in a number field</text><text x="496" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">becomes an error here</text><line x1="340" y1="164" x2="340" y2="192" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk197a)"/><rect x="200" y="192" width="280" height="52" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="340" y="217" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the Validator runs</text><text x="340" y="232" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">triggered only by @Valid</text><rect x="20" y="178" width="160" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="100" y="202" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@NotBlank on name</text><rect x="20" y="224" width="160" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="100" y="248" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@Min(0) on price</text><line x1="180" y1="198" x2="198" y2="210" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk197a)"/><line x1="180" y1="244" x2="198" y2="232" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk197a)"/><rect x="500" y="200" width="160" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="580" y="224" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@ValidPart on the class</text><line x1="498" y1="220" x2="482" y2="220" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk197a)"/><text x="100" y="284" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">field constraints — one property each</text><text x="580" y="258" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">class constraint —</text><text x="580" y="272" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">sees every field at once</text><line x1="340" y1="244" x2="340" y2="272" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk197a)"/><rect x="200" y="272" width="280" height="62" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="340" y="292" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">BindingResult</text><text x="340" y="310" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">name: must not be blank</text><text x="340" y="326" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">price: must be at least 0</text><line x1="340" y1="334" x2="340" y2="358" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk197a)"/><text x="354" y="352" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">result.hasErrors()</text><rect x="40" y="380" width="280" height="54" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="180" y="406" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">true → return "parts/form"</text><text x="180" y="421" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">th:errors prints the messages</text><rect x="360" y="380" width="280" height="54" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="500" y="406" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">false → save and redirect</text><text x="500" y="421" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the happy path, unchanged</text><line x1="336" y1="360" x2="200" y2="376" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk197a)"/><line x1="344" y1="360" x2="480" y2="376" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk197a)"/><text x="340" y="458" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Remove @Valid and the pipeline still runs — it simply skips the Validator, and every annotation becomes decoration.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — annotating a Part entity against a written rule set</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The rules, as a grader would state them: the name is required and at most 100 characters; the price must be greater than zero; inventory, minimum and maximum must all be zero or more; and inventory must lie between minimum and maximum.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Entity
@ValidPart                                    // class-level: the cross-field rule
public class Part {

    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;

    @NotBlank(message = "Part name is required")
    @Size(max = 100, message = "Part name cannot exceed 100 characters")
    private String name;

    @NotNull(message = "Price is required")
    @DecimalMin(value = "0.01", message = "Price must be greater than zero")
    private BigDecimal price;

    @Min(value = 0, message = "Inventory cannot be negative")
    private int inventory;

    @Min(value = 0, message = "Minimum cannot be negative")
    private int min;

    @Min(value = 0, message = "Maximum cannot be negative")
    private int max;
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>@NotBlank</code>, not <code>@NotNull</code>, for the name.</strong> <code>@NotNull</code> accepts <code>""</code> and <code>"   "</code>; <code>@NotBlank</code> requires at least one non-whitespace character. An empty text input posts <code>""</code>, never null, so <code>@NotNull</code> on a String field almost never does what the rule intended. (<code>@NotEmpty</code> sits between them: it rejects <code>""</code> but accepts <code>"   "</code>.)</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>@DecimalMin("0.01")</code> for a <code>BigDecimal</code>.</strong> <code>@Min</code> is for integral types; <code>@Positive</code> would also work here and reads better, but "greater than zero" for money is most precisely one cent. Note the value is a <em>String</em> — that is how the annotation avoids floating-point representation error.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The cross-field rule cannot be a field annotation.</strong> No annotation on <code>inventory</code> can see <code>min</code> and <code>max</code>, because a field constraint receives only that field's value. It must be a class-level constraint, which receives the whole object.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Write the message the rubric asks for, verbatim.</strong> The default for <code>@NotBlank</code> is "must not be blank" — technically accurate and worth no marks if the specification says "Part name is required".</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Nothing here runs without <code>@Valid</code> in the controller.</strong> Annotations declare constraints; <code>@Valid</code> on the handler parameter is what asks for them to be evaluated.</li></ol><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The class-level constraint itself is two small files:</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Target(ElementType.TYPE)                       // on the class, not a field
@Retention(RetentionPolicy.RUNTIME)
@Constraint(validatedBy = PartInventoryValidator.class)
public @interface ValidPart {
    String message() default "Inventory must be between the minimum and the maximum";
    Class&lt;?&gt;[] groups() default {};
    Class&lt;? extends Payload&gt;[] payload() default {};
}

public class PartInventoryValidator implements ConstraintValidator&lt;ValidPart, Part&gt; {
    @Override
    public boolean isValid(Part part, ConstraintValidatorContext context) {
        if (part == null) return true;                     // @NotNull's job, not ours
        return part.getInventory() &gt;= part.getMin()
            &amp;&amp; part.getInventory() &lt;= part.getMax();
    }
}</code></pre><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Field annotations for single-field rules; one class-level constraint for the min/max rule; custom messages matching the specification word for word.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — finding out why validation is being ignored</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">A part with a blank name saves successfully. The annotations are present and correctly spelled. Check the four things that switch validation off, in the order they are likely.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Cause</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">What you see</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">The fix</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>@Valid</code> missing on the parameter</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">blank names save, no errors anywhere</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">add <code>@Valid</code> before <code>@ModelAttribute</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>spring-boot-starter-validation</code> not in <code>pom.xml</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">nothing validates, anywhere in the app</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">add the starter; since Boot 2.3 it is no longer bundled with <code>starter-web</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>BindingResult</code> not immediately after the object</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a <code>MethodArgumentNotValidException</code> instead of a re-rendered form</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">move it so nothing sits between them</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>result.hasErrors()</code> never checked</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">errors are collected, then the save runs anyway</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">return the form view when <code>hasErrors()</code> is true</td></tr></tbody></table></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">// Broken four different ways at once
@PostMapping("/parts")
public String save(@ModelAttribute Part part,     // 1. no @Valid
                   Model model,
                   BindingResult result) {        // 3. Model is in the way
    service.save(part);                            // 4. errors never checked
    return "redirect:/parts";
}

// Correct
@PostMapping("/parts")
public String save(@Valid @ModelAttribute Part part,
                   BindingResult result,
                   Model model) {
    if (result.hasErrors()) {
        return "parts/form";
    }
    service.save(part);
    return "redirect:/parts";
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The parameter order rule is mechanical.</strong> Spring pairs each validated object with the <code>Errors</code>/<code>BindingResult</code> parameter that <em>immediately</em> follows it. With <code>Model</code> in between there is no pairing, so the framework has nowhere to put the violations and throws instead.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The missing-starter case looks identical to the missing-<code>@Valid</code> case</strong>, which is why it is worth checking <code>pom.xml</code> early: if <em>no</em> validation anywhere in the application fires, suspect the dependency, not the annotation.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Collected errors are not enforced errors.</strong> <code>BindingResult</code> is a report; nothing acts on it. The <code>if (result.hasErrors())</code> line is what turns a report into a rejection.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Then display them.</strong> In the template, <code>&lt;span th:if="${#fields.hasErrors('name')}" th:errors="*{name}"&gt;&lt;/span&gt;</code> prints the message attached to that field, and <code>th:errors="*{*}"</code> prints all of them including the class-level one.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Four independent switches, any one of which silently disables validation. <code>@Valid</code> is the first to check, the starter dependency the second.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The examinable pairs: <strong><code>@NotNull</code> vs <code>@NotEmpty</code> vs <code>@NotBlank</code></strong> — null-only, null-or-empty, null-or-empty-or-whitespace, in increasing strictness; <code>@NotBlank</code> is the right one for a required text field. <strong><code>@Valid</code> triggers, <code>BindingResult</code> collects</strong>, and the two must be adjacent parameters. <strong>Field-level vs class-level</strong>: any rule mentioning two fields (min ≤ inventory ≤ max) needs a class-level constraint with a <code>ConstraintValidator</code>. And in the view, <code>th:errors="*{field}"</code> prints the message.</p></div><i data-d287cov_51="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div>Returning <strong>ResponseEntity&lt;T&gt;</strong> from a controller method lets you <strong>control the status code, the headers and the body</strong> of the response, instead of always getting 200 OK with the object as the body.</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">@GetMapping("/api/parts/{id}")
ResponseEntity<Part> one(@PathVariable Long id) {
    return service.find(id)
        .map(ResponseEntity::ok)                        // 200 + body
        .orElseGet(() -> ResponseEntity.notFound().build()); // 404, no body
}</pre><p>Returning a bare <code>Part</code> could not express "not found" — it would send 200 with an empty body. That is the reason to reach for ResponseEntity.</p>"},"D287/ch5/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch5-s2"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Exception Handling in Spring</h3><p>When code throws, you don&rsquo;t want users seeing a stack trace. Spring routes exceptions to handler methods.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a service that throws, a handler that catches</div><pre>// service: enforce a business rule
public void buy(Long id) {
    Part p = repo.findById(id)
        .orElseThrow(() -&gt; new IllegalArgumentException("No part " + id));
    if (p.getInventory() &lt;= p.getMinInventory())
        throw new IllegalStateException("Inventory at minimum");
    p.setInventory(p.getInventory() - 1);
}

// global handler: one place for the whole app
@ControllerAdvice
public class GlobalErrors {
    @ExceptionHandler(IllegalArgumentException.class)
    public String badRequest(IllegalArgumentException ex, Model m) {
        m.addAttribute("msg", ex.getMessage());
        return "error";                 // friendly error page
    }
}</pre>buy(999) throws IllegalArgumentException → Spring finds the matching @ExceptionHandler in the @ControllerAdvice class → the user sees the error page with &ldquo;No part 999,&rdquo; not a stack trace.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">BEST PRACTICES (the exam list)</div>&bull; <b>@ControllerAdvice + @ExceptionHandler</b> = global, centralized handling — preferred over try/catch scattered through controllers.<br>&bull; Throw <b>specific</b> exceptions (IllegalArgumentException for bad input, custom NotFoundException) — never catch bare Exception and continue silently.<br>&bull; Show users a friendly message; <b>log</b> the technical detail.<br>&bull; Validate early so most bad input never becomes an exception.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>A class that handles exceptions for ALL controllers in one place is annotated:<ol type="A"><li>@ControllerAdvice</li><li>@Repository</li><li>@Entity</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A — @ControllerAdvice</b>, with @ExceptionHandler methods inside it for each exception type.</div></details></div></div><div class="vl" data-defglance="1"><div class="vl-box vl-rule def-glance"><div class="t">📖 DEFINITIONS AT A GLANCE — every key term in this section</div></div><table><tr><th style="width:32%">Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr><tr><td><b>checked</b></td><td>exceptions (IOException) must be handled or declared; unchecked (RuntimeException family) surface at runtime — NullPointerException happens when you call a method on a null reference .</td></tr><tr><td><b>unchecked</b></td><td>(RuntimeException family) surface at runtime — NullPointerException happens when you call a method on a null reference .</td></tr><tr><td><b>try-with-resources</b></td><td>auto-closes anything AutoCloseable (files, connections) even on exceptions — no finally boilerplate.</td></tr></table><div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-3);margin:-6px 0 14px">Full lesson with examples below ⬇️ — nothing removed.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Exceptions happen — entities not found, validation failures, database constraints, third-party services down. Without explicit handling, users see a stack trace. With Spring's @ExceptionHandler and @ControllerAdvice, you turn each exception into a clean, useful response.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. @ExceptionHandler in a controller.</strong> @ExceptionHandler(PartNotFoundException.class) public String handle(PartNotFoundException e, Model model). Catches the exception when thrown by any handler method in the same controller. Return a view name to render an error page; or return a model + view object.</p>
<p><strong>2. @ControllerAdvice for global handling.</strong> An @ControllerAdvice class collects @ExceptionHandlers that apply across all controllers. Avoids repeating handlers in each. Useful for cross-cutting concerns: 'every NoSuchElementException becomes a 404 page,' 'every uncaught Exception becomes a generic error page with logging.'</p>
<p><strong>3. @ResponseStatus on exception classes.</strong> @ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND) on a custom PartNotFoundException class. When the exception propagates uncaught, Spring sets that HTTP status. Combines well with @ExceptionHandler — handler renders the body; @ResponseStatus sets the code.</p>
<p><strong>4. Standard error pages.</strong> Spring Boot's default error handler routes to /error with attributes for status, message, exception. Override by adding templates/error.html (catches all errors) or templates/error/404.html (catches 404 specifically). Lowest-effort path to a branded error page.</p>
<p><strong>5. Logging exceptions.</strong> In an @ExceptionHandler, log at appropriate severity: WARN for client errors (validation, not found), ERROR for server errors (unexpected). Include enough context (URL, user, IDs) to debug. NEVER log secrets.</p>
<p><strong>6. Don't swallow exceptions silently.</strong> catch (Exception e) { } is one of the most damaging patterns. The application appears to work; data quietly corrupts; debugging takes weeks. If you catch, log; if you can't handle, rethrow.</p>
<p><strong>7. REST APIs return JSON errors.</strong> For @RestController APIs, exception handlers return ResponseEntity&lt;ErrorResponse with structured JSON. Standardize the error format across endpoints — code, message, timestamp, path. Clients can then handle errors uniformly.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>@ExceptionHandler in only some controllers — inconsistent handling across the app. Use @ControllerAdvice for cross-cutting handlers.</li>
 <li>Catching and swallowing without logging — silent failures multiply over time.</li>
 <li>Returning the raw exception message to users — sometimes leaks implementation details (SQL fragments, file paths).</li>
 <li>Forgetting @ResponseStatus on custom exceptions — they propagate as 500 by default.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Map exceptions to user-friendly responses with @ExceptionHandler (per-controller) or @ControllerAdvice (global). @ResponseStatus on exception classes sets the HTTP code. Override templates/error.html for default branding. Log at appropriate severity. Don't swallow exceptions silently. APIs return structured JSON errors.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ExceptionHandler</span></dt>
 <dd>Method-level annotation routing exceptions to a handler in the same controller.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ControllerAdvice</span></dt>
 <dd>Class-level annotation collecting global handlers (and other cross-cutting concerns).</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ResponseStatus</span></dt>
 <dd>Sets the HTTP status code returned when an annotated exception propagates.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">error.html template</span></dt>
 <dd>Default Spring Boot error view, customizable for branded error pages.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Exception swallowing</span></dt>
 <dd>Anti-pattern of catching exceptions without logging or rethrowing.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Exception fluency: <strong>checked</strong> exceptions (IOException) must be handled or declared; <strong>unchecked</strong> (RuntimeException family) surface at runtime — <strong>NullPointerException happens when you call a method on a null reference</strong>. <strong>try-with-resources</strong> auto-closes anything AutoCloseable (files, connections) even on exceptions — no finally boilerplate.</p><i data-tb287c5s2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the four places Spring looks for a handler, in the order it looks</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 466" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk198a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">an uncaught exception climbs the stack and is offered to four handlers in this order</text><rect x="166" y="44" width="224" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="278" y="65" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">PartService.findById</text><text x="278" y="80" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">throws PartNotFoundException</text><line x1="278" y1="88" x2="278" y2="116" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk198a)"/><text x="404" y="84" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">nothing catches it on the way up</text><rect x="100" y="116" width="356" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="278" y="143" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@ExceptionHandler in the same controller?</text><line x1="456" y1="139" x2="496" y2="139" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk198a)"/><text x="476" y="130" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><text x="502" y="134" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">renders your view —</text><text x="502" y="148" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">that controller only</text><line x1="278" y1="162" x2="278" y2="190" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk198a)"/><text x="290" y="180" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">no</text><rect x="100" y="190" width="356" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="278" y="217" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@ExceptionHandler in an @ControllerAdvice?</text><line x1="456" y1="213" x2="496" y2="213" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk198a)"/><text x="476" y="204" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><text x="502" y="208" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">one global handler</text><text x="502" y="222" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">for the whole app</text><line x1="278" y1="236" x2="278" y2="264" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk198a)"/><text x="290" y="254" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">no</text><rect x="100" y="264" width="356" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="278" y="291" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@ResponseStatus on the exception class?</text><line x1="456" y1="287" x2="496" y2="287" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk198a)"/><text x="476" y="278" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">yes</text><text x="502" y="282" fill="#e8795a" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">sets the status, then</text><text x="502" y="296" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the default page</text><line x1="278" y1="310" x2="278" y2="338" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk198a)"/><text x="290" y="328" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">no</text><rect x="100" y="338" width="356" height="46" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="278" y="365" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">none of the above</text><line x1="456" y1="361" x2="496" y2="361" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk198a)"/><text x="502" y="356" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9.6" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">500 and the /error page</text><text x="502" y="370" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.6" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">(error.html)</text><text x="340" y="424" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.2" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Among matching handlers the most specific exception type wins, so a handler for Exception never hides one for PartNotFoundException.</text><text x="340" y="450" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">The last row is not a fallback you chose. It is the one you get by default — and it shows the user a stack trace.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — turning a missing part into a 404 page instead of a stack trace</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Start from the failing call and build outwards. Three files, each doing one thing.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">// 1. the exception — @ResponseStatus sets the HTTP code even with no handler
@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)
public class PartNotFoundException extends RuntimeException {
    public PartNotFoundException(Long id) {
        super("No part with id " + id);
    }
}

// 2. the service throws it instead of returning null
@Service
public class PartService {
    private final PartRepository repo;
    public PartService(PartRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }

    public Part findById(Long id) {
        return repo.findById(id)
                   .orElseThrow(() -&gt; new PartNotFoundException(id));
    }
}

// 3. one global handler renders a friendly page for every controller
@ControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {

    private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(GlobalExceptionHandler.class);

    @ExceptionHandler(PartNotFoundException.class)
    public String handleNotFound(PartNotFoundException ex, Model model) {
        log.warn("Part lookup failed: {}", ex.getMessage());   // WARN: the client's mistake
        model.addAttribute("message", ex.getMessage());
        return "error/404";
    }

    @ExceptionHandler(Exception.class)
    public String handleEverythingElse(Exception ex, Model model) {
        log.error("Unhandled exception", ex);                  // ERROR: our mistake
        model.addAttribute("message", "Something went wrong. Please try again.");
        return "error/500";
    }
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Throw rather than return null.</strong> <code>Optional.orElseThrow</code> makes the failure explicit at the point it is discovered, which is the only place that knows what was being looked for.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>@ResponseStatus</code> on the exception class</strong> means that even if every handler were deleted, the response would still be 404 rather than 500. Without it, a custom <code>RuntimeException</code> is a server error as far as the browser is concerned.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>@ControllerAdvice</code> makes the handlers global.</strong> The same two methods now cover every controller in the application. Putting <code>@ExceptionHandler</code> inside one controller limits it to that controller — sometimes what you want, usually not.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Both handlers can coexist</strong> because Spring picks the <em>most specific</em> matching type. <code>PartNotFoundException</code> matches both methods; the narrower one wins. Ordering in the file is irrelevant.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Log at the right severity.</strong> WARN for a client asking for something that does not exist — expected, not actionable. ERROR, with the exception object passed as the last argument so the stack trace is printed, for anything unhandled.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Never put <code>ex.getMessage()</code> on the 500 page.</strong> Raw messages leak SQL fragments, file paths and class names. Log the detail; show the user a sentence.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Custom exception + <code>@ResponseStatus</code> + one <code>@ControllerAdvice</code> covers the whole application in about thirty lines.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — choosing between the four mechanisms</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Four scenarios, four different right answers. The question is always "how wide should this handling be?"</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Scenario</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Use</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Why not the others</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Only <code>ReportController</code> needs a special page for <code>ReportTimeoutException</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>@ExceptionHandler</code> inside <code>ReportController</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a global handler would apply it everywhere, including places where a timeout means something else</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Every controller should show the same page for a missing entity</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>@ExceptionHandler</code> in an <code>@ControllerAdvice</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">repeating the method in each controller is the duplication the annotation exists to remove</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">A REST client just needs the right status code, no body</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>@ResponseStatus</code> on the exception class</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a handler is more machinery than the requirement needs</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Brand the generic error page for anything unexpected</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>templates/error.html</code> (or <code>error/404.html</code>, <code>error/5xx.html</code>)</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">you cannot enumerate every possible failure in handlers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Precedence is narrow to wide.</strong> A controller-local handler beats an <code>@ControllerAdvice</code>; an <code>@ControllerAdvice</code> beats <code>@ResponseStatus</code>; and the <code>/error</code> page catches whatever is left. That order is what makes the four mechanisms combine instead of conflict.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>For a REST API the shape differs, not the mechanism.</strong> Return <code>ResponseEntity&lt;ErrorResponse&gt;</code> from an <code>@ExceptionHandler</code> in a <code>@RestControllerAdvice</code>, with a consistent body — timestamp, status, message, path — so clients can parse failures the same way every time.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The anti-pattern to name.</strong> <code>catch (Exception e) { }</code> with an empty body. The request appears to succeed, data quietly goes missing, and there is no record that anything happened. If you catch, log; if you cannot handle it, rethrow.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The default is not neutral.</strong> With nothing configured, Spring Boot shows the Whitelabel Error Page, which in a development profile includes the exception message and trace. That is an information leak in production, and the fix is simply to provide <code>error.html</code>.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Local handler for one controller, <code>@ControllerAdvice</code> for the application, <code>@ResponseStatus</code> for the code alone, <code>error.html</code> for everything unforeseen.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Learn the three annotations by scope: <strong><code>@ExceptionHandler</code></strong> handles exceptions from the controller it lives in; <strong><code>@ControllerAdvice</code></strong> makes those handlers global; <strong><code>@ResponseStatus</code></strong> on an exception class sets the HTTP code with no handler at all. A question that says "applies across all controllers" is asking for <code>@ControllerAdvice</code>. A question that says "returns 404" and shows only an exception class is asking for <code>@ResponseStatus(HttpStatus.NOT_FOUND)</code>. And remember: without <code>@ResponseStatus</code>, an uncaught custom exception is a 500.</p></div>"},"D287/ch5/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch5-s3"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Logging (SLF4J levels &amp; when to use them)</h3><p>Logging is println&rsquo;s grown-up sibling: leveled, timestamped, and controllable per environment without code changes.</p><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — log at the right levels</div><pre>private static final Logger log =
    LoggerFactory.getLogger(PartService.class);

public void buy(Long id) {
    log.debug("buy() called with id={}", id);      // dev detail
    ...
    log.info("Part {} purchased, inventory now {}", id, inv);  // normal event
    if (inv == min) log.warn("Part {} at minimum inventory", id); // heads-up
}
// on failure:
log.error("Purchase failed for part {}", id, ex);   // problem + stack trace</pre>With <code>logging.level.root=INFO</code> in application.properties, the debug line is invisible in production but appears when you dial a package to DEBUG — no recompile. The {} placeholders avoid building strings that would be thrown away.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE LEVEL LADDER</div><b>TRACE</b> &lt; <b>DEBUG</b> (developer detail) &lt; <b>INFO</b> (normal lifecycle events) &lt; <b>WARN</b> (suspicious but running) &lt; <b>ERROR</b> (something failed). Setting a level shows that level AND everything more severe. Spring Boot uses <b>SLF4J</b> as the logging face (Logback underneath by default).</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>With the level set to INFO, which messages appear?<ol type="A"><li>INFO, WARN, and ERROR</li><li>Only INFO</li><li>DEBUG and below</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> A level shows itself and everything MORE severe; DEBUG/TRACE are hidden.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Logging is how applications tell you what happened when nobody was watching. Spring Boot includes a logging stack out of the box. Used well, it's your most valuable debugging tool. Used poorly — too verbose, too sparse, missing context — it's noise.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. The logging stack.</strong> Spring Boot uses SLF4J (the API) and Logback (the implementation) by default. You write to SLF4J's Logger interface; Logback configures destinations, formats, and levels. Other implementations (Log4j2) are options but rarely needed.</p>
<p><strong>2. Getting a logger.</strong> private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(MyClass.class). Lombok's @Slf4j annotation generates the same field — saves typing. Use it everywhere you'd write System.out.println.</p>
<p><strong>3. Log levels.</strong> TRACE (extremely detailed), DEBUG (developer detail), INFO (normal events), WARN (something off but not failing), ERROR (failures). Production typically runs at INFO or WARN; turn on DEBUG temporarily for troubleshooting.</p>
<p><strong>4. Configuring levels.</strong> logging.level.root=INFO sets the default. logging.level.com.myapp=DEBUG enables DEBUG for your code while leaving framework code at INFO. Per-package control is the most useful day-to-day.</p>
<p><strong>5. Parameterized messages, not concatenation.</strong> log.info("Saved part {} for product {}", partId, productId). The {} placeholders are filled only if the level is enabled — avoids constructing the string for nothing. NEVER use string concatenation in log calls — wastes CPU at high volume and obscures the message structure.</p>
<p><strong>6. What to log.</strong> Application startup and shutdown. Errors with full context. State changes (user logged in, order placed). External service calls (URL, latency, status). NOT: every method entry/exit (too noisy), passwords / PII / tokens (security violation), or data that varies on every request without diagnostic value.</p>
<p><strong>7. Structured logging for production.</strong> JSON-formatted logs let log aggregators (ELK, Datadog, Splunk) parse fields automatically. Configure Logback with a JSON encoder; suddenly you can filter logs by user, request ID, error type. The investment pays back the first incident.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Logging passwords, API keys, tokens, or full PII — security and compliance violation.</li>
 <li>String concatenation in log calls — wastes CPU even when the level is disabled.</li>
 <li>Logging every method entry/exit — drowns the signal.</li>
 <li>Wrong level — INFO for things that should be DEBUG, ERROR for things that are normal validation failures.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Spring Boot uses SLF4J + Logback. Get a Logger per class. Use the right level (TRACE/DEBUG for development, INFO for events, WARN for concerns, ERROR for failures). Use parameterized messages, never concatenation. Log meaningful events; never log secrets. Move toward structured (JSON) logging as the app grows.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">SLF4J</span></dt>
 <dd>Logging facade Java code writes against, decoupling from implementation.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Logback</span></dt>
 <dd>Default logging implementation in Spring Boot, behind SLF4J.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Log level</span></dt>
 <dd>Severity classification: TRACE, DEBUG, INFO, WARN, ERROR.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Parameterized message</span></dt>
 <dd>log.info("hello {}", name) — the placeholder is filled only if the level is enabled.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Structured logging</span></dt>
 <dd>Logging in a parseable format (JSON) for ingestion by log aggregators.</dd>
</dl>
<p><strong>Per-package log levels:</strong> in application.properties, logging.level.com.app=DEBUG raises verbosity for that package and everything below it — the standard way to debug one slice of a Spring app.</p><i data-tb287c5s3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the logging pipeline and the gate that discards most of what you write</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 418" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk199a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">your code calls a facade; the level gate decides whether anything is ever written</text><rect x="30" y="48" width="250" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="155" y="72" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"></text><text x="155" y="73" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">log.debug("loading part {}", id)</text><rect x="30" y="100" width="250" height="40" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="155" y="124" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle"></text><text x="155" y="125" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">log.warn("inventory below min")</text><line x1="280" y1="68" x2="330" y2="84" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk199a)"/><line x1="280" y1="120" x2="330" y2="104" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk199a)"/><rect x="330" y="66" width="150" height="56" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="405" y="93" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">SLF4J</text><text x="405" y="108" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the API you compile against</text><line x1="480" y1="94" x2="520" y2="94" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk199a)"/><rect x="520" y="66" width="140" height="56" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="590" y="93" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Logback</text><text x="590" y="108" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the implementation</text><text x="524" y="146" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">swap in Log4j2 and not one</text><text x="524" y="160" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">line of your code changes</text><line x1="590" y1="122" x2="590" y2="186" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk199a)"/><rect x="150" y="186" width="480" height="96" rx="10" fill="#e8795a" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="390" y="208" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the level gate</text><text x="390" y="228" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">logging.level.com.example.inventory = INFO</text><rect x="160" y="244" width="80" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="200" y="261" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">TRACE</text><text x="200" y="296" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9.4" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✗ dropped</text><rect x="256" y="244" width="80" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="296" y="261" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">DEBUG</text><text x="296" y="296" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9.4" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✗ dropped</text><rect x="352" y="244" width="80" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="392" y="261" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">INFO</text><text x="392" y="296" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.4" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✓ written</text><rect x="448" y="244" width="80" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="488" y="261" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">WARN</text><text x="488" y="296" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.4" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✓ written</text><rect x="544" y="244" width="80" height="26" rx="6" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.5"/><text x="584" y="261" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">ERROR</text><text x="584" y="296" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.4" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✓ written</text><line x1="390" y1="306" x2="390" y2="332" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk199a)"/><rect x="180" y="332" width="190" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="275" y="358" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">console appender</text><rect x="410" y="332" width="190" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="505" y="358" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">file appender</text><line x1="378" y1="330" x2="300" y2="330" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk199a)"/><line x1="402" y1="330" x2="490" y2="330" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk199a)"/><text x="340" y="402" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">The threshold is a runtime setting, not a code change: raise it to DEBUG for one package while everything else stays at INFO.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — why the {} placeholder is not a style preference</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The usual explanation is "it is cleaner". The real reason is that string concatenation is evaluated before the call, whether or not the level is enabled. This snippet makes the cost visible.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">public class F {
    static String describe(String sku) {
        System.out.println("  [built the description for " + sku + "]");
        return sku;
    }
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        boolean debugEnabled = false;                    // stands in for the level gate

        String message = "loaded part " + describe("BP-1042");   // built no matter what
        if (debugEnabled) System.out.println(message);

        System.out.println("---");

        if (debugEnabled) System.out.println("loaded part " + describe("HB-2210"));

        System.out.println("request finished");
    }
}</code></pre><div style="font-size:11px; font-weight:700; letter-spacing:0.07em; color:var(--text-3); margin:14px 0 -2px;">OUTPUT — verified on JDK 21</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto; border-left:3px solid #10b981;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">  [built the description for BP-1042]
---
request finished</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The first description was built and thrown away.</strong> Its trace line printed even though nothing was logged, because the argument was evaluated before the guard.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The second was never built at all.</strong> Guarding the call skipped the concatenation entirely — no trace line for HB-2210 appears.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>SLF4J's <code>{}</code> form is that guard, built in.</strong> <code>log.debug("loaded part {}", describe(sku))</code> still evaluates the argument, but <code>log.debug("loaded part {}", part)</code> defers the far more common cost — calling <code>toString()</code> and building the string — until Logback has confirmed the level is enabled.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Scale it.</strong> One wasted concatenation is nothing; a DEBUG line in a loop over ten thousand rows, on a server running at INFO, is ten thousand strings built and discarded per request.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The rule.</strong> Use <code>log.debug("saved part {} for product {}", partId, productId)</code>. Never <code>log.debug("saved part " + partId)</code>. And pass an exception as the final argument with no placeholder — <code>log.error("import failed", ex)</code> — which is what prints the stack trace.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: The placeholder defers the formatting until the level check has passed. Concatenation performs the work first and then finds out it was not needed.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — picking a level, and configuring it per package</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Five events from one request through the inventory application. Assign a level to each, then set the thresholds.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Event</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Level</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Reasoning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Entering <code>PartService.save</code> with the full object</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">TRACE</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">useful for half an hour of debugging, noise forever after</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">The SQL Hibernate generated for a query</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">DEBUG</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">developer detail; switch it on when a query surprises you</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">"Part 42 saved by user natasha"</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">INFO</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a state change worth having in the record</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Inventory dropped below its minimum</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">WARN</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">nothing failed, but somebody should look</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">The nightly supplier import threw an exception</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">ERROR</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a failure; include the exception object</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">The password from the login form</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><strong>never</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">logging secrets is a security incident, not a debugging technique</td></tr></tbody></table></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;"># application.properties
logging.level.root=INFO
logging.level.com.example.inventory=DEBUG
logging.level.org.hibernate.SQL=DEBUG
logging.level.org.springframework.web=INFO

logging.file.name=logs/inventory.log
logging.pattern.console=%d{HH:mm:ss} %-5level %logger{20} - %msg%n</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Levels are ordered, and the setting is a floor.</strong> TRACE &lt; DEBUG &lt; INFO &lt; WARN &lt; ERROR. Setting a logger to INFO writes INFO, WARN and ERROR, and discards DEBUG and TRACE.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Per-package control is the useful part.</strong> <code>logging.level.com.example.inventory=DEBUG</code> turns on your own detail while the framework stays at INFO — otherwise DEBUG on <code>root</code> buries your five lines under Spring's thousand.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Configuration beats code.</strong> Changing a threshold is a property, not an edit and a redeploy. This is exactly why <code>System.out.println</code> is not an acceptable substitute: it has no level, no logger name, no timestamp, and no way to turn it off.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Get the logger once, per class:</strong> <code>private static final Logger log = LoggerFactory.getLogger(PartService.class);</code> — <code>static final</code> so it is created once, and passing the class is what gives the logger the name that the per-package thresholds match against.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Production settles around INFO or WARN.</strong> DEBUG goes on temporarily, for one package, while something is being investigated.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: TRACE and DEBUG for developers, INFO for events, WARN for concerns, ERROR for failures — and nothing at all for credentials or personal data.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Three things get asked. <strong>The stack:</strong> SLF4J is the API you code against, Logback is Spring Boot's default implementation behind it. <strong>The order:</strong> TRACE &lt; DEBUG &lt; INFO &lt; WARN &lt; ERROR, and a threshold admits its own level and everything above it. <strong>The idiom:</strong> <code>log.info("saved part {}", id)</code> with a placeholder, never string concatenation — because the argument is only formatted if the level is enabled. Bonus marks come from knowing <code>logging.level.&lt;package&gt;=DEBUG</code> is how you raise the level for your own code alone, and that passwords, tokens and personal data are never logged at any level.</p></div><i data-d287cov_53="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>@Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)</strong> means the <strong>database generates the primary key</strong> — an auto-increment column. You leave the id null when saving, and JPA reads back the value the database assigned.</div><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Strategy</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Who generates the key</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><strong>IDENTITY</strong></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the database, via an auto-increment column</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">SEQUENCE</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a database sequence object</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">TABLE</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a dedicated table of counters</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">AUTO</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">the provider picks one for you</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#f59e0b;"><div class="callout-label">⚠️ COMMON MISTAKE</div>Setting the id yourself on a new entity with IDENTITY makes JPA treat the object as an <em>existing</em> row, so <code>save()</code> becomes an update to a row that may not exist. Leave generated ids null on creation.</div>"},"D287/ch6/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch6-s1"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — The PA: Part Management Application (the map)</h3><p>The D287 Performance Assessment starts from a provided Spring Boot &ldquo;inventory&rdquo; starter app (products made of parts) that you customize and extend. Here&rsquo;s the shape of the work:</p><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE TASK LIST, IN ORDER</div>1. <b>Set up</b> the starter project in IntelliJ, run it, commit to GitLab (small, frequent commits with messages — they&rsquo;re graded).<br>2. <b>Customize the shop</b>: rename the store/brand in the templates for your imaginary business.<br>3. <b>Add an About page</b>: new @Controller mapping + Thymeleaf template + a nav link.<br>4. <b>Seed sample inventory</b>: ~5 parts and ~5 products added at startup, guarded so they only load when the database is empty.<br>5. <b>Add min/max inventory fields</b> to Part: entity fields + form inputs + validation.<br>6. <b>Enforce the rules</b>: buying/updating can&rsquo;t push inventory below min or above max — with friendly error messages.<br>7. <b>Add unit tests</b> for the new max/min behavior.<br>8. <b>Record the video demo</b> and submit.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — how one requirement maps to code you know</div>&ldquo;Sample inventory appears when the app first runs&rdquo; = a <b>CommandLineRunner</b> (ch4/s4) with <code>if (repo.count() == 0)</code> around the saves. &ldquo;Add an About page&rdquo; = a <b>@GetMapping("/about")</b> returning "about" (ch3/s1) + about.html in templates (ch3/s2). Every PA requirement is one of the patterns from chapters 2-5 — nothing new appears in the PA itself.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>Sample data must load only when the database is empty. The standard guard is:<ol type="A"><li>if (repo.count() == 0) around the seed saves</li><li>Deleting the database manually first</li><li>A while(true) loop</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Check count()==0 in the CommandLineRunner so restarts don&rsquo;t duplicate the sample rows.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>The D287 Performance Assessment is a Spring Boot inventory app for parts and products. provides starter code; you extend it. Knowing the structure of the starter and the rubric expectations before you write code prevents two common failure modes: building too much (you only need to add specific features) and missing required pieces.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. The starter code provides scaffolding.</strong> 's starter typically includes: a Spring Boot project with Thymeleaf, JPA, and H2 wired up. Entity classes for Part (abstract) with Inhouse and Outsourced subclasses. A Product entity. Repositories. Controllers and templates for listing, creating, updating, deleting. The shape is in place; specific behaviors are missing.</p>
<p><strong>2. What you typically must add.</strong> Validation rules (min/max inventory, name not blank, price positive). Business logic (preventing inventory below min or above max). Possibly additional CRUD operations the starter stubs out. JUnit tests for the validation and business logic. A write-up explaining your design decisions and how you implemented the requirements.</p>
<p><strong>3. Inhouse vs Outsourced — entity inheritance.</strong> Part is the parent class with @Inheritance. Inhouse adds a machineId field. Outsourced adds a companyName field. Single-table inheritance is typical — one table with a discriminator column distinguishes the two subtypes. The PA tests both create and edit flows for each subtype.</p>
<p><strong>4. Product-Part relationship.</strong> A Product can have many Parts; a Part can be in many Products. @ManyToMany with a join table. UI typically shows associated parts on a product detail page and lets you add/remove parts from products.</p>
<p><strong>5. Read the rubric BEFORE coding.</strong> 's rubric is detailed. Each row maps to a specific deliverable: validation rule, error message, test case, write-up section. Treat the rubric as a checklist. Many students lose points not because they couldn't do the work but because they missed a row.</p>
<p><strong>6. The write-up matters.</strong> Your code is checked against the rubric, but so is your written explanation. You usually have to explain: what you implemented, why you chose that approach, what alternatives you considered, how you tested. Write clearly; cite specifics; show understanding, not just code.</p>
<p><strong>7. Submitting clean code.</strong> Reviewers look for: consistent style, meaningful variable names, no dead code, no commented-out blocks, no debug System.out.println left over. Run a formatter. Read each changed file once before submitting.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Modifying parts of the starter that the rubric doesn't ask you to touch — risks breaking provided functionality.</li>
 <li>Missing the write-up, or submitting one that's a few sentences — usually accounts for significant rubric weight.</li>
 <li>Tests that pass but don't actually exercise the validation rules (e.g., test for valid input only).</li>
 <li>Submitting without running the app one final time — small breaks (typo in template, wrong route) are common late-stage.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>The PA gives you a Spring Boot starter; extend it with validation, business logic, tests, and a write-up. Inhouse and Outsourced extend Part via JPA inheritance. Products and Parts have @ManyToMany. Read the rubric first and treat it as a checklist. The write-up matters. Run the app one last time before submitting.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">PA (Performance Assessment)</span></dt>
 <dd>'s project-style assessment, graded against a rubric.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Starter code</span></dt>
 <dd>Project skeleton provides; you extend it without rewriting.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Inheritance hierarchy</span></dt>
 <dd>Part → Inhouse / Outsourced; persisted with JPA inheritance annotations.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@ManyToMany</span></dt>
 <dd>JPA relationship between Product and Part, requiring a join table.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Rubric</span></dt>
 <dd>Detailed checklist of what graders score; treat it as the spec.</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c6s1="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the layered round trip: a request down through controller, service and repository, and the answer back up</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 522" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk200a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">one "Save part" click, all the way down and all the way back</text><rect x="212" y="46" width="276" height="44" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="350" y="67" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the browser</text><text x="350" y="82" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">a Thymeleaf form on parts/form.html</text><rect x="212" y="126" width="276" height="50" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="350" y="150" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@Controller  PartController</text><text x="350" y="165" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">HTTP only: bind, validate, pick a view</text><rect x="212" y="212" width="276" height="50" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="350" y="236" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@Service  PartService</text><text x="350" y="251" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the rules: min ≤ inventory ≤ max, @Transactional</text><rect x="212" y="298" width="276" height="50" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="350" y="322" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">@Repository  PartRepository</text><text x="350" y="337" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">an interface; Spring Data wrote the class</text><rect x="212" y="384" width="276" height="50" rx="9" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.7"/><text x="350" y="408" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Hibernate → JDBC → H2</text><text x="350" y="423" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">entities become SQL, rows become entities</text><line x1="258" y1="90" x2="258" y2="126" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk200a)"/><circle cx="232" cy="108" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="232" y="112" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">1</text><text x="200" y="104" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">POST /parts</text><text x="200" y="118" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">name=Brake+pad&amp;inventory=40</text><line x1="258" y1="176" x2="258" y2="212" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk200a)"/><circle cx="232" cy="194" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="232" y="198" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">2</text><text x="200" y="190" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">service.save(part)</text><text x="200" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">a Part object — no HTTP down here</text><line x1="258" y1="262" x2="258" y2="298" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk200a)"/><circle cx="232" cy="280" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="232" y="284" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">3</text><text x="200" y="276" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">repo.save(part)</text><text x="200" y="290" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">no business rules down here either</text><line x1="258" y1="348" x2="258" y2="384" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk200a)"/><circle cx="232" cy="366" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="232" y="370" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">4</text><text x="200" y="362" fill="var(--text)" font-size="9.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">INSERT INTO parts …</text><text x="200" y="376" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="end">generated by Hibernate</text><line x1="442" y1="384" x2="442" y2="348" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk200a)"/><circle cx="468" cy="366" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="468" y="370" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">5</text><text x="500" y="362" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">id = 42 assigned</text><text x="500" y="376" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">by the database, on INSERT</text><line x1="442" y1="298" x2="442" y2="262" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk200a)"/><circle cx="468" cy="280" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="468" y="284" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">6</text><text x="500" y="276" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">the saved Part</text><text x="500" y="290" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">now managed, now has an id</text><line x1="442" y1="212" x2="442" y2="176" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk200a)"/><circle cx="468" cy="194" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="468" y="198" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">7</text><text x="500" y="190" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">returns; commit</text><text x="500" y="204" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the transaction ends here</text><line x1="442" y1="126" x2="442" y2="90" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk200a)"/><circle cx="468" cy="108" r="10.5" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="468" y="112" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">8</text><text x="500" y="104" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="start">"redirect:/parts"</text><text x="500" y="118" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="start">the browser GETs the list again</text><text x="340" y="462" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Each layer knows only the one below it. That is what makes the service testable without HTTP</text><text x="340" y="478" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.8" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">and the repository swappable without touching a single business rule.</text><text x="340" y="506" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.2" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">Rules in the controller, or SQL in the service, and the whole arrangement stops paying for itself.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — placing the PA's classes in the right layer</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Most marks lost on layering are lost by writing correct code in the wrong file. Here is the rule for each layer, and the specific temptation it resists.</p><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Layer</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">May do</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Must not do</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">The PA temptation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>@Controller</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">bind the form, call <code>@Valid</code>, choose a view, redirect</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">contain business rules or call a repository</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">checking <code>inventory &gt; max</code> inside the handler</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>@Service</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">enforce rules, coordinate repositories, own <code>@Transactional</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">know about HTTP, sessions, or view names</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">returning a view name from a service method</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>@Repository</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">query and persist</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">validate or decide anything</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">putting an <code>if</code> in a repository method</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;"><code>@Entity</code></td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">hold state and field-level constraints</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">reach a repository or a service</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">looking a product up from inside the entity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">@Controller
@RequestMapping("/parts")
public class PartController {

    private final PartService service;                 // depends downward only
    public PartController(PartService service) { this.service = service; }

    @PostMapping
    public String save(@Valid @ModelAttribute("part") Inhouse part,
                       BindingResult result,
                       RedirectAttributes redirect) {
        if (result.hasErrors()) {
            return "parts/form";                       // a view decision — correct here
        }
        service.save(part);                            // the rules live one layer down
        redirect.addFlashAttribute("message", "Part saved.");
        return "redirect:/parts";
    }
}

@Service
public class PartService {

    private final PartRepository repo;
    public PartService(PartRepository repo) { this.repo = repo; }

    @Transactional
    public Part save(Part part) {
        if (part.getInventory() &lt; part.getMin() || part.getInventory() &gt; part.getMax()) {
            throw new InvalidInventoryException(
                "Inventory must be between " + part.getMin() + " and " + part.getMax());
        }
        return repo.save(part);                        // no SQL, no HTTP
    }
}

public interface PartRepository extends JpaRepository&lt;Part, Long&gt; {
    List&lt;Part&gt; findByNameContainingIgnoreCase(String fragment);   // no rules at all
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Dependencies point in one direction only.</strong> Controller → service → repository. A service that imports <code>HttpServletRequest</code>, or a repository that imports a service, has broken the arrangement even if it compiles.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The controller's decisions are presentation decisions.</strong> "Which template", "redirect or re-render" — those belong nowhere else. "Is this inventory legal" is not one of them.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>@Transactional</code> belongs on the service</strong>, because a transaction spans a business operation, which may touch several repositories. On a repository method it is too narrow to be useful; on a controller it holds the transaction open across view rendering.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The payoff is the test.</strong> <code>new PartService(mock(PartRepository.class))</code> exercises every inventory rule with no Spring context, no HTTP and no database — which is the kind of test the PA rubric asks for.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Controller: HTTP and views. Service: rules and transactions. Repository: persistence. Entity: state. Every PA rubric row lands in exactly one of them.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — reading the starter project before changing anything</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The PA supplies a working application and asks you to extend it. An hour spent reading saves a day of undoing. Read in this order.</p><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The entity hierarchy first, because everything else follows from it.</strong> <code>Part</code> is abstract with <code>@Inheritance(strategy = InheritanceType.SINGLE_TABLE)</code>; <code>Inhouse</code> adds <code>machineId</code> and <code>Outsourced</code> adds <code>companyName</code>. Single-table inheritance means one <code>parts</code> table with a discriminator column naming the subtype — which is why the two subclasses cannot both use <code>NOT NULL</code> columns for their own fields.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The Product–Part relationship.</strong> <code>@ManyToMany</code> with a join table, because a part can appear in several products and a product contains several parts. Note which side carries <code>@JoinTable</code> and which carries <code>mappedBy</code>, because that decides which side you have to update for a change to persist.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The repositories.</strong> They are interfaces extending <code>JpaRepository</code>. There is no implementation to find — if you go looking for one, you will waste an afternoon.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>The controllers and templates as pairs.</strong> Each handler returns a view name; find the matching file under <code>src/main/resources/templates</code>. That mapping is the fastest way to learn what the application already does.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong><code>application.properties</code> and any <code>data.sql</code>.</strong> These tell you the database mode, the <code>ddl-auto</code> setting, and what sample data the graders will see when they start your submission.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Only then, the rubric.</strong> Turn each row into a checklist item naming the file you will change. Rows that ask for a specific user-facing message are quoting the words they will search for — copy them exactly.</li></ol><div style="overflow-x:auto; margin:14px 0;"><table style="border-collapse:collapse; width:100%; font-size:14.5px;"><thead><tr><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Rubric row</th><th style="text-align:left; padding:7px 12px 7px 0; border-bottom:2px solid var(--border); font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.04em;">Where the change goes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Inventory must be between minimum and maximum</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>Part</code> (class-level constraint) or <code>PartService.save</code></td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">A part in use cannot be deleted</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>PartService.delete</code> — it needs repository state, so it cannot be an annotation</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Buying more than are on hand is refused</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>ProductService</code>, with a message the template displays</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">Unit tests for each rule</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);"><code>src/test/java</code>, one passing and one failing case per rule</td></tr><tr><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border); font-weight:600;">The write-up</td><td style="padding:6px 12px 6px 0; border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);">a separate document — worth real marks, and the easiest thing to under-write</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Read the entity hierarchy, the relationship, the repositories, the controller/template pairs, then the configuration — and only then start turning rubric rows into file names.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The PA-flavoured questions are about structure, not syntax. <strong>Which layer?</strong> Business rules go in the <code>@Service</code>; the controller only binds, validates and picks a view; the repository only persists. <strong>Which JPA feature?</strong> <code>Inhouse</code>/<code>Outsourced</code> extending <code>Part</code> is <code>@Inheritance</code> — single-table with a discriminator column by default; Product-to-Part is <code>@ManyToMany</code> with a join table. <strong>Which annotation makes it transactional?</strong> <code>@Transactional</code> on the service method. If a question offers "put the validation in the controller", it is wrong for the same reason every time.</p></div><i data-d287cov_61="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">What the performance assessment actually asks for</h4><p>The <strong>D287 performance assessment</strong> is to build a <strong>Spring Boot part management web application from a supplied template</strong> — parts and products, validation, search, and the relationship between them — rather than to start from an empty project.</p><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the layered flow every request follows</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 620 170" style="width:100%; max-width:620px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk327a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><rect x="-12" y="52" width="104" height="44" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="40" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Browser</text><line x1="92" y1="68" x2="112" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk327a)"/><rect x="108" y="52" width="104" height="44" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="160" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Controller</text><line x1="212" y1="68" x2="232" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk327a)"/><rect x="233" y="52" width="104" height="44" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="285" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Service</text><line x1="337" y1="68" x2="357" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk327a)"/><rect x="358" y="52" width="104" height="44" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="410" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Repository</text><line x1="462" y1="68" x2="482" y2="68" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk327a)"/><rect x="478" y="52" width="104" height="44" rx="8" fill="none" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="530" y="79" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">Database</text><line x1="478" y1="84" x2="462" y2="84" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk327a)"/><line x1="358" y1="84" x2="337" y2="84" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk327a)"/><line x1="233" y1="84" x2="212" y2="84" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk327a)"/><line x1="108" y1="84" x2="92" y2="84" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.6" marker-end="url(#fk327a)"/><text x="310" y="122" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">requests go down the layers, data comes back up</text><text x="310" y="142" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">the Controller returns a Thymeleaf view name, which renders the HTML</text></svg></div></div><p>So the flow is <strong>Controller → Service → Repository → Database, with Thymeleaf views</strong>. Each layer talks only to its neighbour: putting a repository call in a controller, or HTML in a service, is the structural mistake evaluators look for.</p>"},"D287/ch6/s2":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch6-s2"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — The PA&rsquo;s Core Logic: min/max, buy-now, @Transactional</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — enforce min/max inventory</div><pre>// on the entity
@Min(0) private int minInventory;
private int maxInventory;

// in the service, before saving a part or completing a buy
if (part.getInventory() &lt; part.getMinInventory())
    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
        "Inventory below minimum for " + part.getName());
if (part.getInventory() &gt; part.getMaxInventory())
    throw new IllegalArgumentException(
        "Inventory above maximum for " + part.getName());</pre>Try to save inventory 2 with min 5 → the rule throws → the handler/form shows &ldquo;Inventory below minimum&rdquo; → nothing bad reaches the DB. The graders test exactly these edges: below min, above max, and the boundary values themselves.</div><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE 2 — the &ldquo;Buy Now&rdquo; action done right</div>A buy must do TWO things together: record the purchase AND decrement product inventory —
 and show success/failure to the user:<br><pre>@Transactional                    // ← all-or-nothing
public void buyProduct(Long id) {
    Product p = repo.findById(id).orElseThrow();
    if (p.getInventory() &lt;= 0)
        throw new IllegalStateException("Out of stock");
    p.setInventory(p.getInventory() - 1);   // decrement
    // save purchase record here if required
}</pre><b>@Transactional</b> wraps the method in a database transaction: if ANY step throws, every change rolls back — you can&rsquo;t decrement inventory and then lose the purchase record. Success → confirmation page; failure → error message. That pairing is the graded behavior.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">TIMESTAMPS (createdAt / updatedAt)</div>Audit fields update themselves with lifecycle hooks: <code>@PrePersist</code> sets createdAt once on first save; <code>@PreUpdate</code> refreshes updatedAt on every change. (Or @CreationTimestamp / @UpdateTimestamp with Hibernate.) Questions phrase it as &ldquo;set automatically when the row is created/modified.&rdquo;</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>@Transactional on a service method guarantees:<ol type="A"><li>All its DB changes commit together or roll back together</li><li>The method runs faster</li><li>The method can&rsquo;t throw exceptions</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> One transaction: any failure rolls back every change the method made — no half-finished buys.</div></details></div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>The validation and business logic you add are the meat of the PA. They turn a starter into a working inventory system. The rubric is specific — each rule is a row — and getting them right requires both annotation-based validation and class-level checks for cross-field rules.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. Inventory min/max rules.</strong> The classic PA rule: inventory must satisfy min ≤ inventory ≤ max. This is a cross-field rule, so a single field-level annotation doesn't cover it. Implement as a class-level constraint (@ValidPart with a ConstraintValidator) or check programmatically in the service before save.</p>
<p><strong>2. Field-level validation annotations.</strong> @NotBlank on name. @Min(0) and @Max appropriate to the rubric on inventory and price. @Positive on price. Apply directly to the entity fields. These trigger automatically when @Valid is on the controller parameter.</p>
<p><strong>3. Triggering validation in the controller.</strong> @PostMapping("/parts") public String save(@Valid @ModelAttribute Part part, BindingResult result, Model model) { if (result.hasErrors()) return "parts/form"; partService.save(part); return "redirect:/parts"; }. Re-render the form on errors; redirect on success.</p>
<p><strong>4. Custom error messages mapped to rubric language.</strong> If the rubric requires the message 'Inventory cannot be less than minimum,' put exactly that in @Min(message = ...) or your custom validator. Graders look for specific user-facing messages; vague defaults lose points.</p>
<p><strong>5. Class-level constraint for min/max inventory.</strong> @ValidPart on the Part class. ConstraintValidator implementation: isValid checks part.getInventory() >= part.getMin() && part.getInventory() <= part.getMax(). On failure, add a constraint violation to the context with a clear message. This catches the cross-field rule field-level annotations can't.</p>
<p><strong>6. Service layer for additional logic.</strong> PartService.save() can: check duplicate names, prevent deleting a part that's in a product, ensure price > 0. Things that depend on database state or domain rules belong here, not in the entity. Keep the controller thin.</p>
<p><strong>7. Testing your validations.</strong> JUnit tests in src/test/java exercise the rules. Each rubric rule should have at least one test confirming it triggers on bad input AND one confirming it does NOT trigger on good input. Use Spring's TestEntityManager or mock the repository.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Hard-coded literal messages that don't match the rubric — graders look for the exact wording specified.</li>
 <li>Validation only via field-level annotations when the rule is cross-field — min/max inventory needs class-level or service logic.</li>
 <li>Forgetting to re-render the form on errors — user input is lost when the controller redirects.</li>
 <li>Tests that only check valid input — half the rubric is failure cases.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Add @NotBlank, @Min, @Max, @Positive on entity fields. Use class-level constraints or service logic for cross-field rules (inventory between min and max). Write error messages matching rubric language. Trigger with @Valid + BindingResult in the controller; re-render on errors. Cover each rule with positive AND negative JUnit tests.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Field-level validation</span></dt>
 <dd>Annotations on individual fields (@NotBlank, @Min, @Max).</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Class-level validation</span></dt>
 <dd>Custom annotations on the class for cross-field rules.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">ConstraintValidator</span></dt>
 <dd>Interface to implement for custom validation logic.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Service-layer validation</span></dt>
 <dd>Domain rules checked in @Service methods, often using repository state.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Negative test case</span></dt>
 <dd>Test that confirms invalid input is rejected, not just that valid input is accepted.</dd>
</dl>
<i data-tb287c6s2="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — the inventory rule on a number line, and the reason it has to be a class-level constraint</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 396" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk201a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the rule min ≤ inventory ≤ max, and why no single field annotation can express it</text><line x1="70" y1="108" x2="620" y2="108" stroke="var(--text)" stroke-width="1.8"/><line x1="70" y1="103" x2="70" y2="113" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="70" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">0</text><line x1="138.75" y1="103" x2="138.75" y2="113" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="138.75" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">20</text><line x1="207.5" y1="103" x2="207.5" y2="113" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="207.5" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">40</text><line x1="276.25" y1="103" x2="276.25" y2="113" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="276.25" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">60</text><line x1="345" y1="103" x2="345" y2="113" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="345" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">80</text><line x1="413.75" y1="103" x2="413.75" y2="113" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="413.75" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">100</text><line x1="482.5" y1="103" x2="482.5" y2="113" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="482.5" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">120</text><line x1="551.25" y1="103" x2="551.25" y2="113" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="551.25" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">140</text><line x1="620" y1="103" x2="620" y2="113" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="620" y="130" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9.4" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">160</text><rect x="104.375" y="84" width="378.125" height="24" rx="5" fill="#10b981" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1"/><text x="293.4375" y="100" fill="var(--surface)" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the only legal band</text><line x1="104.375" y1="60" x2="104.375" y2="84" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="104.375" y="52" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">min = 10</text><line x1="482.5" y1="60" x2="482.5" y2="84" stroke="#8b5cf6" stroke-width="1.6"/><text x="482.5" y="52" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">max = 120</text><circle cx="87.1875" cy="108" r="6.5" fill="#e11d48" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="87.1875" y1="114" x2="87.1875" y2="152" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/><text x="87.1875" y="166" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">inventory = 5</text><text x="87.1875" y="180" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9.2" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">below min</text><circle cx="207.5" cy="108" r="6.5" fill="#10b981" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="207.5" y1="114" x2="207.5" y2="152" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/><text x="207.5" y="166" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">inventory = 40</text><text x="207.5" y="180" fill="#10b981" font-size="9.2" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">legal</text><circle cx="551.25" cy="108" r="6.5" fill="#e11d48" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.6"/><line x1="551.25" y1="114" x2="551.25" y2="152" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2" stroke-dasharray="3 2"/><text x="551.25" y="166" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">inventory = 140</text><text x="551.25" y="180" fill="#e11d48" font-size="9.2" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">above max</text><text x="340" y="216" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">what each kind of constraint can actually see</text><rect x="30" y="232" width="300" height="118" rx="10" fill="#e11d48" opacity="0.10" stroke="#e11d48" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="180" y="252" fill="#e11d48" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@Min(0) on the inventory field</text><text x="180" y="274" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the validator receives one argument: 5</text><text x="180" y="292" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">It has no access to min or max —</text><text x="180" y="308" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">they are other fields of another object.</text><text x="180" y="332" fill="#e11d48" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✗ all three values above pass</text><rect x="350" y="232" width="300" height="118" rx="10" fill="#10b981" opacity="0.10" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.2"/><text x="500" y="252" fill="#10b981" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@ValidPart on the class</text><text x="500" y="274" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">the validator receives the whole Part</text><text x="500" y="292" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">isValid: inventory >= min</text><text x="500" y="308" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">      && inventory <= max</text><text x="500" y="332" fill="#10b981" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">✓ rejects 5 and 140, accepts 40</text><text x="340" y="380" fill="var(--text)" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">A field constraint is handed one value. A class constraint is handed the object. Any rule naming two fields needs the second kind.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Every rubric row is one of two shapes, and naming the shape gives you the implementation. <strong>One field ⇒ a field annotation</strong> — <code>@NotBlank</code> on the name, <code>@Min(0)</code> on inventory, <code>@Positive</code> or <code>@DecimalMin("0.01")</code> on the price. <strong>Two or more fields ⇒ a class-level constraint</strong> with a <code>ConstraintValidator</code>, or a check in the service method. The min/max inventory rule is the canonical example and is asked about constantly. <strong>Rules needing the database</strong> — "a part in use cannot be deleted", "cannot buy more than are on hand" — cannot be annotations at all, because a validator sees only the object; they belong in the <code>@Service</code>. And whatever the rule, copy the required message text verbatim from the rubric: the default "must not be blank" earns nothing when the specification says "Part name is required".</p></div><i data-d287cov_62="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">Concurrent updates to inventory</h4><p>Two users buying the last item at the same moment is a <strong>race condition</strong>: both read inventory = 1, both subtract 1, both save, and the stock ends at 0 having sold two items. Nothing in the code is obviously wrong — the reads simply overlapped.</p><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>Optimistic locking</strong> fixes it. Add a <code>@Version</code> field and JPA includes it in the WHERE clause of every update, then checks how many rows changed. The second writer finds the version has moved on, updates nothing, and gets an <code>OptimisticLockException</code> instead of silently overwriting.</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">@Entity
public class Part {
    @Id @GeneratedValue(strategy = GenerationType.IDENTITY)
    private Long id;
    private int inv;

    @Version
    private Long version;   // JPA maintains this
}</pre><p>It is called <em>optimistic</em> because it assumes conflicts are rare and only detects them at write time — no rows are locked while a user is thinking.</p>"},"D287/ch6/s3":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-deepdive="d287-ch6-s3"><h3>📖 Learn it step by step — Testing &amp; Submitting the PA</h3><div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">WORKED EXAMPLE — a unit test for the max-inventory rule</div><pre>@Test
void rejectsInventoryAboveMax() {
    Part p = new Part("Bolt", 0.25);
    p.setMaxInventory(10);
    p.setInventory(11);                    // over the max

    assertThrows(IllegalArgumentException.class,
                 () -&gt; service.validate(p));   // must throw
}

@Test
void acceptsInventoryInRange() {
    Part p = new Part("Bolt", 0.25);
    p.setMinInventory(1);  p.setMaxInventory(10);
    p.setInventory(5);
    assertDoesNotThrow(() -&gt; service.validate(p));
}</pre>One test per rule, both directions: the bad case <b>throws</b>, the good case <b>doesn&rsquo;t</b>. Run with the Maven <code>test</code> goal; all green before submitting.</div><div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">SUBMISSION CHECKLIST</div>&bull; All required features work (about page, seed data, min/max, buy button with confirm/error).<br>&bull; Unit tests for the added logic pass.<br>&bull; GitLab history shows <b>multiple commits with meaningful messages</b> (graded — don&rsquo;t one-shot commit).<br>&bull; <b>Video demo</b>: record yourself running the app and showing each requirement working, face on camera if required, within the time limit — then submit repo link + video together.</div><div class="vl-oaq"><div class="qt">🎯 QUICK CHECK</div>The PA wants your GitLab history to show:<ol type="A"><li>Multiple small commits with descriptive messages</li><li>One giant final commit</li><li>No commits — just a zip upload</li></ol><details><summary>Show answer</summary><div class="ans"><b>A.</b> Incremental commits with clear messages are an explicit grading item — commit as you complete each requirement.</div></details></div></div><div class="vl" data-defglance="1"><div class="vl-box vl-rule def-glance"><div class="t">📖 DEFINITIONS AT A GLANCE — every key term in this section</div></div><table><tr><th style="width:32%">Term</th><th>Definition</th></tr><tr><td><b>should_throwException_when_invalidArg</b></td><td>reads as a specification.</td></tr><tr><td><b>AssertJ</b></td><td>gives fluent assertions: assertThat(list).hasSize(3).contains(x).</td></tr><tr><td><b>Coverage</b></td><td>is the percentage of code executed by tests — track it, but remember executed is not the same as verified.</td></tr></table><div style="font-size:12px;color:var(--text-3);margin:-6px 0 14px">Full lesson with examples below ⬇️ — nothing removed.</div></div>
<div class="callout callout-why">
 <div class="callout-label">◆ WHY THIS MATTERS</div>
 <p>Tests prove your validations work — both for graders and for you. The submission process has its own rules: format, originality, write-up. Hitting the rubric on every line takes deliberate care; first-time-pass submissions follow a checklist, not luck.</p>
</div>

<h3>Core concepts</h3>
<p><strong>1. JUnit tests for validations.</strong> @Test methods exercising each rule. Use a Validator instance: ValidatorFactory factory = Validation.buildDefaultValidatorFactory(); Validator validator = factory.getValidator(); Set&lt;ConstraintViolation&lt;Part violations = validator.validate(part); assertFalse(violations.isEmpty()) — for negative cases. assertTrue(violations.isEmpty()) — for valid input.</p>
<p><strong>2. Spring Boot's @SpringBootTest.</strong> For full-context tests (controller + service + repository), @SpringBootTest spins up the application context. Heavier than unit tests but verifies wiring. Use sparingly; prefer focused unit tests for validation rules.</p>
<p><strong>3. @DataJpaTest for repository tests.</strong> Configures only the JPA layer with an in-memory database. Faster than full @SpringBootTest. Use TestEntityManager to set up test data, then exercise the repository.</p>
<p><strong>4. @WebMvcTest for controller tests.</strong> Configures only the web layer. Use MockMvc to simulate HTTP requests and assert responses. Good for testing controller behavior without spinning up the full app.</p>
<p><strong>5. End-to-end smoke testing.</strong> Before submitting, manually run the app and click through every flow: list parts, add part, edit part, delete part, validate inputs (empty name, negative price, inventory out of range). The grader will do this; catch bugs first.</p>
<p><strong>6. Write-up structure.</strong> Typical sections: introduction (what was built), implementation (what you added, with code references), validation rules (mapped to rubric), test approach, challenges and decisions, screenshots showing it working. Concise but complete; reference specific files and methods.</p>
<p><strong>7. Submission format.</strong> Check 's instructions: usually a zip of source code (excluding target/ and IDE files), a PDF write-up, and possibly screenshots. Verify all files open. Re-read the rubric once more before submitting.</p>

<div class="callout callout-warning">
 <div class="callout-label">⚠ COMMON PITFALLS</div>
 <ul>
 <li>Submitting without running tests — they fail in CI/grader and your submission gets returned.</li>
 <li>Including the target/ build folder, IDE files (.idea, .vscode), or local credentials in the zip.</li>
 <li>Write-up that lists what you did but doesn't explain why or how — reviewers want decisions, not just an inventory.</li>
 <li>Missing screenshots when the rubric specifies them.</li>
 </ul>
</div>

<h3>Quick recap</h3>
<p>Cover each validation rule with positive and negative JUnit tests. Use @SpringBootTest, @DataJpaTest, or @WebMvcTest as appropriate. Manually smoke-test before submitting. Write a structured write-up mapped to the rubric. Submit clean (no target/, no IDE files), verify files open, re-read the rubric once.</p>

<h3>Glossary</h3>
<dl>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@SpringBootTest</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring Boot test annotation that boots the full application context.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@DataJpaTest</span></dt>
 <dd>Test annotation configuring only JPA layer for fast repository tests.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">@WebMvcTest</span></dt>
 <dd>Test annotation configuring only the web layer with MockMvc.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">MockMvc</span></dt>
 <dd>Spring's mock HTTP request framework for testing controllers.</dd>
 <dt><span class="key-term">Smoke test</span></dt>
 <dd>Manual end-to-end run-through verifying core flows work before submission.</dd>
</dl>
<p>Test craft: name tests by behavior — <strong>should_throwException_when_invalidArg</strong> reads as a specification. <strong>AssertJ</strong> gives fluent assertions: assertThat(list).hasSize(3).contains(x). <strong>Coverage</strong> is the percentage of code executed by tests — track it, but remember executed is not the same as verified.</p><i data-tb287c6s3="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="vl" data-mathvis="1" data-c959fig="1" style="margin-top:18px;"><div style="font-weight:700; font-size:12.5px; letter-spacing:0.05em; margin-bottom:6px;">📊 FIGURE — what each test annotation loads, layer by layer</div><div style="border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2); padding:14px 10px; overflow-x:auto;"><svg viewBox="0 0 680 368" style="width:100%; max-width:680px; display:block; margin:0 auto; font-family:inherit;" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><defs><marker id="fk202a" viewBox="0 0 10 10" refX="9" refY="5" markerWidth="6" markerHeight="6" orient="auto-start-reverse"><path d="M0,1 L9,5 L0,9 z" fill="var(--text)"/></marker></defs><text x="340" y="24" fill="var(--text)" font-size="12.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">the three test annotations differ in one way only: how much of the application they start</text><text x="148" y="103" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">@Controller</text><text x="148" y="145" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">@Service</text><text x="148" y="187" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">@Repository</text><text x="148" y="229" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">Hibernate + H2</text><text x="148" y="271" fill="var(--text)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="end">embedded Tomcat</text><text x="230" y="58" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@WebMvcTest</text><text x="230" y="74" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">fast — the web layer alone</text><rect x="168" y="84" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#0ea5e9" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="230" y="104" fill="#0ea5e9" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">loaded</text><rect x="168" y="126" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="230" y="146" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">@MockBean</text><rect x="168" y="168" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="230" y="188" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not loaded</text><rect x="168" y="210" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="230" y="230" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not loaded</text><rect x="168" y="252" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="230" y="272" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not loaded</text><text x="380" y="58" fill="#e8795a" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@DataJpaTest</text><text x="380" y="74" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">fast — JPA and an embedded DB</text><rect x="318" y="84" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="380" y="104" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not loaded</text><rect x="318" y="126" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="380" y="146" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not loaded</text><rect x="318" y="168" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="380" y="188" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">loaded</text><rect x="318" y="210" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#e8795a" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="380" y="230" fill="#e8795a" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">loaded</text><rect x="318" y="252" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="none" stroke="var(--text-3)" stroke-width="1.1" stroke-dasharray="4 3"/><text x="380" y="272" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">not loaded</text><text x="530" y="58" fill="#10b981" font-size="11.5" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle" font-family="ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace">@SpringBootTest</text><text x="530" y="74" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="9" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle">slow — everything</text><rect x="468" y="84" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="530" y="104" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">loaded</text><rect x="468" y="126" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="530" y="146" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">loaded</text><rect x="468" y="168" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="530" y="188" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">loaded</text><rect x="468" y="210" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="530" y="230" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">loaded</text><rect x="468" y="252" width="124" height="30" rx="7" fill="var(--surface)" stroke="#10b981" stroke-width="1.8"/><text x="530" y="272" fill="#10b981" font-size="10" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">loaded</text><text x="340" y="322" fill="var(--text-3)" font-size="10.5" font-weight="400" text-anchor="middle" font-style="italic">A slice test starts in about a second; @SpringBootTest starts the whole context, which is why you write few of them.</text><text x="340" y="352" fill="#8b5cf6" font-size="11" font-weight="700" text-anchor="middle">And a plain JUnit test with a hand-built Validator needs none of these — it is the fastest way to cover a validation rule.</text></svg></div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — one positive and one negative test for the inventory rule</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The rubric asks for tests that prove each rule fires <em>and</em> that it does not fire on good input. For a validation rule the cheapest correct test uses the Jakarta Validation API directly — no Spring context at all.</p><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:12px 14px; margin:10px 0; overflow-x:auto;"><code style="font-family:ui-monospace,SFMono-Regular,Menlo,monospace; font-size:12.8px; line-height:1.62; white-space:pre;">class PartValidationTest {

    private static Validator validator;

    @BeforeAll
    static void setUp() {
        ValidatorFactory factory = Validation.buildDefaultValidatorFactory();
        validator = factory.getValidator();
    }

    private Inhouse partWith(int min, int inventory, int max) {
        Inhouse part = new Inhouse();
        part.setName("Brake pad");
        part.setPrice(new BigDecimal("12.50"));
        part.setMin(min);
        part.setInventory(inventory);
        part.setMax(max);
        return part;
    }

    @Test
    void inventoryInsideTheBandIsAccepted() {                 // positive case
        Set&lt;ConstraintViolation&lt;Inhouse&gt;&gt; violations = validator.validate(partWith(10, 40, 120));
        assertTrue(violations.isEmpty(), "expected no violations, got " + violations);
    }

    @Test
    void inventoryBelowTheMinimumIsRejected() {               // negative case
        Set&lt;ConstraintViolation&lt;Inhouse&gt;&gt; violations = validator.validate(partWith(10, 5, 120));
        assertEquals(1, violations.size());
        assertEquals("Inventory must be between the minimum and the maximum",
                     violations.iterator().next().getMessage());
    }

    @Test
    void inventoryAboveTheMaximumIsRejected() {               // the other boundary
        assertFalse(validator.validate(partWith(10, 140, 120)).isEmpty());
    }

    @Test
    void blankNameIsRejected() {
        Inhouse part = partWith(10, 40, 120);
        part.setName("   ");                                  // @NotBlank, not @NotNull
        assertFalse(validator.validate(part).isEmpty());
    }
}</code></pre><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Test both directions of every rule.</strong> A suite that only feeds valid input passes even when validation has been deleted, which is why "tests that only check the happy path" is called out explicitly in the rubric.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Test the boundaries, not the middle.</strong> With min 10 and max 120, the interesting values are 9, 10, 120 and 121. A test at 40 proves less than a test at 10.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Assert the message, not just the count.</strong> If the specification names the wording, the test should be what proves you produced that wording.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Build the validator once in <code>@BeforeAll</code>.</strong> It is expensive to create and stateless to use.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Use the heavier annotations only where they earn it.</strong> <code>@DataJpaTest</code> for a derived query method — it needs a real database to prove the generated SQL is right. <code>@WebMvcTest</code> with <code>MockMvc</code> for "does a POST with a blank name re-render the form". <code>@SpringBootTest</code> for one end-to-end smoke test, and no more than that.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Four short tests, no Spring context, roughly a second to run. That is the shape the rubric is asking for.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#0ea5e9;"><div class="callout-label">▸ WORKED EXAMPLE — the pre-submission checklist</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">The last hour is where marks are lost by people who did the work. Run this in order.</p><ol style="margin:8px 0 4px 18px; padding:0; line-height:1.75;"><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Run the whole suite from the command line</strong>, not from the IDE: <code>./mvnw clean test</code>. A clean build catches the test that only passed because a stale class file was lying around.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Start the application fresh and click every flow.</strong> List, add, edit, delete, for both <code>Inhouse</code> and <code>Outsourced</code>; add and remove a part from a product; then deliberately break each rule and confirm the message appears on screen and the typed values survive. The grader will do exactly this.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Confirm the seed data loads.</strong> The database is in-memory, so whatever you typed in is gone the moment you restart. If the rubric expects sample parts to be visible, they must come from <code>data.sql</code>.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Read every file you changed, once.</strong> Delete commented-out blocks, stray <code>System.out.println</code> calls, unused imports and TODOs. Reviewers notice, and it costs nothing to fix.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Check what is in the archive.</strong> <code>src/</code>, <code>pom.xml</code>, <code>mvnw</code>, <code>mvnw.cmd</code>, <code>.mvn/</code>. Not <code>target/</code>, not <code>.idea/</code>, not <code>.vscode/</code>, not <code>*.iml</code>. Unzip it somewhere else and build it there — that is the only real proof it opens.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Write the write-up as an explanation, not an inventory.</strong> For each rubric row: what you implemented, which file and method it lives in, why you chose that approach over the alternative, and how you tested it. "I added validation" scores nothing; "I implemented min ≤ inventory ≤ max as a class-level <code>@ValidPart</code> constraint rather than a field annotation, because a field validator receives only one value" scores the row.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Add the screenshots if the rubric asks for them</strong>, and make them show the behaviour — the error message on screen, not just the form.</li><li style="margin-bottom:6px;"><strong>Re-read the rubric one final time against your checklist.</strong> Most returned submissions fail on a row nobody read carefully, not on code that did not work.</li></ol><div style="margin-top:10px; padding:8px 12px; border-radius:8px; background:var(--surface-2); font-weight:700; line-height:1.6;">Answer: Clean build, manual smoke test of every flow including the failures, a clean archive that builds elsewhere, and a write-up that explains decisions rather than listing changes.</div></div><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div><p style="margin:10px 0; line-height:1.7;">Match the annotation to the layer under test: <strong><code>@WebMvcTest</code></strong> loads controllers only and gives you <code>MockMvc</code> (services get <code>@MockBean</code>); <strong><code>@DataJpaTest</code></strong> loads JPA and an embedded database and gives you <code>TestEntityManager</code>; <strong><code>@SpringBootTest</code></strong> loads the entire context and is the slow one. For a pure validation rule, no annotation is needed at all — build a <code>Validator</code> from <code>Validation.buildDefaultValidatorFactory()</code> and call <code>validate(part)</code>. And remember the rubric wording: every rule needs a <strong>negative</strong> test as well as a positive one.</p></div><i data-d287cov_63="1" style="display:none"></i><h4 style="margin:22px 0 8px; font-size:15.5px;">Mockito, central exception handling, and writing for the evaluator</h4><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div><strong>Mockito</strong> is a Java <strong>mocking library</strong>: it creates test doubles for a class's dependencies so a unit test exercises one class in isolation, with no database and no Spring context.</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">@Test
void returnsPartWhenFound() {
    PartRepository repo = mock(PartRepository.class);
    when(repo.findById(1L)).thenReturn(Optional.of(new InHousePart()));

    PartService service = new PartService(repo);   // constructor injection pays off here
    assertTrue(service.find(1L).isPresent());
}</pre><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#8b5cf6;"><div class="callout-label">📘 DEFINITION</div>A class annotated <strong>@ControllerAdvice</strong> with <code>@ExceptionHandler</code> methods <strong>handles exceptions across all controllers in one central place</strong>, so error handling is not repeated in every controller.</div><pre style="background:var(--surface-2); border:1px solid var(--border); border-radius:8px; padding:11px 13px; overflow-x:auto; font-size:12.5px; line-height:1.5;">@ControllerAdvice
public class GlobalExceptionHandler {
    @ExceptionHandler(PartNotFoundException.class)
    public String handleMissing(PartNotFoundException e, Model model) {
        model.addAttribute("message", e.getMessage());
        return "error";
    }
}</pre><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#e11d48;"><div class="callout-label">🎯 EXAM TIP</div>Writing your submission "for the evaluator" means <strong>making each requirement easy to find and verify</strong>: name classes and methods after the rubric language, and put a brief comment where a rubric item is satisfied. An evaluator who cannot locate a requirement records it as missing, however well it works.</div>"},"D287/pa_sim/sim":{"body":"<h2>Practice Assessment — 50-Question Simulation</h2><p>Fifty questions curated from every unit of this course for full-breadth coverage. Take it timed (75 minutes), closed-book, on your scratch space. Review every miss — each explanation names the exact rule tested.</p><i data-simfresh="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">✓ FRESH QUESTIONS</div>Every one of these 50 questions is one you have <b>not answered before</b>, so the score is a real read on where you stand.</div>"},"D287/oa_sim/sim":{"body":"<h2>Objective Assessment — 50-Question Simulation</h2><p>Led by this course’s exam-calibrated final bank, filled to 50 with zero overlap against your PA. Timed, closed-book. Score 85%+ here and you are walking into the real OA over-prepared.</p><i data-simfresh="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">✓ FRESH QUESTIONS</div>Every one of these 66 questions is one you have <b>not answered before</b>, so the score is a real read on where you stand.</div>"},"D287/weak_drill/sim":{"body":"<h2>🎯 Weak Spots</h2><p>A practice set drawn from the <strong>topics you score lowest on</strong> — computed live from your quiz history (any topic under 80% accuracy, weakest first). The more you practice, the more precisely this targets your gaps. No history in this course yet? It starts you on the hardest questions until it learns where you struggle.</p>"},"D287/review_missed/sim":{"body":"<h2>🔁 Review Missed</h2><p>Every question you’ve <strong>answered incorrectly</strong> in this course, gathered in one place so you can retry them (most-recent misses first, up to 30). Get one right here and you’ll see the explanation reinforce the exact rule tested. Nothing missed yet? It falls back to this course’s hardest questions.</p>"},"D287/pa_fresh/sim":{"body":"<h2>🆕 Fresh Questions — Brand-New Practice</h2><p>40 brand-new questions written for this course and mapped across its competencies — not repeated anywhere else in the app. Take it timed and closed-book, then review every miss.</p><i data-simfresh="1" style="display:none"></i><div class="callout" data-lb="key" style="border-left-color:#22c55e;"><div class="callout-label">✓ FRESH QUESTIONS</div>Every one of these 40 questions is one you have <b>not answered before</b>, so the score is a real read on where you stand.</div>"},"D287/aistote/s1":{"body":"<div class="vl" data-visual="aistote-method">
<h3>🧠 The Aistote Method (J-Method)</h3>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE IDEA</div>The Aistote app is built on two of the most evidence-backed study techniques — <b>active recall</b> and <b>spaced repetition</b> — run in short, intense <b>25-minute</b> blocks (its "J-Method"). Instead of slowly re-reading, you spend the time <b>testing yourself</b> and re-testing what you miss. This app already has every tool the method needs.</div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">THE 3-STEP LOOP</div>
<table><tr><th>Step</th><th>What you do</th><th>Where in this app</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>1. Convert</b></td><td>Turn the material into questions instead of re-reading it.</td><td>Open a section, skim the lesson once, then jump to its <b>Quiz</b>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>2. Recall</b></td><td>Answer from memory first — no peeking — then check.</td><td>Take the section Quiz, or a <b>PA / OA simulation</b>; rate your confidence.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>3. Space it</b></td><td>Re-test only what you missed, on a growing schedule.</td><td>Every miss becomes a <b>Flashcard</b>; review them on the app's spaced-repetition schedule.</td></tr></table></div>
<div class="vl-ex"><div class="t">A 25-MINUTE J-METHOD SESSION</div>
<table><tr><th>Time</th><th>Do this</th></tr>
<tr><td class="num">0–3 min</td><td>Skim the lesson's key terms and the big idea — don't memorize yet, just get the lay of the land.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">3–18 min</td><td>Take the section quiz (or a 15-question drill). Try to <b>recall the answer first</b>, reveal it after.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">18–23 min</td><td>Redo <b>only</b> the questions you got wrong.</td></tr>
<tr><td class="num">23–25 min</td><td>Send the stubborn ones to <b>Flashcards</b> and note tomorrow's quick review.</td></tr></table>
Start the block with the <b>⚡ Focus</b> timer (25 min) on any lesson — that is your J-Method session.</div>
<div class="vl-warn"><div class="t">⚠️ THE ONE RULE</div>Always try to answer <b>from memory before you look</b>. The effort of retrieving is what actually builds the memory — re-reading feels productive but barely moves it.</div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">WHY IT WORKS</div><b>Active recall</b> (testing yourself) forces your brain to reconstruct the answer, which strengthens it far more than recognition. <b>Spaced repetition</b> reviews each fact just as you're about to forget it, moving it into long-term memory with the fewest reps. The 25-minute block simply makes you actually do both, every day.</div>
<div class="vl-rule"><div class="t">DAILY RHYTHM</div>Two or three 25-minute J-Method blocks a day (new material in one, missed-question review in the next) beats a single long cram session — and it's how the Aistote method delivers "learn it in 25 minutes" instead of hours.</div>
</div>"},"D287/userdocs/add":{"body":"<h3 style="margin-top:0;">➕ Add a document to this class</h3><p style="font-size:13.5px; color:var(--text-3); line-height:1.6;">Paste your document below (or choose a .txt file). It will be kept <b>word-for-word, exactly as you add it</b>. Word/PDF files: open them, select all, copy, and paste here — that preserves your text precisely.</p><div data-docform="D287" style="display:flex; flex-direction:column; gap:12px; max-width:720px;"><input data-doc-title type="text" placeholder="Title (e.g., Unit 3 Sample Test)" style="padding:11px 13px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid var(--border); background:var(--surface-2); color:var(--text); font-size:14px;"><div style="display:flex; gap:16px; flex-wrap:wrap; font-size:13.5px;"><label style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; cursor:pointer;"><input data-doc-type type="radio" name="doctype-D287" value="notes" checked> 📒 Notes (shown as-is)</label><label style="display:flex; align-items:center; gap:6px; cursor:pointer;"><input data-doc-type type="radio" name="doctype-D287" value="test"> 📝 Practice test (answers held until the end)</label></div><textarea data-doc-content rows="12" placeholder="Paste the document here, exactly as you want it shown…" style="padding:12px 13px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid var(--border); background:var(--surface-2); color:var(--text); font-size:14px; line-height:1.6; resize:vertical;"></textarea><textarea data-doc-key rows="4" placeholder="(Practice tests only, optional) Paste the ANSWER KEY here — it will be hidden until you open it at the end. You can skip this if your document already has a line saying “Answer Key” before the answers." style="padding:12px 13px; border-radius:10px; border:1px solid var(--border); background:var(--surface-2); color:var(--text); font-size:13.5px; line-height:1.6; resize:vertical;"></textarea><div style="display:flex; gap:10px; align-items:center; flex-wrap:wrap;"><label style="font-size:13px; color:var(--text-3); cursor:pointer; padding:9px 13px; border:1px dashed var(--border); border-radius:10px;">📎 Choose .txt file<input data-doc-file type="file" accept=".txt,.md,.csv,text/plain" style="display:none;"></label><button data-doc-save="D287" style="border:none; background:var(--accent, #6366f1); color:#fff; font-weight:700; font-size:14px; padding:11px 20px; border-radius:10px; cursor:pointer;">Save document</button><span data-doc-msg style="font-size:12.5px; color:var(--text-4);"></span></div></div>"},"D287/cheat/c1":{"body":"<h2>📋 Java Frameworks Overview</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Frameworks vs Libraries</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE ONE-LINE DISTINCTION A library is code YOU call when you want it (you own the program flow — e.g., calling a math or JSON function). A framework CALLS YOUR code — it owns the flow and invokes the classes/methods you plug into it. This is called Inversion of Control ( don t call us, we ll call…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ WHY USE A FRAMEWORK? (the pros question) The main benefit: the framework supplies the plumbing — request handling, database wiring, security, configuration — so you write only business logic. Less boilerplate, faster development, tested infrastructure, consistent structure across teams. Trade-off:…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ SPRING IS MODULAR Spring isn t one blob — you pull in the modules you need: Spring Core (the IoC container/DI), Spring MVC (web layer), Spring Data/JPA (databases), Spring Security (auth), Spring Test . Spring Boot then auto-configures the modules you include.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">IoC and Dependency Injection</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE VOCABULARY Bean — an object created and managed by the Spring container. ApplicationContext — the container itself: it creates beans, wires them together, and manages their lifecycle. @Autowired / constructor injection — how you mark where a dependency should be injected (constructor injection…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ WHY DI MATTERS (the exam answer) DI gives loose coupling (swap implementations without editing the class) and testability (inject mocks). If a question asks why not just use new? — that s the answer.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Spring Ecosystem</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE FAMILY, ONE LINE EACH Spring Framework (Core) — the IoC container + DI. Everything builds on this. Spring Boot — opinionated quick-start: auto-configuration, embedded server (Tomcat), starter dependencies. Just run it. Spring MVC — the web layer: controllers, request mappings, views. Spring…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ BOOT vs FRAMEWORK Spring Boot is NOT a replacement for Spring — it sits ON TOP, pre-configuring it. The tell for Boot questions: auto-configuration , starters (spring-boot-starter-web), and the embedded server — no external Tomcat install, no web.xml, just run the JAR. If a question mentions any of those three, the answer is Boot.</p></div>"},"D287/cheat/c2":{"body":"<h2>📋 Spring Boot Basics</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Project Setup and Structure</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE SKELETON, FILE BY FILE pom.xml ← Maven build file: dependencies (starters) live here src/main/java/... ← your code; Application.java at the root package src/main/resources/ application.properties ← configuration (port, DB url, ...) templates/ ← Thymeleaf HTML templates static/ ← css/js/images…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ WHERE Application.java LIVES MATTERS The main class sits in the root package ; component scanning finds beans in its package and BELOW. Put a controller in a package outside that tree and Spring silently never finds…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">@SpringBootApplication and Beans</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ @SpringBootApplication = THREE annotations in one @SpringBootApplication public class App { public static void main(String[] args) { SpringApplication.run(App.class, args); } } It bundles: @Configuration (this class can define beans) + @EnableAutoConfiguration (set things up from the classpath) +…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ STEREOTYPE ANNOTATIONS — same engine, different label @Component, @Service, @Repository, @Controller all do the same core thing: mark the class so component scanning registers it as a bean . The names declare the LAYER for readability (and small extras: @Repository translates DB exceptions,…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Configuration Properties</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ WHAT TO KNOW Boot reads application.properties (or .yml) automatically. @Value("${key}") injects a single property. Profiles : application-dev.properties vs application-prod.properties, activated with spring.profiles.active=dev — different settings per environment without code changes. ddl-auto…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ CONFIG BEATS CODE EDITS If an exam question asks how do you change the port / DB URL, the answer is a properties change ( server.port=9090 ), never edit and recompile Java.</p></div>"},"D287/cheat/c3":{"body":"<h2>📋 Spring MVC and Thymeleaf</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">@Controller and Request Mappings</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE MAPPING TOOLKIT @GetMapping / @PostMapping (+ Put/Delete) — HTTP verb + URL → method. @PathVariable — pull a value from the URL: @GetMapping("/parts/{id}") + @PathVariable Long id → /parts/7 gives id = 7. @RequestParam — pull a query value: /parts?q=bolt → @RequestParam String q . Wildcard :…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Thymeleaf Templates</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE th: TOOLKIT th:text="${x}" — put a value into the element s text. th:each="item : ${list}" — repeat the element per item. th:if / th:unless — render conditionally. th:href="@{/parts/{id}(id=${p.id})}" — build URLs safely. th:field="*{name}" — bind a form input to a field of the form-backing…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ ${...} vs *{...} ${x} reads from the Model. *{x} reads a field of the object named by th:object (used in forms). Mixing them up is the top Thymeleaf error.</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Forms and Model Binding</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ THE BINDING CHAIN MUST MATCH th:object names the backing object; th:field="*{name}" must match a real property (getName/setName). A typo breaks silently — the object arrives with null in that field. When a form loses data on submit, check the binding chain first: object name, field names, getters/setters — one mismatch and the value silently vanishes.</p></div>"},"D287/cheat/c4":{"body":"<h2>📋 JPA, Hibernate, and Repositories</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">JPA Entities</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE ANNOTATIONS TO KNOW @Entity — map class ↔ table. Needs a no-arg constructor. @Id — the primary-key field. @GeneratedValue — the database generates the key (IDENTITY = auto-increment). @Column(name="unit_price") — customize the column; optional when names match. @Transient — exclude a field from…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Relationships</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE RELATIONSHIP TOOLKIT @OneToMany / @ManyToOne — the classic parent/child pair. @ManyToMany — needs a join table. cascade = CascadeType.ALL — operations on the parent ripple to children (save the product → its parts save too). orphanRemoval = true — remove a child from the parent s collection and…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ orphanRemoval ≠ cascade REMOVE Cascade REMOVE fires when the PARENT is deleted. orphanRemoval also fires when a child is merely removed from the collection — product.getParts().remove(p) deletes p s row. Know that…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Spring Data JPA Repositories</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ WHAT TO KNOW CrudRepository&lt;T, ID> — basic CRUD. JpaRepository extends it (adds paging/flush). Derived queries: findBy&lt;Field>&lt;Op> — GreaterThan, Between, OrderBy @Query("...") — write JPQL yourself when the name-parser isn t enough. findById returns Optional — handle the missing case (orElseThrow)…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">H2 In-Memory Database</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ IN-MEMORY MEANS GONE ON RESTART H2 (jdbc:h2: mem :...) loses everything when the app stops — that s a feature in dev and a bug in production. The PA expects sample data to appear via a seeder (CommandLineRunner or data.sql), so the grader sees populated pages on a fresh run instead of an empty database.</p></div>"},"D287/cheat/c5":{"body":"<h2>📋 Validation, Exceptions, and Logging</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Bean Validation</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE RULES YOU LL USE @NotNull — must exist. @NotBlank — string with real content. @NotEmpty — not empty (string/collection). @Min / @Max — numeric bounds (the PA s min/max inventory checks!). @Size(min, max) — length bounds. @Email , @Pattern — formats. @Valid on the controller parameter triggers…</p><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55; color:#ef4444;">⚠ ORDER OF PARAMETERS save(@Valid Part part, BindingResult result) works. Put another parameter between them and Spring throws instead of collecting errors. BindingResult directly follows the @Valid object — memorize…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Exception Handling</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ BEST PRACTICES (the exam list) @ControllerAdvice + @ExceptionHandler = global, centralized handling — preferred over try/catch scattered through controllers. Throw specific exceptions (IllegalArgumentException for bad input, custom NotFoundException) — never catch bare Exception and continue…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Logging</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE LEVEL LADDER TRACE &lt; DEBUG (developer detail) &lt; INFO (normal lifecycle events) &lt; WARN (suspicious but running) &lt; ERROR (something failed). Setting a level shows that level AND everything more severe. Spring Boot uses SLF4J as the logging face (Logback underneath by default).</p></div>"},"D287/cheat/c6":{"body":"<h2>📋 PA: Part Management Application</h2><p style="color:var(--text-3); font-size:12.5px;">Condensed from your lessons — the rules, traps and topics of this chapter in one place.</p><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">PA Project Overview</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ THE TASK LIST, IN ORDER 1. Set up the starter project in IntelliJ, run it, commit to GitLab (small, frequent commits with messages — they re graded). 2. Customize the shop : rename the store/brand in the templates for your imaginary business. 3. Add an About page : new @Controller mapping +…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Adding Validations and Logic</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ TIMESTAMPS (createdAt / updatedAt) Audit fields update themselves with lifecycle hooks: @PrePersist sets createdAt once on first save; @PreUpdate refreshes updatedAt on every change. (Or @CreationTimestamp / @UpdateTimestamp with Hibernate.) Questions phrase it as set automatically when the row is…</p></div><div style="margin:14px 0; padding:12px 14px; border:1px solid var(--border); border-left:3px solid var(--accent); border-radius:12px; background:var(--surface-2);"><div style="font-weight:800; font-size:14px; margin-bottom:2px;">Testing and Submission</div><p style="margin:6px 0; font-size:13px; line-height:1.55;">▸ SUBMISSION CHECKLIST All required features work (about page, seed data, min/max, buy button with confirm/error). Unit tests for the added logic pass. GitLab history shows multiple commits with meaningful messages (graded — don t one-shot commit). Video demo : record yourself running the app and walking through each requirement on camera — show the feature working, not just the code that should make it work.</p></div>"}}
